{"turn": 4, "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "call_00_9FZYBUnQeWod5dYHA3hh3478", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"documents/zoning-ordinance-excerpt.docx\"}", "result": "**[CONESTOGA TOWNSHIP ZONING ORDINANCE]{.underline}**\n\n**[Chapter 27 --- Zoning]{.underline}**\n\n**Lancaster County, Pennsylvania**\n\n**Section 27-605(B)(14): Utility-Scale Solar Energy Systems** **Conditional Use in the Agricultural-Rural (A-R) Zoning District**\n\n*As amended by Ordinance No. 2023-12, adopted December 18, 2023, effective December 28, 2023.*\n\nThis document is an excerpt from the Conestoga Township Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 27, and is provided for reference purposes only. The complete Zoning Ordinance is available for inspection at the Conestoga Township offices, 1220 Millwood Road, Conestoga, Pennsylvania 17516, and on the Township website at www.conestogatwp.org. In the event of any discrepancy between this excerpt and the official codified ordinance on file with the Township Secretary, the official codified ordinance shall control.\n\n**[Section 27-601. Purpose and Intent]{.underline}**\n\nConditional uses are those land uses which, due to their particular characteristics, may be appropriate in certain zoning districts but which require individual review and approval by the Board of Supervisors to ensure compatibility with surrounding properties, consistency with the goals and objectives of the Conestoga Township Comprehensive Plan, and compliance with the general and specific standards set forth in this Article VI. The Board of Supervisors shall grant a conditional use application only upon a finding that the applicant has satisfied all applicable general standards of Section 27-602 and the specific standards applicable to the proposed use as set forth in Section 27-605. The authority of the Board of Supervisors to review and approve conditional uses is conferred by the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code (MPC), 53 P.S. §§ 10101 *et seq.*, and in particular Article VI thereof, and shall be exercised in accordance with the substantive and procedural requirements of the MPC and this Ordinance.\n\n**[Section 27-602. General Standards for All Conditional Uses]{.underline}**\n\nIn addition to the specific standards applicable to each individual conditional use as set forth in Section 27-605, all conditional use applications shall be evaluated against the following general standards. No conditional use shall be approved unless the Board of Supervisors finds that the applicant has demonstrated compliance with each of the following:\n\n> \\(a\\) The proposed use shall not be detrimental to the health, safety, and welfare of the residents of the Township and the surrounding community.\n>\n> \\(b\\) The proposed use shall be consistent with the goals, objectives, and policies of the Conestoga Township Comprehensive Plan, as adopted and amended from time to time.\n>\n> \\(c\\) The proposed use shall not adversely affect the character, property values, or orderly development of the surrounding neighborhood or zoning district in which the use is proposed to be located.\n>\n> \\(d\\) Adequate public infrastructure, including but not limited to roads, stormwater management facilities, water supply, and sewage disposal, exists or will be provided by the applicant to serve the proposed use in a manner that does not place an undue burden on the Township or its residents.\n>\n> \\(e\\) The applicant has demonstrated compliance with all other applicable Township, county, state, and federal regulations, including but not limited to stormwater management, erosion and sedimentation control, floodplain management, wetland protection, and historic preservation requirements.\n\nThese general standards shall apply in addition to, and not in lieu of, the specific standards applicable to each conditional use category as set forth in Section 27-605 of this Ordinance.\n\n**[Section 27-605. Conditional Uses in the Agricultural-Rural (A-R) District]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 27-605(A). Permitted Conditional Uses**\n\nThe following land uses may be permitted as conditional uses in the Agricultural-Rural (A-R) Zoning District, subject to the applicable general standards of Section 27-602 and the specific standards set forth in Section 27-605(B) for each use category:\n\n> (B)(1) Public and private schools.\n>\n> (B)(2) Places of worship and religious institutions.\n>\n> (B)(3) Cemeteries and memorial gardens.\n>\n> (B)(4) Agricultural commercial enterprises.\n>\n> (B)(5) Communication towers and wireless telecommunications facilities.\n>\n> (B)(6) Bed-and-breakfast establishments.\n>\n> (B)(7) Rural event venues.\n>\n> (B)(8) Veterinary clinics and animal hospitals.\n>\n> (B)(9) Farm-based composting facilities.\n>\n> (B)(10) Wind energy systems.\n>\n> (B)(11) Mineral extraction and quarrying operations.\n>\n> (B)(12) Outdoor recreation facilities.\n>\n> (B)(13) Intensive agricultural operations.\n>\n> **(B)(14) Utility-scale solar energy systems.**\n>\n> **(B)(15) Energy storage facilities.**\n>\n> (B)(16) Agritourism operations.\n\nEach conditional use category listed above is subject to separate and distinct specific standards as set forth in the corresponding subsection of Section 27-605(B). Where a proposed development includes components that fall within more than one conditional use category, the applicant shall demonstrate compliance with the specific standards applicable to each category, unless this Ordinance expressly provides otherwise.\n\n**[Section 27-605(B)(14). Utility-Scale Solar Energy Systems --- Conditional Use Standards]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 27-605(B)(14)(a). Definitions**\n\nFor the purposes of this Section 27-605(B)(14), the following terms shall have the meanings ascribed to them herein. Where a term defined in this subsection conflicts with a definition of the same term in Section 27-210 of this Ordinance, the definition in this subsection shall control for the purposes of interpreting and applying the provisions of this Section 27-605(B)(14).\n\n**\\\"Utility-Scale Solar Energy System\\\"** --- A solar photovoltaic energy generation facility with a nameplate alternating current (AC) capacity exceeding ten (10) megawatts (MW), including all associated solar panels, racking systems, inverters, transformers, power conversion stations, electrical collection systems, access roads, and related infrastructure located on the project site. The term \\\"utility-scale solar energy system\\\" shall not include small-scale or community solar energy systems with a nameplate AC capacity of ten (10) megawatts or less, which are subject to separate provisions of this Ordinance.\n\n**\\\"Project Site\\\"** --- The contiguous or non-contiguous parcels of land upon which the utility-scale solar energy system and its associated infrastructure are proposed to be constructed, installed, and operated, as depicted on the approved site plan submitted as part of the conditional use application. The project site shall include all parcels identified in the application as hosting solar panels, racking systems, inverters, substations, energy storage facilities, internal access roads, stormwater management facilities, and vegetative screening buffers. The project site shall not include off-site linear infrastructure such as transmission lines, generation tie lines, access roads located outside the project boundary, or utility interconnection facilities not located on the parcels identified in the conditional use application.\n\n**\\\"Energy Storage Facility\\\"** --- A system designed to store electrical energy, including but not limited to battery energy storage systems (BESS), flywheel energy storage, compressed air energy storage, and other technologies, for later injection into the electrical grid or for use by the generation facility, with a storage capacity exceeding five (5) megawatt-hours (MWh). An energy storage facility may be approved as a standalone conditional use under Section 27-605(B)(15) of this Ordinance or as an accessory or secondary use to a utility-scale solar energy system under this Section 27-605(B)(14), provided that all dimensional and performance standards applicable to energy storage facilities under Section 27-605(B)(15) are also met when the energy storage facility is associated with a utility-scale solar energy system.\n\n**\\\"Non-Participating Property\\\"** --- Any lot or parcel of land, as recorded in the office of the Lancaster County Recorder of Deeds, that is not subject to a lease, easement, option, or other written agreement with the applicant or the applicant\\'s affiliates, successors, or assigns for the purpose of hosting, supporting, or otherwise facilitating the construction, installation, or operation of the utility-scale solar energy system or any component thereof.\n\n**\\\"Non-Participating Property Line\\\"** --- The property boundary of a non-participating property, as depicted on the recorded deed or subdivision plan for such property.\n\n**\\\"Occupied Dwelling\\\"** --- Any building or structure designed for or used as a permanent or seasonal residence that is occupied or capable of occupancy at the time the conditional use application is filed with the Township. A dwelling shall be deemed \\\"capable of occupancy\\\" if it is served by water and sewage facilities, is structurally sound, and has not been formally condemned by the Township or other governmental authority.\n\n**\\\"Decommissioning\\\"** --- The removal of all above-ground and below-ground components of a utility-scale solar energy system, including but not limited to solar panels, racking systems, inverters, transformers, substations, energy storage equipment, fencing, access roads, and foundations, the restoration of the project site to substantially its pre-construction condition suitable for the prior use of the land, and the recycling or proper disposal of all removed materials in accordance with applicable state and federal law, upon the cessation of operations.\n\n**\\\"Solar Glare\\\"** --- The reflection of sunlight from solar panels, racking systems, or related surfaces toward an observer, including reflected light that is visible from public roadways, occupied dwellings on non-participating properties, and aircraft flight paths, and that may create a hazard or nuisance.\n\n**\\\"Type C Vegetative Screening Buffer\\\"** --- A landscaped buffer area consisting of a mix of native evergreen and deciduous trees and shrubs, planted at intervals and densities sufficient to provide a year-round visual screen of at least seventy-five percent (75%) opacity within five (5) years of planting, as further specified in Section 27-605(B)(14)(d) of this Ordinance.\n\n**\\\"Gen-Tie Line\\\" or \\\"Generation Tie Line\\\"** --- An electrical transmission line connecting a generation facility, such as a utility-scale solar energy system, to the point of interconnection with the regional transmission system or an existing electrical substation operated by the local electric distribution company or regional transmission operator. A gen-tie line is not considered part of the utility-scale solar energy system or the project site for purposes of this Section 27-605(B)(14).\n\n**Section 27-605(B)(14)(b). Application Requirements**\n\nAn applicant seeking conditional use approval for a utility-scale solar energy system under this Section shall submit the following materials to the Township Zoning Officer as part of a complete conditional use application:\n\n> \\(1\\) A completed conditional use application form, as adopted by the Board of Supervisors, signed by the applicant or its authorized representative, and accompanied by the application fee established by resolution of the Board of Supervisors.\n>\n> \\(2\\) A site plan, prepared and sealed by a licensed professional engineer or licensed surveyor registered in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania, drawn to a scale no smaller than 1 inch = 100 feet, depicting: (i) all parcel boundaries comprising the project site, with tax parcel identification numbers; (ii) the proposed locations of all solar panels, racking systems, inverters, power conversion stations, substations, energy storage facilities, internal collection lines, access roads, fencing, gates, and other infrastructure; (iii) all applicable setback lines as required by Section 27-605(B)(14)(c); (iv) all existing structures, including occupied dwellings, within one thousand (1,000) feet of the project site boundary; (v) proposed vegetative screening buffer locations and landscaping; (vi) existing topography at two-foot contour intervals; and (vii) proposed grading and finished elevations.\n>\n> \\(3\\) A narrative description of the proposed technology, including solar panel type and manufacturer, racking system design (fixed-tilt or single-axis tracker), inverter specifications and locations, transformer and substation design, and the nameplate generation capacity of the facility in both alternating current (AC) and direct current (DC) megawatts.\n>\n> \\(4\\) Evidence of site control, in the form of executed leases, options to lease, easements, or proof of fee ownership, for all parcels comprising the project site.\n>\n> \\(5\\) A preliminary stormwater management plan prepared in accordance with the Conestoga Township Stormwater Management Ordinance, Chapter 26, and the Lancaster County Act 167 Stormwater Management Plan, demonstrating compliance with all applicable stormwater management requirements.\n>\n> \\(6\\) A preliminary erosion and sedimentation control plan prepared in accordance with the regulations of the Pennsylvania Department of Environmental Protection and the Lancaster County Conservation District.\n>\n> \\(7\\) A noise impact assessment prepared by a qualified acoustical engineer, modeling the anticipated noise levels from all components of the utility-scale solar energy system at the nearest non-participating property lines and nearest occupied dwellings on non-participating properties.\n>\n> \\(8\\) A solar glare analysis evaluating the potential for solar glare from the proposed facility to affect occupied dwellings on non-participating properties, public roadways, and known aircraft flight paths in the vicinity of the project site.\n>\n> \\(9\\) A decommissioning plan and cost estimate prepared by a licensed professional engineer registered in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania, in accordance with Section 27-605(B)(14)(i).\n>\n> \\(10\\) Evidence of consultation with the Pennsylvania Natural Diversity Inventory (PNDI) program, including the initial PNDI screening results, and copies of any required follow-up correspondence with the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, the Pennsylvania Fish and Boat Commission, and/or the Pennsylvania Game Commission.\n>\n> \\(11\\) A cultural resources assessment, prepared by a qualified professional meeting the Secretary of the Interior\\'s Professional Qualification Standards, if the project site or any portion thereof is within two hundred (200) feet of a known or potentially eligible historic resource listed in or eligible for listing in the National Register of Historic Places, or if such assessment is required by the Pennsylvania Historical and Museum Commission (PHMC).\n>\n> \\(12\\) A property value impact assessment, if requested by the Board of Supervisors at or prior to the first hearing on the conditional use application, to be prepared by a state-certified general appraiser retained by the Township at the applicant\\'s expense.\n>\n> \\(13\\) A traffic impact study addressing both construction-phase and operational-phase vehicle trips, prepared in accordance with PennDOT requirements and the standards of the Institute of Transportation Engineers (ITE).\n>\n> \\(14\\) Proof of application to the applicable regional transmission organization for electrical interconnection of the proposed facility.\n>\n> \\(15\\) An advisory review letter from the Lancaster County Planning Commission, as required by Section 609 of the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code, 53 P.S. § 10609.\n>\n> \\(16\\) Such additional information as the Board of Supervisors may reasonably request to evaluate the conditional use application and ensure compliance with the standards of this Ordinance.\n\nAn application shall not be deemed complete for purposes of scheduling a public hearing until all items listed above have been submitted to the Township Zoning Officer. The Zoning Officer shall notify the applicant in writing within thirty (30) days of submission if any items are missing or incomplete.\n\n**Section 27-605(B)(14)(c). Dimensional Standards**\n\nAll utility-scale solar energy systems shall comply with the following dimensional standards. Where an energy storage facility is proposed as an accessory or secondary use to a utility-scale solar energy system, the dimensional standards for energy storage facilities under Section 27-605(B)(15)(c) shall also apply to such energy storage components.\n\n**Table 27-605-1: Dimensional Standards for Utility-Scale Solar Energy Systems**\n\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Requirement**                                                                                                      **Standard**\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Minimum Lot Area (project site total)                                                                                50 acres\n\n  Maximum Lot Coverage (impervious surfaces within fenced area)                                                        75% of fenced area\n\n  Maximum Building Height --- Solar Panels (at maximum tilt)                                                           20 feet\n\n  Maximum Building Height --- Substations, Inverters, Power Conversion Stations                                        45 feet\n\n  Maximum Building Height --- Fencing                                                                                  8 feet\n\n  Setback from Non-Participating Property Line --- Solar Panels and Racking                                            100 feet\n\n  Setback from Non-Participating Property Line --- Substations, Inverters, Power Conversion Stations                   250 feet\n\n  Setback from Non-Participating Property Line --- Energy Storage Facilities (BESS)                                    300 feet (See Note 1)\n\n  Setback from Public Road Right-of-Way --- All Components                                                             150 feet\n\n  Setback from Occupied Dwelling on Non-Participating Property --- Solar Panels and Racking                            500 feet\n\n  Setback from Occupied Dwelling on Non-Participating Property --- Energy Storage Facilities (BESS)                    1,000 feet\n\n  Setback from Occupied Dwelling on Non-Participating Property --- Substations, Inverters, Power Conversion Stations   500 feet\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Note 1:** Where an energy storage facility is proposed as an accessory or secondary use to a utility-scale solar energy system under this Section 27-605(B)(14), the dimensional standards of Section 27-605(B)(15) shall apply to the energy storage components. The setback of three hundred (300) feet from any non-participating property line for energy storage facilities is established by Section 27-605(B)(15)(c)(3) and is incorporated herein by reference.\n\n**Note 2:** All setbacks specified in this table shall be measured from the nearest point of the applicable component (including any foundation, concrete pad, or supporting structure) to the nearest point of the applicable boundary line (property line, right-of-way line, or exterior wall of an occupied dwelling, as applicable). Setbacks shall be measured horizontally.\n\n**Note 3:** Where a parcel within the project site is subject to overlay district requirements or other dimensional standards elsewhere in this Ordinance, the more restrictive standard shall apply.\n\n**Section 27-605(B)(14)(d). Screening and Landscaping**\n\nThe following screening and landscaping standards shall apply to all utility-scale solar energy systems:\n\n> \\(1\\) A Type C vegetative screening buffer, as defined in Section 27-605(B)(14)(a), shall be installed along any project site boundary that is adjacent to: (i) a non-participating property containing an occupied dwelling located within one thousand (1,000) feet of the project site boundary; or (ii) a public road right-of-way.\n>\n> \\(2\\) The vegetative screening buffer shall be a minimum of thirty (30) feet in width, measured perpendicular to the project site boundary, and shall be located entirely within the project site boundary.\n>\n> \\(3\\) The buffer shall consist of a mix of native evergreen and deciduous trees and shrubs, planted at densities and intervals sufficient to achieve the opacity standard specified in subsection (4) below. A minimum of sixty percent (60%) of the plantings shall be evergreen species to ensure year-round screening effectiveness.\n>\n> \\(4\\) All plantings shall be a minimum of four (4) feet in height at the time of installation and shall achieve a minimum mature height of twelve (12) to fifteen (15) feet. The buffer shall achieve a minimum of seventy-five percent (75%) visual opacity within five (5) years of planting, as determined by the Township Engineer or a qualified landscape architect retained by the Township.\n>\n> \\(5\\) The applicant shall submit a landscaping plan prepared by a licensed landscape architect registered in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania, identifying species, spacing, projected growth rates, and a maintenance schedule. The landscaping plan shall be submitted as part of the conditional use application and shall be subject to review and approval by the Board of Supervisors.\n>\n> \\(6\\) All plantings shall consist of species native to southeastern Pennsylvania, as identified by the Pennsylvania Department of Conservation and Natural Resources or an equivalent authoritative source. Invasive species, as listed on the Pennsylvania Department of Agriculture\\'s Noxious Weed Control List, shall not be used.\n>\n> \\(7\\) The applicant shall be responsible for the ongoing maintenance, irrigation (if necessary to establish plantings), and replacement of any dead, diseased, or damaged plantings for the operational life of the facility. Any planting that fails to survive shall be replaced with a plant of the same or comparable species and minimum size within the next planting season following discovery of the loss.\n>\n> \\(8\\) Existing mature vegetation, hedgerows, and tree lines within the buffer area may be credited toward compliance with the screening requirements of this subsection if the Township Engineer or a qualified landscape architect retained by the Township determines that such existing vegetation provides visual screening equivalent to or exceeding the standards set forth herein.\n\n**Section 27-605(B)(14)(e). Perimeter Fencing and Security**\n\nThe following fencing and security standards shall apply to all utility-scale solar energy systems:\n\n> \\(1\\) The entire project area containing solar panels, energy storage facilities, inverters, substations, power conversion stations, and related electrical equipment shall be enclosed by security fencing. The security fence shall define a continuous and secure perimeter around all energized and potentially hazardous components of the facility.\n>\n> \\(2\\) Fencing shall be a minimum of seven (7) feet in height and shall be constructed of durable, weather-resistant materials. Fencing shall be topped with angled extension arms or similar deterrent devices to discourage unauthorized entry. The use of barbed wire, razor wire, or electrified fencing is expressly prohibited.\n>\n> \\(3\\) Wildlife passage openings shall be incorporated into the perimeter fence at intervals of no more than five hundred (500) feet along the total perimeter length to allow passage of small mammals, reptiles, and other ground-dwelling wildlife. Each wildlife passage opening shall be a minimum of six (6) inches in height and twelve (12) inches in width at ground level, and shall be designed to prevent unauthorized human entry while permitting wildlife movement.\n>\n> \\(4\\) All fencing shall be set back a minimum of ten (10) feet from the interior edge of any vegetative screening buffer to allow adequate access for buffer maintenance, including mowing, pruning, irrigation, and replanting activities.\n>\n> \\(5\\) Vehicular access gates shall be provided at each designated access point to the project site and shall be designed and constructed to accommodate emergency vehicle access. All gates shall remain locked when the facility is unattended and shall be equipped with a lock box or similar device providing emergency responders with rapid access.\n>\n> \\(6\\) Warning signage reading \\\"No Trespassing\\\" and \\\"Danger --- High Voltage\\\" shall be posted at intervals of no more than two hundred fifty (250) feet along the perimeter fence and at all gates. Signage shall be weather-resistant, legible at a distance of at least twenty-five (25) feet, and shall comply with applicable OSHA and National Electrical Code standards.\n\n**Section 27-605(B)(14)(f). Environmental and Natural Resource Protection**\n\n> \\(1\\) **Stormwater Management.** The applicant shall comply with all applicable stormwater management requirements of the Conestoga Township Stormwater Management Ordinance, Chapter 26, and the Lancaster County Act 167 Stormwater Management Plan. A final stormwater management plan, prepared and sealed by a licensed professional engineer registered in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania, shall be submitted to and approved by the Township Engineer prior to the issuance of any building permit or grading permit. The stormwater management plan shall first be reviewed and approved by the Lancaster County Conservation District. The plan shall address both the construction phase and the operational phase of the facility, including the effects of impervious and semi-impervious surfaces (such as solar panels and access roads) on stormwater runoff volumes, peak flow rates, and water quality. The applicant shall demonstrate that post-development stormwater runoff will not exceed pre-development conditions for the two-year, ten-year, twenty-five-year, fifty-year, and one-hundred-year design storm events.\n>\n> \\(2\\) **Erosion and Sediment Control.** The applicant shall obtain all required NPDES General Permits for Stormwater Discharges Associated with Construction Activities (PAG-02) from the Pennsylvania Department of Environmental Protection and shall prepare and implement an Erosion and Sedimentation (E&S) Control Plan approved by the Lancaster County Conservation District prior to any land disturbance activity on the project site. The E&S Control Plan shall be prepared in accordance with 25 Pa. Code Chapter 102 and the standards of the Lancaster County Conservation District.\n>\n> \\(3\\) **Threatened and Endangered Species.** The applicant shall complete a Pennsylvania Natural Diversity Inventory (PNDI) environmental review for the entire project site and shall comply with all required consultations with the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, the Pennsylvania Fish and Boat Commission, and/or the Pennsylvania Game Commission regarding potential impacts to federal or state-listed threatened and endangered species. All necessary consultations shall be completed and all required clearance letters or determinations shall be obtained prior to any land disturbance activity on the affected parcels. The applicant shall implement all avoidance, minimization, and mitigation measures identified through the consultation process.\n>\n> \\(4\\) **Cultural and Historic Resources.** If the project site or any portion thereof is located within two hundred (200) feet of a known or potentially eligible historic resource listed in or eligible for listing in the National Register of Historic Places, or if the project involves a federal action or federal funding triggering review under Section 106 of the National Historic Preservation Act, the applicant shall complete consultation with the Pennsylvania Historical and Museum Commission (PHMC) in accordance with applicable state and federal laws, including the Pennsylvania History Code, 37 Pa. C.S. §§ 101 *et seq.* The applicant shall not commence grading, excavation, or other earth-disturbing activities in the vicinity of any such resource until the PHMC has issued a written determination regarding the project\\'s potential effect on the resource and any required mitigation measures have been agreed upon.\n>\n> \\(5\\) **Wetlands and Watercourses.** No solar panels, energy storage facilities, permanent access roads, or permanent structures shall be located within any wetland, jurisdictional watercourse, floodplain, or riparian buffer area as defined and delineated in accordance with applicable federal regulations (including Section 404 of the Clean Water Act and the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers Wetland Delineation Manual), applicable state regulations, and the Conestoga Township Floodplain Management Ordinance. The applicant shall provide a wetland delineation and a floodplain analysis prepared by qualified professionals as part of the conditional use application if any portion of the project site is identified as potentially containing wetlands or floodplain areas.\n>\n> \\(6\\) **Agricultural Mitigation.** For any utility-scale solar energy system located on land classified as prime agricultural land, as defined by the U.S. Department of Agriculture, Natural Resources Conservation Service (NRCS), including soils classified as \\\"prime farmland\\\" or \\\"farmland of statewide importance,\\\" the applicant shall make a one-time agricultural mitigation payment to a qualified agricultural land conservation organization approved by the Board of Supervisors. The mitigation payment shall be calculated at a rate of one thousand two hundred dollars (\\$1,200.00) per acre of prime agricultural land converted from active agricultural use as a result of the construction and operation of the solar energy system. The total acreage subject to the mitigation payment shall be the acreage within the project site that is classified as prime farmland or farmland of statewide importance, as verified by the NRCS soil survey data and confirmed by the Lancaster County Conservation District. The payment shall be made in full prior to the issuance of any building permit or grading permit for the project.\n\n**Section 27-605(B)(14)(g). Performance Standards**\n\nAll utility-scale solar energy systems shall comply with the following performance standards during both construction and operation:\n\n> \\(1\\) **Noise.** Operational noise generated by a utility-scale solar energy system, including but not limited to noise from inverters, transformers, power conversion stations, tracking motors, and energy storage system cooling equipment, shall not exceed forty-five (45) dBA as measured at the nearest non-participating property line. Noise measurements shall be conducted in accordance with applicable ANSI standards for environmental sound measurement. The noise impact assessment required by Section 27-605(B)(14)(b)(7) shall model noise levels under worst-case operating conditions and shall identify any mitigation measures necessary to achieve compliance with the forty-five (45) dBA standard. Construction-phase noise shall be exempt from this standard but shall comply with applicable Township noise regulations and the hours-of-construction limitations imposed by the Board of Supervisors as a condition of approval.\n>\n> \\(2\\) **Solar Glare.** The applicant shall demonstrate through a solar glare analysis that the utility-scale solar energy system will not produce solar glare that creates a significant hazard or nuisance to occupants of nearby occupied dwellings on non-participating properties, users of public roadways, or aircraft operating in the vicinity of the project site. A solar glare analysis shall be submitted with the conditional use application as required by Section 27-605(B)(14)(b)(8). Where solar glare impacts are identified, the applicant shall propose mitigation measures such as panel orientation adjustments, anti-reflective coatings, or vegetative screening to eliminate or minimize the identified impacts to the satisfaction of the Board of Supervisors.\n>\n> \\(3\\) **Electromagnetic Interference.** The utility-scale solar energy system shall not cause interference with radio, television, telephone, cellular, emergency communication, or other communication signals to neighboring properties or public facilities. The applicant shall promptly investigate and resolve any documented interference complaints within thirty (30) days of receiving written notice from the Township or an affected property owner.\n>\n> \\(4\\) **Lighting.** All on-site lighting shall be shielded and directed downward to minimize light pollution and shall not cause illumination to be cast upon any adjacent property or public roadway in excess of 0.5 foot-candles measured at the property line. Lighting shall be limited to the minimum necessary for safety and security purposes and shall comply with the Township\\'s outdoor lighting standards set forth in Chapter 27, Section 27-710 of this Ordinance. Security lighting at substations, energy storage facilities, and access gates may be equipped with motion-activated sensors to reduce continuous nighttime lighting.\n>\n> \\(5\\) **Dust and Erosion.** During construction, the applicant shall implement dust suppression measures, including but not limited to watering of access roads and exposed soil areas, application of dust suppressants, and stabilization of stockpiled materials, to minimize airborne particulate matter. All disturbed areas not covered by solar panels, equipment, access roads, or hardscaping shall be stabilized with permanent vegetation or approved ground cover within thirty (30) days of final grading of each phase of construction. Seed mixes used for ground cover shall consist of native grasses and pollinator-friendly species appropriate for southeastern Pennsylvania.\n\n**Section 27-605(B)(14)(h). Access and Transportation**\n\n> \\(1\\) The applicant shall provide a traffic impact study addressing construction-phase traffic, including the delivery of oversized and overweight equipment, solar panels, racking systems, energy storage components, and aggregate materials, and operational-phase traffic, including routine maintenance vehicle trips and periodic equipment replacement activities. The traffic impact study shall be prepared in accordance with PennDOT and ITE standards and shall identify the proposed construction vehicle routes, estimated number and type of vehicle trips by construction phase, and proposed mitigation measures for any identified impacts.\n>\n> \\(2\\) All construction traffic shall utilize routes approved by the Township Engineer in consultation with PennDOT and the applicable road authority. The applicant shall enter into a road maintenance agreement with the Township, in a form satisfactory to the Township Solicitor, and shall post a road maintenance bond or other acceptable financial security in an amount determined by the Board of Supervisors, based upon the recommendation of the Township Engineer, to ensure the repair of any damage to Township roads caused by construction-related traffic and activities. The road maintenance bond shall be posted prior to the commencement of construction.\n>\n> \\(3\\) Access to the project site shall be provided via a minimum of two (2) access points, each with a minimum driveway width of twenty (20) feet, designed and constructed to accommodate emergency vehicle access, including fire apparatus. Access points shall be designed to provide adequate sight distance in accordance with PennDOT standards and shall be approved by the Township Engineer.\n>\n> \\(4\\) During the operational phase, the facility shall not generate more than ten (10) vehicle trips per day on average, calculated on a monthly basis, excluding scheduled maintenance events, emergency response activities, and deliveries of replacement equipment. The applicant shall maintain a log of operational vehicle trips and shall make such log available to the Township upon request.\n\n**Section 27-605(B)(14)(i). Decommissioning**\n\n> \\(1\\) The applicant shall submit a decommissioning plan with the conditional use application describing in detail the process for removal of all above-ground and below-ground components of the utility-scale solar energy system, including solar panels, racking systems, inverters, transformers, substations, energy storage equipment, fencing, access roads, foundations, and underground electrical conduit, and the restoration of the project site to substantially its pre-construction condition suitable for resumption of the prior land use.\n>\n> \\(2\\) The applicant shall provide a decommissioning cost estimate prepared and sealed by a licensed professional engineer registered in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania, which shall include: (i) the estimated gross cost of decommissioning, including removal of all above-ground and below-ground components to a depth of no less than thirty-six (36) inches below finished grade, site restoration, revegetation, and the proper disposal or recycling of all removed materials in accordance with applicable state and federal waste management regulations; and (ii) the estimated net salvage value of all recoverable materials, supported by current market data for recyclable commodities.\n>\n> \\(3\\) Decommissioning security shall be posted by the applicant in an amount equal to one hundred twenty-five percent (125%) of the estimated net decommissioning cost (the gross cost of decommissioning minus the estimated net salvage value of recoverable materials). The net salvage value credit shall not reduce the required security amount to less than seventy-five percent (75%) of the estimated gross decommissioning cost.\n>\n> \\(4\\) Acceptable forms of decommissioning security shall include: (i) an irrevocable standby letter of credit issued by a financial institution with a long-term credit rating of A- or better from a nationally recognized statistical rating organization (such as Standard & Poor\\'s, Moody\\'s, or Fitch), or an equivalent rating from a comparable rating agency; (ii) a surety bond issued by a surety company authorized to do business in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania with an A.M. Best rating of A- VII or better; or (iii) an escrow account held by a financial institution meeting the credit rating requirements of clause (i), with the Township named as a beneficiary. The form and terms of the decommissioning security instrument shall be subject to approval by the Township Solicitor.\n>\n> \\(5\\) The decommissioning security shall be posted prior to the issuance of the first building permit for the utility-scale solar energy system. No building permit shall be issued until the Township Solicitor has confirmed that the decommissioning security instrument is in proper form and has been fully executed and delivered.\n>\n> \\(6\\) The decommissioning cost estimate shall be updated every five (5) years from the date of commercial operation by a licensed professional engineer registered in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania. Each updated estimate shall be submitted to the Township within thirty (30) days of completion. If the updated estimate results in a net decommissioning cost that exceeds the amount of the existing security, the applicant shall increase the security to one hundred twenty-five percent (125%) of the updated net decommissioning cost within sixty (60) days of the date of the updated estimate. If the updated estimate results in a net decommissioning cost that is less than the existing security amount, the applicant may request a reduction in the security, which shall be approved by the Board of Supervisors upon verification of the updated estimate by the Township Engineer.\n>\n> \\(7\\) Decommissioning shall be completed within twelve (12) months of the date on which the facility ceases generating and delivering electricity to the grid for a continuous period of twelve (12) months, unless the applicant demonstrates to the satisfaction of the Board of Supervisors, by clear and convincing evidence, that the cessation of operations is temporary and that the facility will resume generating electricity within a reasonable period not to exceed an additional twelve (12) months. In no event shall the total period of non-generation exceed twenty-four (24) consecutive months without the commencement of decommissioning.\n>\n> \\(8\\) If the applicant fails to complete decommissioning within the timeframe required by subsection (7) above, the Township shall provide written notice to the applicant and the issuer of the decommissioning security instrument. If decommissioning is not substantially completed within ninety (90) days of such notice, the Township may draw upon the decommissioning security and utilize the proceeds to complete the decommissioning of the facility at the applicant\\'s expense. Any costs incurred by the Township in excess of the security amount shall constitute a lien against the project site parcels in accordance with applicable law.\n\n**Section 27-605(B)(14)(j). Financial Assurances and Fiscal Impact**\n\n> \\(1\\) The Board of Supervisors may require the applicant to enter into a Payment in Lieu of Taxes (PILOT) Agreement with the Township as a condition of conditional use approval if the applicant intends to seek any tax exemption, abatement, alternative energy credit, or alternative assessment for the utility-scale solar energy system under applicable state law, including but not limited to the Alternative Energy Production Tax Credit Act or any other statute that may reduce or eliminate the applicant\\'s real property tax liability for the solar energy facility or its components.\n>\n> \\(2\\) The terms, duration, and payment amounts of any PILOT agreement shall be negotiated between the applicant and the Township and shall be set forth in a written agreement in a form satisfactory to the Township Solicitor. The PILOT agreement shall be executed and recorded in the office of the Lancaster County Recorder of Deeds prior to the issuance of the first building permit, if required by the Board of Supervisors as a condition of approval.\n>\n> \\(3\\) The applicant shall remain responsible for all applicable real estate taxes assessed on non-exempt improvements on the project site parcels as determined by the Lancaster County Board of Assessment Appeals.\n>\n> \\(4\\) The applicant shall remain responsible for all applicable school district real estate taxes, which shall not be subject to any PILOT agreement between the applicant and the Township. The Township shall have no authority to negotiate or enter into any agreement affecting school district tax revenues.\n>\n> \\(5\\) A property value impact assessment may be required at the discretion of the Board of Supervisors, to be prepared by a state-certified general appraiser retained by the Township at the applicant\\'s expense. The assessment shall evaluate the potential impact of the proposed facility on the market value of residential properties within one thousand (1,000) feet of the project site boundary.\n\n**Section 27-605(B)(14)(k). Scope of Approval**\n\n> \\(1\\) A conditional use approval granted under this Section 27-605(B)(14) shall apply only to the project site as identified in the conditional use application and as depicted on the site plan approved by the Board of Supervisors. The conditional use approval shall not extend to, nor shall it authorize construction or operation of, any facilities, structures, or infrastructure located outside the boundaries of the project site.\n>\n> \\(2\\) Off-site infrastructure, including but not limited to generation tie lines, transmission lines, distribution lines, access roads located outside the project site boundary, and utility interconnection facilities not located on the parcels identified in the conditional use application, shall be subject to separate zoning, land development, subdivision, and permitting approvals as applicable under this Ordinance, the Conestoga Township Subdivision and Land Development Ordinance, the ordinances of adjacent municipalities through which such off-site infrastructure may be routed, and applicable state and federal law. The approval of a conditional use under this Section shall not constitute, nor shall it be construed as, approval of any off-site infrastructure.\n>\n> \\(3\\) Modification, expansion, or reconfiguration of the approved project beyond the scope of the approved site plan shall require either: (i) an amendment to the conditional use approval, to be reviewed and approved by the Board of Supervisors following the same substantive and procedural requirements as an original conditional use application under this Article VI, including public notice and hearing; or (ii) a new conditional use application filed and processed in accordance with this Article VI.\n>\n> \\(4\\) The conditional use approval shall be personal to the applicant identified in the conditional use application and the Decision and Order of the Board of Supervisors. The approval may be transferred or assigned to a successor, affiliate, or third party only with the prior written consent of the Board of Supervisors, which consent shall not be unreasonably withheld, conditioned, or delayed. The transferee or assignee shall assume all obligations, conditions, and requirements of the conditional use approval and shall demonstrate to the satisfaction of the Board of Supervisors that it has the financial, technical, and organizational capacity to construct, operate, and decommission the facility in accordance with the approved conditions.\n>\n> \\(5\\) The conditional use approval shall expire and shall be of no further force or effect if the applicant has not obtained a building permit for the utility-scale solar energy system within twenty-four (24) months of the date of the Decision and Order granting the conditional use approval, unless extended by the Board of Supervisors upon written request by the applicant filed prior to the expiration date and demonstrating good cause for the extension. Any extension granted by the Board of Supervisors shall not exceed an additional twelve (12) months.\n\n**Section 27-605(B)(14)(l). Annual Reporting and Compliance Monitoring**\n\n> \\(1\\) The applicant, or its successors or assigns approved by the Board of Supervisors, shall provide an annual report to the Board of Supervisors within sixty (60) days of each anniversary of the commercial operation date, summarizing: (i) facility operations, including total energy production in megawatt-hours (MWh) during the reporting period; (ii) maintenance activities performed during the reporting period, including any equipment replacements; (iii) compliance with all conditions of the conditional use approval and the provisions of this Ordinance; (iv) the current status and adequacy of the decommissioning security, including confirmation that the security remains in full force and effect; and (v) any material changes to the facility, its ownership, or its operations during the reporting period.\n>\n> \\(2\\) The Township Engineer, the Township Zoning Officer, or a designee of the Board of Supervisors shall be permitted to conduct an annual site inspection of the facility upon reasonable prior written notice to the applicant of not less than ten (10) business days. The applicant shall bear the reasonable cost of such inspection, including the fees of the Township Engineer. The annual inspection shall verify compliance with the conditions of the conditional use approval, including but not limited to screening and landscaping, fencing and security, noise and glare standards, stormwater management, and general site maintenance.\n>\n> \\(3\\) Failure to comply with any condition of the conditional use approval, any provision of this Section 27-605(B)(14), or any other applicable provision of this Ordinance may result in enforcement action by the Township, including but not limited to issuance of a notice of violation by the Township Zoning Officer, assessment of fines and penalties as authorized by this Ordinance and the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code, or initiation of proceedings for revocation of the conditional use approval following a hearing before the Board of Supervisors. The Township shall provide the applicant with written notice of any alleged violation and a reasonable opportunity to cure, not to exceed thirty (30) days, prior to commencing revocation proceedings, except where the violation poses an imminent threat to public health, safety, or welfare.\n\n**[Section 27-605(B)(15). Energy Storage Facilities --- Conditional Use Standards (Relevant Excerpt)]{.underline}**\n\nThe following is an excerpt of Section 27-605(B)(15) to the extent it establishes dimensional standards cross-referenced by Section 27-605(B)(14).\n\n**Section 27-605(B)(15)(c). Dimensional Standards for Energy Storage Facilities**\n\n> \\(3\\) **Setback from Non-Participating Property Line.** An energy storage facility, including all battery enclosures, containers, inverters, transformers, thermal management systems, and associated support structures, shall be set back a minimum of three hundred (300) feet from any non-participating property line. This setback shall be measured from the nearest point of the energy storage equipment to the nearest point of the non-participating property line.\n>\n> \\(4\\) **Setback from Occupied Dwelling on Non-Participating Property.** An energy storage facility shall be set back a minimum of one thousand (1,000) feet from any occupied dwelling, as defined in Section 27-605(B)(14)(a), located on a non-participating property. This setback shall be measured from the nearest point of the energy storage equipment to the nearest exterior wall of the occupied dwelling.\n\nWhere an energy storage facility is proposed as a secondary or accessory use to a utility-scale solar energy system under Section 27-605(B)(14), the dimensional standards of this Section 27-605(B)(15)(c) shall apply in full to all energy storage components. The applicant shall demonstrate compliance with both the dimensional standards of Section 27-605(B)(14)(c) (as applicable to solar energy system components) and the dimensional standards of this Section 27-605(B)(15)(c) (as applicable to energy storage components) on the site plan submitted with the conditional use application.\n\n**[Section 27-210. Definitions (Relevant Excerpt)]{.underline}**\n\nThe following definitions are excerpted from Section 27-210 (Definitions) of Chapter 27, to the extent they are relevant to the interpretation of Section 27-605(B)(14) and the conditions of any conditional use approval granted thereunder:\n\n**\\\"Building Permit\\\"** --- A permit issued by the Township Zoning Officer, or by a designated third-party building code official, authorizing the construction, erection, alteration, modification, or demolition of any building or structure within the Township, in accordance with the applicable provisions of the Pennsylvania Uniform Construction Code, 34 Pa. Code Chapters 401-405, and this Ordinance. A building permit is distinct from and is not to be confused with a grading permit, a zoning permit, or any other permit issued by the Township.\n\n**\\\"Grading Permit\\\"** --- A permit issued by the Township Zoning Officer authorizing earth-disturbing activities on a parcel, including but not limited to excavation, filling, grading, stockpiling of materials, and related activities. A grading permit may be issued separately from or concurrently with a building permit, depending on the scope and phasing of the proposed construction activity.\n\n**\\\"Commencement of Construction\\\"** --- The initiation of physical construction activities on a project site, including but not limited to clearing, grubbing, grading, excavation, installation of foundations, driving of piles, or installation of any permanent structure or infrastructure component. The term \\\"commencement of construction\\\" shall not include surveying, geotechnical testing, soil sampling, environmental testing, installation of monitoring equipment, or other pre-construction investigative activities that do not involve significant physical disturbance of the land surface.\n\n**\\\"Commercial Operation Date (COD)\\\"** --- With respect to a utility-scale solar energy system or energy storage facility, the date on which the facility first delivers electrical energy to the electrical grid for commercial sale pursuant to an executed power purchase agreement, interconnection agreement, or wholesale market participation agreement, as certified by the facility operator and confirmed by the applicable regional transmission organization or electric distribution company.\n\n**CERTIFICATION**\n\nCERTIFIED as a true and correct excerpt from the Conestoga Township Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 27 --- Zoning, Lancaster County, Pennsylvania, as amended through Ordinance No. 2023-12, adopted December 18, 2023, effective December 28, 2023. This excerpt includes Sections 27-601, 27-602, 27-605(A), 27-605(B)(14), 27-605(B)(15)(c)(3)-(4), and relevant definitions from Section 27-210.\n\nCONESTOGA TOWNSHIP\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: Karen M. Brandt\n\nTitle: Township Secretary\n\nDate: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nThis excerpt is provided for reference purposes only and does not constitute an official codification. In the event of any discrepancy between this excerpt and the official codified Conestoga Township Zoning Ordinance on file with the Township Secretary, the official codified ordinance shall control. Interested parties are encouraged to consult the complete Zoning Ordinance for all applicable provisions.\n"}
