{"turn": 5, "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "call_00_VctDmD4LzBEiz77fZSr10483", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"documents/applicant-cover-letter.docx\"}", "result": "**PINNACLE RENEWABLES LLC** 4200 Commerce Parkway, Suite 310 Wilmington, DE 19801 Tel: (302) 555-8140 www.pinnaclerenewables.com\n\nNovember 1, 2024\n\nHonorable Harold Zimmerman, Chair and Members of the Board of Supervisors Conestoga Township 129 East Main Street Conestoga, PA 17516\n\n**Re: Brightfield Solar Project --- Conditional Use Application, Response to Public Hearing Testimony and Summary of Proposed Conditions**\n\nDear Chairman Zimmerman and Members of the Board:\n\nI write on behalf of Pinnacle Renewables LLC (\\\"Pinnacle\\\" or the \\\"Applicant\\\") and its wholly owned subsidiary, Brightfield Solar Project LLC (\\\"Brightfield Solar\\\" or the \\\"Project Company\\\"), a Pennsylvania limited liability company formed on March 15, 2024, in connection with the Conditional Use Application filed on June 3, 2024, for the Brightfield Solar Project (the \\\"Project\\\") under Section 27-605(B)(14) of the Conestoga Township Zoning Ordinance (the \\\"Ordinance\\\"), as amended in December 2023. The purpose of this letter is threefold: first, to provide a comprehensive summary of the Project for the Board\\'s consideration; second, to respond substantively to the testimony and concerns raised by members of the public and neighboring property owners during the three public hearings held on August 14, September 11, and October 9, 2024; and third, to outline the Applicant\\'s understanding of the proposed conditions of approval that the Board may wish to include in its decision, ahead of the Board\\'s anticipated vote at its regularly scheduled meeting on November 18, 2024.\n\nThe Applicant acknowledges and appreciates the advisory recommendation issued by the Lancaster County Planning Commission on July 22, 2024, in which Director of Planning Thomas Reinhart rendered a generally favorable advisory opinion regarding the Project\\'s consistency with the Lancaster County Comprehensive Plan and the Conestoga Township Zoning Ordinance. The Applicant has carefully reviewed the advisory recommendation and has incorporated several of Director Reinhart\\'s suggestions into its revised project design and proposed conditions, as further described below. We believe the record developed over the course of these proceedings amply demonstrates that the Brightfield Solar Project satisfies all applicable standards for conditional use approval and will serve the public interest.\n\n**[Project Summary]{.underline}**\n\nThe Brightfield Solar Project is a utility-scale solar energy generation facility utilizing single-axis tracking photovoltaic solar panel technology. The Project has a nameplate capacity of 120 megawatts alternating current (MW AC) and 156 megawatts direct current (MW DC), reflecting a DC-to-AC ratio of approximately 1.30. The facility will consist of approximately 296,000 solar modules mounted on single-axis tracker systems, served by 40 string inverters and 3 central power conversion stations. In addition to the solar generation components, the Application includes a 40 MW / 160 MWh battery energy storage system (\\\"BESS\\\") as a secondary use, which will enable the Project to provide dispatchable energy and grid reliability services.\n\nThe Project site encompasses approximately 850 acres across seven contiguous parcels situated in the Agricultural-Rural (A-R) zoning district. The fenced area of the facility will encompass approximately 720 acres, with solar panel coverage occupying approximately 580 acres of that area. The remaining acreage within the fence line will accommodate access roads, stormwater management facilities, the BESS compound, power conversion stations, and vegetative buffers.\n\nThe Project will interconnect to the regional transmission grid at the existing Conestoga Substation via a new 138 kV generation-tie line extending approximately 2.3 miles from the Project site. The Project holds queue position AG2-2024-0847 with Mid-Atlantic Grid Coordination LLC and is progressing through the interconnection study process.\n\nThe estimated total construction cost of the Project is approximately \\$200 million, consisting of \\$168 million for the solar generation facility and \\$32 million for the BESS components. The Applicant\\'s target date for commencement of construction is April 1, 2026, with a projected commercial operation date of December 31, 2027.\n\nThe seven parcels comprising the Project site are as follows:\n\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Parcel ID**     **Owner(s)**                   **Acreage**       **Lease Term**\n  ----------------- ------------------------------ ----------------- ----------------------------------\n  120-45-001        Elmer J. & Ruth A. Stoltzfus   145 acres         35 years + two 5-year renewals\n\n  120-45-002        Stoltzfus Family Trust         110 acres         35 years + two 5-year renewals\n\n  120-45-003        Brubaker Agricultural LLC      130 acres         30 years + two 5-year renewals\n\n  120-45-004        Brubaker Agricultural LLC      95 acres          30 years + two 5-year renewals\n\n  120-45-005        David M. Herr                  120 acres         35 years + two 5-year renewals\n\n  120-45-006        Lancaster Heritage Land Co.    140 acres         30 years + three 5-year renewals\n\n  120-45-007        Lancaster Heritage Land Co.    110 acres         30 years + three 5-year renewals\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nAll land lease agreements have been fully executed and are currently held in escrow with Commonwealth Title & Escrow LLC pending satisfaction of applicable conditions. The Revised Site Plan dated September 1, 2024, has been submitted as part of the application record and reflects the current project layout incorporating modifications made in response to Township staff and public comments received during the hearing process.\n\n**[Response to Public Hearing Concerns]{.underline}**\n\nThroughout the three public hearings, members of the public and neighboring property owners raised a number of substantive concerns regarding the Project. The Applicant takes these concerns seriously and has endeavored to address each category of concern through project design modifications, supplemental studies, and proposed conditions of approval. The following discussion addresses each principal area of concern in turn.\n\n**A. Visual and Aesthetic Impact**\n\nThe Applicant acknowledges the concerns expressed by several neighboring property owners regarding the potential visual impact of the Project on the rural character of the surrounding landscape. In response, the Applicant has committed to installing a Type C vegetative screening buffer along the northern and eastern project boundaries, specifically in areas adjacent to non-participating properties containing occupied residences. The vegetative screening plan will incorporate a native species mix appropriate for the Lancaster County region, including species such as Eastern red cedar, American holly, and native viburnum varieties. All plantings will have a minimum installed height of four feet, with an expected mature height of twelve to fifteen feet, ensuring effective visual screening within three to five growing seasons. The Applicant is willing to work collaboratively with the Township and affected property owners in finalizing the screening plan to ensure that plantings are appropriately situated and maintained. In addition, the Project\\'s perimeter fencing will be at least seven feet in height and will include wildlife passage openings at intervals no greater than 500 feet to facilitate the movement of small and medium-sized wildlife. The solar panels are designed with a maximum height of 14.5 feet at full tilt, which is well under any 20-foot height limitation applicable in the A-R district.\n\n**B. Noise.** Several residents expressed concerns regarding potential noise impacts from the operation of the solar facility and BESS components. In response, the Applicant commissioned a comprehensive noise impact assessment prepared by Ridgepoint Environmental Consultants LLC, under the direction of Lead Consultant Priya Mukherjee. The noise study was conducted in accordance with ANSI/ASA S12.9-2013/Part 2 methodology, which established ambient sound level baselines at multiple receptor locations surrounding the Project site through continuous monitoring over a period of seven consecutive days. The study demonstrates that the facility, under worst-case operational scenarios --- including all 40 string inverters and all 3 central power conversion stations operating at full capacity simultaneously --- will not exceed 45 dBA at the nearest non-participating property line. The BESS components, including thermal management systems and associated auxiliary equipment, were also modeled as part of the worst-case scenario analysis. The results confirm that the combined operational noise from all Project components will remain within acceptable thresholds at all modeled receptor locations. Based on the findings of this study, the Applicant accepts a condition of approval limiting operational noise to 45 dBA at the nearest non-participating property line, as measured in accordance with the ANSI/ASA S12.9-2013/Part 2 standard referenced in the noise study.\n\n**C. Property Values**\n\nThe Applicant acknowledges the concerns raised by neighboring property owners regarding potential impacts on residential property values. The Board will recall that a property value impact assessment was prepared by the Clarendon Appraisal Group, under the direction of Lead Appraiser Michael Engstrom, MAI, at the Township\\'s direction and at the Applicant\\'s expense. That study, which analyzed comparable sales data and utilized paired-sales methodology across multiple jurisdictions with existing utility-scale solar installations, concluded that there is no statistically significant negative impact on property values for residences located beyond 500 feet of a solar project boundary. The Applicant notes that the setback requirements proposed as conditions of approval provide additional protection: solar panels must be set back a minimum of 500 feet from any occupied dwelling on a non-participating parcel, and BESS components must be set back a minimum of 1,000 feet from any such dwelling. These generous setbacks substantially exceed the distances at which the Clarendon study found no measurable impact.\n\n**D. Agricultural Land Preservation**\n\nThe Applicant acknowledges the deep significance of the Project\\'s location on agricultural land and Pinnacle\\'s sincere respect for the farming heritage of Lancaster County and Conestoga Township in particular. We recognize that the conversion of productive farmland to solar energy generation is a matter of legitimate concern for the community, and we have taken meaningful steps to mitigate the impact of the Project on the agricultural resource base.\n\nBased on USDA Natural Resources Conservation Service soils mapping, 623 acres of the 850-acre Project site have been classified as prime agricultural land. In recognition of this, the Applicant has agreed to make an agricultural mitigation payment to the Lancaster Farmland Trust in the amount of \\$1,200 per acre of prime agricultural land converted, for a total agricultural mitigation payment of \\$747,600 (calculated as 623 acres multiplied by \\$1,200 per acre). This payment will be made in full prior to commencement of construction and will support the Lancaster Farmland Trust\\'s ongoing efforts to permanently preserve working farmland in Lancaster County through the acquisition of agricultural conservation easements.\n\nIn addition, the Project has been designed to maintain agricultural compatibility during its operational life. The Applicant intends to implement a managed grazing program utilizing sheep or, alternatively, to establish native pollinator habitat plantings under and between the panel rows, both of which have been demonstrated to provide meaningful ecological and agricultural co-benefits. Finally, the Applicant\\'s decommissioning obligations, discussed further below, will ensure that the land can be fully restored to agricultural use at the end of the Project\\'s useful life.\n\n**E. Stormwater and Erosion**\n\nThe Applicant submitted a preliminary stormwater management plan as part of the Conditional Use Application. The plan was prepared in accordance with the Lancaster County Act 167 Stormwater Management Plan and the Pennsylvania Department of Environmental Protection Chapter 102 requirements governing erosion and sediment control. A final stormwater management plan will be prepared and submitted for review and approval by the Lancaster County Conservation District, and subsequently to the Township Engineer, Gregory Shultz, P.E., of Fieldstone Engineering Associates, prior to issuance of any grading or building permits. The Applicant will obtain all required NPDES General Permit coverage (PAG-02) from the Pennsylvania DEP prior to commencement of earth disturbance activities. In addition, the Applicant will prepare and submit a comprehensive Erosion and Sedimentation Control Plan to the Lancaster County Conservation District for review and approval, and will ensure that all erosion and sediment control best management practices are installed and functioning prior to any land disturbance activities on the Project site.\n\n**F. Traffic and Road Impacts**\n\nThe traffic impact study submitted with the Application demonstrates that the Project\\'s construction phase will generate approximately 50 to 80 vehicle trips per day during peak construction activity, with vehicle types consisting primarily of construction worker personal vehicles, material delivery trucks, and specialized equipment transporters. During operations, traffic will decline substantially to approximately 2 to 5 vehicle trips per day for routine maintenance and monitoring activities. Construction access via Township roads will be coordinated with the Township Engineer, Gregory Shultz, P.E., to minimize disruption to local traffic patterns, and the Applicant will comply with all posted weight restrictions or, where necessary, seek appropriate permits for overweight loads.\n\nTo ensure that any road damage attributable to Project construction or operations is promptly repaired, the Applicant has agreed to post a road maintenance bond of \\$500,000 during the construction period, to be reduced to \\$100,000 during the operational period. The bond will remain in place for the duration of the Project\\'s operational life and will be released only upon the Applicant\\'s satisfactory completion of road restoration obligations as confirmed by the Township Engineer. Road improvement specifications will be developed in coordination with Gregory Shultz, P.E., and the Township\\'s Road Department.\n\n**[Environmental Compliance Summary]{.underline}**\n\nThe Applicant has completed or initiated the following environmental due diligence activities in connection with the Project:\n\n**Phase I Environmental Site Assessment.** A Phase I Environmental Site Assessment was completed in May 2024 in accordance with ASTM E1527-21 standards. The assessment identified no Recognized Environmental Conditions (RECs), Controlled RECs, or Historical RECs on any of the seven parcels comprising the Project site.\n\n**Cultural Resources Survey.** A cultural resources survey was completed in April 2024 by Ridgepoint Environmental Consultants LLC. The survey identified one potentially eligible historic resource --- the Stoltzfus Farmstead (circa 1847) --- located within approximately 200 feet of the Project boundary on parcel 120-45-001. The Applicant has initiated consultation with the Pennsylvania Historical and Museum Commission (\\\"PHMC\\\") regarding the Project\\'s potential effects on this resource and is committed to completing a Section 106-equivalent review process in coordination with PHMC. The Applicant will obtain written confirmation from PHMC regarding the Project\\'s effect on the Stoltzfus Farmstead, and any required mitigation measures, prior to commencement of any grading activities on parcels adjacent to the farmstead.\n\n**Threatened and Endangered Species.** A Pennsylvania Natural Diversity Inventory (\\\"PNDI\\\") review identified potential habitat for the bog turtle (*Glyptemys muhlenbergii*), a federally listed threatened species, on parcels 120-45-003 and 120-45-004, both owned by Brubaker Agricultural LLC. Ridgepoint Environmental Consultants LLC is coordinating with the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service regarding Phase 2 habitat assessment requirements for those parcels. The Applicant is committed to completing all necessary federal and state threatened and endangered species consultations and obtaining all required clearances prior to any land disturbance on those parcels.\n\n**Solar Glare Analysis.** The Applicant will prepare and submit a solar glare analysis utilizing the Sandia National Laboratories GlareGauge tool (or equivalent), demonstrating that the Project will not produce significant glare impacts on public roadways or occupied residences within one mile of the Project boundary. The glare analysis will be submitted to the Township for review and comment prior to issuance of building permits.\n\n**[Financial Assurances and Community Benefits]{.underline}**\n\n**A. PILOT Agreement**\n\nThe Applicant has negotiated proposed Payment in Lieu of Taxes (\\\"PILOT\\\") terms with the Township. Under the proposed PILOT structure, the Applicant will pay \\$385,000 per year for Years 1 through 15 of commercial operations. Commencing in Year 16, the annual PILOT payment will be subject to a 2% annual escalator, resulting in a Year 16 payment of \\$392,700. The Applicant also remains responsible for all applicable Conestoga Valley School District real estate taxes assessed on non-exempt improvements associated with the Project. The estimated total annual tax revenue to the Township is approximately \\$427,000, consisting of \\$385,000 in PILOT payments and approximately \\$42,000 in real estate taxes on non-exempt improvements. The formal PILOT agreement is being finalized in coordination with the Township Solicitor and will be executed in a form satisfactory to the Township prior to the issuance of any building permits for the Project.\n\n**B. Decommissioning.** The Applicant is committed to the responsible decommissioning of the Project at the end of its useful life. The initial decommissioning cost estimate, prepared by a licensed professional engineer, projects a gross decommissioning cost of \\$8.4 million, offset by an estimated salvage value of \\$2.1 million for recyclable materials (principally steel, aluminum, copper, and silicon), yielding a net decommissioning cost of \\$6.3 million. In accordance with the proposed conditions of approval, the Applicant will post decommissioning security in an amount equal to 125% of the net decommissioning cost, or \\$7,875,000. This security will be posted prior to issuance of the first building permit for the Project. Acceptable forms of security include a surety bond, an irrevocable letter of credit, or an escrow account maintained at a financial institution rated A- or better by a nationally recognized credit rating agency. The Applicant is currently in discussions with Northbrook Surety Company regarding the issuance of a surety bond. The decommissioning cost estimate will be updated every five years by a licensed professional engineer, and the security amount will be adjusted accordingly. The decommissioning plan ensures that all above-ground and below-ground project components (to a depth of 36 inches) will be removed and the land restored to a condition suitable for agricultural use within 12 months of facility deactivation.\n\n**C. Road Maintenance Bond**\n\nAs described in Section IV.F above, the Applicant has agreed to post a road maintenance bond of \\$500,000 during the construction period, to be reduced to \\$100,000 during the operational period, to address any road damage attributable to the Project.\n\n**D. Agricultural Mitigation**\n\nAs described in Section IV.D above, the Applicant has agreed to make an agricultural mitigation payment of \\$747,600 to the Lancaster Farmland Trust, calculated on the basis of 623 acres of prime agricultural land at \\$1,200 per acre.\n\n**[Summary of Applicant\\'s Accepted Conditions]{.underline}**\n\nThe Applicant understands and accepts the following conditions of approval as discussed during the public hearing process and as recommended by the Lancaster County Planning Commission in its advisory letter dated July 22, 2024. The Applicant respectfully submits the following summary of its understanding of the principal conditions:\n\n> 1\\. **Setback Requirements.** Compliance with all applicable setback requirements, including: 100 feet from non-participating property lines for solar panels; 300 feet from non-participating property lines for BESS components; 150 feet from public road rights-of-way; 500 feet from occupied dwellings on non-participating parcels for solar panels; 1,000 feet from occupied dwellings on non-participating parcels for BESS components; and 250 feet from occupied dwellings on non-participating parcels for substations and inverter stations.\n>\n> 2\\. **Vegetative Screening.** Installation and maintenance of a Type C vegetative screening buffer along the northern and eastern project boundaries adjacent to non-participating properties with occupied residences, utilizing native species with a minimum installed height of four feet.\n>\n> 3\\. **Noise Limitation.** Operational noise shall not exceed 45 dBA at the nearest non-participating property line, as measured in accordance with the methodology set forth in the noise impact assessment.\n>\n> 4\\. **Solar Glare Analysis.** Preparation and submission of a solar glare analysis demonstrating no significant glare impact on public roadways or occupied residences within one mile.\n>\n> 5\\. **Decommissioning Security.** Posting of decommissioning security in the amount of \\$7,875,000, representing 125% of the estimated net decommissioning cost of \\$6.3 million, prior to issuance of the first building permit.\n>\n> 6\\. **PILOT Agreement.** Execution of a PILOT agreement providing for annual payments of \\$385,000 for Years 1 through 15, with a 2% annual escalator commencing in Year 16.\n>\n> 7\\. **Road Maintenance Bond.** Posting of a road maintenance bond in the amount of \\$500,000 during the construction period, reduced to \\$100,000 during the operational period.\n>\n> 8\\. **Agricultural Mitigation.** Payment of \\$747,600 to the Lancaster Farmland Trust, representing an agricultural mitigation payment of \\$1,200 per acre for 623 acres of prime agricultural land.\n>\n> 9\\. **Stormwater Management.** Submission and approval of a final stormwater management plan by the Lancaster County Conservation District and the Township Engineer prior to issuance of grading permits.\n>\n> 10\\. **Environmental and Cultural Resource Consultations.** Completion of all required consultations with the Pennsylvania Historical and Museum Commission and the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, and receipt of all necessary clearances, prior to commencement of land disturbance activities on affected parcels.\n>\n> 11\\. **Perimeter Fencing.** Installation of perimeter fencing with a minimum height of seven feet, incorporating wildlife passage openings at intervals no greater than 500 feet.\n>\n> 12\\. **NPDES and E&S Permits.** Obtaining all required NPDES and Erosion and Sedimentation Control permits from the Pennsylvania DEP and Lancaster County Conservation District.\n>\n> 13\\. **Annual Reporting.** Submission of an annual report to the Board of Supervisors summarizing operational status, maintenance activities, complaint logs, and compliance with conditions of approval.\n>\n> 14\\. **FAA Determination.** Obtaining an FAA determination of no hazard, if applicable, for any project structures exceeding 200 feet in height.\n>\n> 15\\. **General Compliance.** Compliance with all applicable federal, state, and local laws, regulations, and ordinances throughout the construction and operational life of the Project.\n\n**[Closing and Request for Approval]{.underline}**\n\nThe Applicant wishes to express its sincere appreciation for the Board\\'s thorough and diligent review of the Conditional Use Application, the meaningful engagement of members of the public throughout the hearing process, and the professionalism and responsiveness of Township staff in administering this proceeding. The Brightfield Solar Project represents a capital investment of approximately \\$200 million in Conestoga Township, will generate an estimated \\$427,000 in annual tax revenue to the Township and the Conestoga Valley School District, will support approximately 300 to 400 jobs during the peak construction period, and will provide long-term clean energy generation sufficient to power approximately 25,000 Pennsylvania homes.\n\nThe Applicant respectfully submits that the Brightfield Solar Project meets all applicable requirements of Section 27-605(B)(14) of the Conestoga Township Zoning Ordinance and the general conditional use standards set forth in Sections 603(c)(2) and 913.2 of the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code, 53 P.S. §§ 10603(c)(2), 10913.2. On the basis of the record developed during this proceeding, the Applicant respectfully requests that the Board vote to approve the Conditional Use Application at its November 18, 2024 meeting, subject to the conditions summarized herein.\n\nThe Applicant\\'s legal counsel, Christine Navarro, Esq., of Hathaway & Lowell LLP, and our environmental consultants at Ridgepoint Environmental Consultants LLC, remain available to address any additional questions the Board or Township staff may have in advance of the November 18 meeting.\n\nRespectfully submitted,\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nDerek Holcomb Vice President of Development Pinnacle Renewables LLC 4200 Commerce Parkway, Suite 310 Wilmington, DE 19801 Tel: (302) 555-8147\n\nEmail: dholcomb@pinnaclerenewables.com\n\ncc: Christine Navarro, Esq., Hathaway & Lowell LLP cc: Priya Mukherjee, Ridgepoint Environmental Consultants LLC cc: Gregory Shultz, P.E., Fieldstone Engineering Associates cc: Margaret Tsai, CEO, Pinnacle Renewables LLC\n\n**Enclosures:**\n\n> 1\\. Revised Site Plan (dated September 1, 2024)\n>\n> 2\\. Noise Impact Assessment (Ridgepoint Environmental Consultants LLC; ANSI/ASA S12.9-2013/Part 2 methodology)\n>\n> 3\\. Preliminary Stormwater Management Plan\n>\n> 4\\. Decommissioning Cost Estimate and Plan\n>\n> 5\\. Phase I Environmental Site Assessment (May 2024)\n>\n> 6\\. Cultural Resources Survey Report (April 2024)\n>\n> 7\\. PNDI Receipt and Agency Correspondence\n>\n> 8\\. Property Value Impact Assessment (Clarendon Appraisal Group)\n>\n> 9\\. Traffic Impact Study\n>\n> 10\\. Draft PILOT Agreement Terms Sheet\n"}
