{"turn": 6, "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "call_00_6E1DQ3Mld3WPGppQrhq40160", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"documents/planning-commission-advisory-letter.docx\"}", "result": "**LANCASTER COUNTY PLANNING COMMISSION** 150 North Queen Street, Suite 320 Lancaster, PA 17603\n\nTelephone: (717) 299-8333 \\| Facsimile: (717) 295-3659\n\nJuly 22, 2024\n\nHarold Zimmerman, Chair Conestoga Township Board of Supervisors Conestoga Township Municipal Building 1217 River Road Conestoga, PA 17516\n\n**Re: Advisory Review and Recommendation --- Conditional Use Application No. CU-2024-012, Brightfield Solar Project, Pinnacle Renewables LLC**\n\nDear Chair Zimmerman:\n\nThe Lancaster County Planning Commission (\\\"Commission\\\") has completed its advisory review of Conditional Use Application No. CU-2024-012, filed by Pinnacle Renewables LLC (\\\"Applicant\\\") on June 3, 2024, for the proposed Brightfield Solar Project. This review was conducted pursuant to Section 609.1 of the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code (Act 247, as amended), which requires that conditional use applications be referred to the county planning commission for advisory review and comment prior to final action by the governing body. This letter constitutes the Commission\\'s non-binding advisory recommendation to the Conestoga Township Board of Supervisors. The application was reviewed at the Commission\\'s regularly scheduled meeting on July 17, 2024, and the findings and recommendations set forth herein reflect the consensus of the Commission at that meeting. In preparing this letter, the Commission reviewed the application materials and supporting documentation submitted by the Applicant, the Conestoga Township Zoning Ordinance (Chapter 27, as amended December 2023), the applicable provisions of the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code, and relevant county planning policies.\n\n**[I. PROJECT SUMMARY]{.underline}**\n\nThe Applicant, Pinnacle Renewables LLC, is a Delaware limited liability company with principal offices at 4200 Commerce Parkway, Suite 310, Wilmington, DE 19801. The project entity is Brightfield Solar Project LLC, a Pennsylvania limited liability company and wholly owned subsidiary of Pinnacle Renewables LLC, which will serve as the owner and operator of the proposed facility. The proposed project consists of a 120 MW AC (156 MW DC) single-axis tracking photovoltaic solar energy facility with an optional 40 MW / 160 MWh battery energy storage system (\\\"BESS\\\"). The facility would be located on approximately 850 acres across seven contiguous parcels situated in the Agricultural-Rural (A-R) zoning district of Conestoga Township, Lancaster County, Pennsylvania. The parcels comprising the project site are identified as follows:\n\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Parcel ID**           **Owner(s)**                   **Acreage**\n  ----------------------- ------------------------------ -----------------------\n  120-45-001              Elmer J. & Ruth A. Stoltzfus   185.3 acres\n\n  120-45-002              Stoltzfus Family Trust         142.7 acres\n\n  120-45-003              Brubaker Agricultural LLC      210.4 acres\n\n  120-45-004              Brubaker Agricultural LLC      98.6 acres\n\n  120-45-005              David M. Herr                  75.2 acres\n\n  120-45-006              Lancaster Heritage Land Co.    88.9 acres\n\n  120-45-007              Lancaster Heritage Land Co.    48.9 acres\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe project as proposed includes approximately 296,000 solar modules mounted on single-axis tracking structures, 40 string inverters, and 3 central power conversion stations. The total fenced area encompasses approximately 720 acres, with panel coverage of approximately 580 acres. Perimeter fencing will consist of 7-foot chain-link fencing with wildlife passage openings at approximately 500-foot intervals. Electrical interconnection to the regional transmission grid will be accomplished via a 2.3-mile, 138 kV generation tie line (\\\"gen-tie line\\\") connecting the project substation to the existing Conestoga Substation. The Applicant holds interconnection queue position AG2-2024-0847 with Mid-Atlantic Grid Coordination LLC.\n\nThe estimated total project cost is approximately \\$200 million, consisting of approximately \\$168 million for the solar facility and approximately \\$32 million for the BESS. The projected commercial operation date is December 31, 2027. Pursuant to Section 27-605(B)(14) of the Conestoga Township Zoning Ordinance, solar energy systems exceeding 10 MW in generating capacity are classified as a Conditional Use in the Agricultural-Rural (A-R) zoning district, thereby requiring approval by the Board of Supervisors following public hearings.\n\n**[II. CONSISTENCY WITH COUNTY COMPREHENSIVE PLAN]{.underline}**\n\nThe Commission has evaluated the proposed Brightfield Solar Project for general consistency with the policies and objectives of the Lancaster County Comprehensive Plan. The County Comprehensive Plan supports the diversification of energy sources throughout the county and encourages the responsible siting of renewable energy facilities in a manner that balances economic development with the preservation of community character and natural resources. The Commission acknowledges the inherent tension between renewable energy development and the preservation of agricultural land, which remains a core priority of the County Comprehensive Plan. The project site consists primarily of prime agricultural soils, with approximately 623 acres classified as prime agricultural land under the United States Department of Agriculture Natural Resources Conservation Service soil classifications.\n\nWhile the proposed project will convert a significant area of prime agricultural land to solar energy use for the duration of the project\\'s operational life, the Commission notes that the land lease agreements submitted by the Applicant provide for decommissioning and full restoration of the land to agricultural condition following the useful life of the facility. The reversible nature of the land use conversion is an important consideration in the Commission\\'s assessment. Nevertheless, the Commission recommends that the Township require an agricultural mitigation payment to offset the temporary conversion of prime agricultural land, with such payment directed to Lancaster Farmland Trust at a rate consistent with prevailing agricultural conservation easement values in Lancaster County. The Commission finds that, on balance, the proposed project is generally consistent with the County Comprehensive Plan, subject to conditions sufficient to mitigate impacts on agricultural resources, neighboring properties, and community infrastructure.\n\n**[III. REVIEW FINDINGS AND RECOMMENDATIONS]{.underline}**\n\nThe Commission offers the following specific findings and recommendations for the Township\\'s consideration in its deliberations on this application.\n\n**A. Setbacks and Dimensional Requirements**\n\nThe Commission has reviewed the Applicant\\'s site plan in relation to the setback and dimensional requirements established by Section 27-605(B)(14) of the Conestoga Township Zoning Ordinance. Based on the materials submitted, the Commission finds that the site plan generally complies with the applicable setback requirements as follows: solar panels are set back a minimum of 100 feet from any non-participating property line; solar infrastructure is set back a minimum of 150 feet from any public road right-of-way; all solar components are located a minimum of 500 feet from any occupied dwelling on a non-participating parcel; and substation and inverter equipment are set back a minimum of 250 feet from any non-participating property line. With respect to the battery energy storage system, the proposed battery energy storage system setback of 250 feet from non-participating property lines is consistent with the ordinance. The BESS is also set back a minimum of 1,000 feet from any occupied dwelling on a non-participating parcel, which satisfies the ordinance requirement for energy storage facilities in proximity to residential structures. The proposed solar panel maximum height at full tilt is 14.5 feet, which is well within the 20-foot maximum height permitted by the ordinance. The Commission recommends that the Township confirm that all setbacks are accurately depicted on the final site plan prior to the issuance of any building or zoning permits.\n\n**B. Screening and Visual Mitigation**\n\nThe Commission recommends the installation of vegetative screening buffers along all project boundaries adjacent to non-participating properties with occupied residences, with particular attention to the northern and eastern boundaries of the project site where the proximity to existing residential structures is greatest. The Commission recommends a Type C vegetative screening buffer utilizing native species, with a minimum planting height of 4 feet at the time of installation and an expected mature height of 12 to 15 feet. The screening plan should be reviewed and approved by the Township Engineer prior to implementation. The Commission considers visual screening to be an important measure for community acceptance and the protection of neighboring property values. The Commission notes that the Township has retained Clarendon Appraisal Group (Michael Engstrom, MAI) to prepare a property value impact assessment and encourages completion of that assessment prior to final action on the conditional use application. Visual screening buffers should be installed along all required frontages to ensure adequate mitigation of visual impacts on surrounding properties and public roadways.\n\n**C. Stormwater Management**\n\nGiven the scale of the proposed project, the Commission recommends that the Applicant submit a comprehensive stormwater management plan prepared in accordance with the Pennsylvania Stormwater Best Management Practices Manual and the Lancaster County Act 167 Stormwater Management Plan. The stormwater management plan should be reviewed and approved by both the Lancaster County Conservation District and the Township Engineer, Gregory Shultz, P.E., of Fieldstone Engineering Associates. The Commission notes particular concern regarding the extent of impervious and semi-impervious surface coverage, as approximately 580 acres of panel coverage has the potential to concentrate and redirect stormwater runoff in ways that may adversely affect adjacent agricultural operations and downstream waterways. The Commission recommends post-construction stormwater monitoring for a minimum period of three years following the commencement of commercial operations. The Applicant should obtain an NPDES General Permit (PAG-02) prior to any land disturbance activities, and the Township should require an Erosion and Sedimentation Control Plan approved by the Lancaster County Conservation District as a condition of any grading or building permit.\n\n**D. Environmental and Cultural Resources**\n\nThe Applicant submitted a Phase I Environmental Site Assessment completed in May 2024, which indicated no Recognized Environmental Conditions (RECs) on the project site. A Cultural Resources Survey was completed by Ridgepoint Environmental Consultants LLC (Priya Mukherjee, Lead Consultant) in April 2024. That survey identified one potentially eligible historic resource --- the Stoltzfus Farmstead, dating to approximately 1847 --- located within 200 feet of the project boundary. The Commission recommends that the Township require formal consultation with the Pennsylvania Historical and Museum Commission (PHMC) regarding the Stoltzfus Farmstead and obtain written confirmation of no adverse effect prior to the issuance of any grading permits for adjacent areas.\n\nAdditionally, the Pennsylvania Natural Diversity Inventory (PNDI) review conducted by the Applicant identified potential habitat for the bog turtle (*Glyptemys muhlenbergii*), a species listed as threatened under the federal Endangered Species Act, on parcels 120-45-003 and 120-45-004. The Commission recommends that the Township require completion of all necessary federal and state consultations regarding threatened and endangered species prior to any land disturbance on the affected parcels. The Applicant should coordinate directly with the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service regarding potential bog turtle habitat and provide documentation of the outcome of such consultation to the Township.\n\n**E. Decommissioning.** The Commission recommends that the Township require a comprehensive decommissioning plan and adequate financial security to ensure removal of the facility and restoration of the site at the end of the project\\'s useful life. The Applicant has submitted a preliminary decommissioning cost estimate of \\$8.4 million (gross) less \\$2.1 million in anticipated salvage value, yielding a net decommissioning cost estimate of \\$6.3 million. The Commission recommends that the Township require financial security in an amount equal to at least 125% of the estimated net decommissioning cost, which would equal \\$7,875,000 at current estimates. The decommissioning cost estimate should be updated every five years by a licensed professional engineer, with corresponding adjustments to the required financial security. Acceptable forms of financial security should include a surety bond, an irrevocable letter of credit, or a funded escrow account held at a creditworthy financial institution. Financial security should be posted prior to the issuance of the first building permit for the project.\n\n**F. Infrastructure and Road Impacts**\n\nConstruction of a project of this scale and cost --- estimated at \\$200 million --- will generate significant heavy vehicle traffic during the construction period, with the potential to cause damage to local Township roads. The Commission recommends that the Applicant be required to post a road maintenance bond for the duration of the construction period to protect Township roads from damage. The Applicant should conduct pre-construction and post-construction road condition surveys on all designated construction traffic routes, and the Township and Applicant should execute a formal road maintenance agreement addressing road repair obligations, load limits, traffic routing, and scheduling of construction activities. Coordination with the Township Engineer on these matters is essential. The Commission also notes that the proposed 2.3-mile, 138 kV gen-tie line to the Conestoga Substation traverses land outside the project site and may require additional approvals or coordination with affected landowners and potentially adjacent municipalities. The Township should confirm that all necessary easements and approvals for the gen-tie line are secured by the Applicant.\n\n**[IV. NOISE AND GLARE]{.underline}**\n\nThe Commission recommends that the Township impose a noise limit of 45 dBA measured at the nearest non-participating property line, consistent with the performance standards established in the Conestoga Township Zoning Ordinance. The Commission notes that the Applicant has submitted a noise study as part of the application materials. The Commission further recommends that a solar glare analysis be completed to assess potential glare impacts on public roadways and occupied residences within the vicinity of the project site. The Applicant should demonstrate that no significant glare impact will result within one mile of the project boundary, and the Township should consider requiring mitigation measures if the analysis identifies areas of concern.\n\n**[V. FINANCIAL AND COMMUNITY BENEFIT CONSIDERATIONS]{.underline}**\n\nThe Commission notes the Applicant\\'s proposed payment in lieu of taxes (\\\"PILOT\\\") agreement with the Township, which provides for annual payments of \\$385,000 for Years 1 through 15, with a 2% annual escalator thereafter for the remaining term of the project. The total estimated annual revenue to the Township, inclusive of the PILOT and real estate taxes on non-exempt improvements, is approximately \\$427,000. The Commission encourages the Township to negotiate a PILOT agreement that reflects the scale, duration, and community impact of the project, and to consider directing a portion of the PILOT revenue to community benefit purposes. Additionally, and as noted above, the Commission recommends an agricultural mitigation payment to Lancaster Farmland Trust to offset the conversion of approximately 623 acres of prime agricultural land. The Commission further notes the potential for local economic benefits during the construction period associated with the estimated \\$200 million project investment. The Applicant should be encouraged to provide annual reports to the Township regarding project operations, revenue payments, and compliance with conditions of approval.\n\n**[VI. OVERALL RECOMMENDATION]{.underline}**\n\nBased on the foregoing review, the Lancaster County Planning Commission recommends **approval** of Conditional Use Application No. CU-2024-012 for the Brightfield Solar Project, **subject to conditions** addressing the matters identified in this letter, including but not limited to agricultural mitigation, vegetative screening, stormwater management, environmental and cultural resource protection, decommissioning financial security, road maintenance, noise limits, and glare mitigation. This recommendation is advisory and non-binding pursuant to Section 609.1 of the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code. The final determination on the conditional use application rests solely with the Conestoga Township Board of Supervisors following the completion of public hearings as required by law. The Commission encourages the Township to give careful consideration to the recommendations set forth herein in formulating any conditions of approval. The Commission remains available to provide additional technical assistance upon request.\n\nRespectfully submitted,\n\nLANCASTER COUNTY PLANNING COMMISSION\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nThomas Reinhart Director of Planning\n\nDate: July 22, 2024\n\n**cc:**\n\n> Barbara Kreider, Zoning Hearing Officer, Lancaster County Planning Commission Derek Holcomb, Vice President of Development, Pinnacle Renewables LLC Gregory Shultz, P.E., Fieldstone Engineering Associates (Township Engineer) Conestoga Township Board of Supervisors (all members)\n"}
