{"turn": 7, "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "call_00_yQh6yBEE15Uoe0eB5vPj4944", "tool_name": "bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd $DOCUMENTS_DIR && cat township-engineer-email.eml\"}", "result": "From: Gregory Shultz, P.E. <gshultz@fieldstoneengineering.com>\nTo: Derek Holcomb <dholcomb@pinnaclerenewables.com>\nCc: Harold Zimmerman <hzimmerman@conestogatwp.org>\nDate: Fri, 10 Jan 2025 02:47:00 -0000\nSubject:\n RE: Brightfield Solar Project — Post-Approval Condition Implementation Items\nContent-Type: text/plain; charset=\"utf-8\"\nContent-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable\nMIME-Version: 1.0\n\nDerek,\n\nThanks for the email =E2=80=94 good to hear from you. I can confirm that the =\nappeal period on the December 6, 2024 Decision and Order expired on January 5=\nth with no appeal filed, so we're clear to move forward on implementation in =\nearnest. Glad we can get moving on this. It's been a long road since the firs=\nt hearing back in June, and I know your team is eager to start putting shovel=\ns in the ground. I wanted to get back to you with some thoughts on the items =\nyou raised, plus a few additional things on my end.\n\n**Stormwater Management Plan Review**\n\nThis is probably the most time-sensitive item, so I'll start here. As you kno=\nw, Condition 10 of the Decision requires that the final post-construction sto=\nrmwater management plan be submitted to me as Township Engineer within 90 day=\ns of the Decision date, which puts the deadline at March 6, 2025. Condition 1=\n1 further requires that the stormwater plan be reviewed and approved by the L=\nancaster County Conservation District prior to submission to the Township Eng=\nineer.\n\nI'd strongly encourage your team to get the plan submitted to the Conservatio=\nn District as soon as possible if you haven't already. In my experience, the =\nConservation District typically takes 60 to 90 days to review and act on a st=\normwater plan for a project of this scale. They've been busy lately with seve=\nral warehouse projects in the county, so I wouldn't count on a quick turnarou=\nnd. The sooner your civil engineer gets a complete application package in fro=\nnt of them, the better positioned you'll be.\n\nOn our end at Fieldstone, I'm happy to do an informal pre-submission review o=\nf the stormwater plan before it's formally submitted to me as Township Engine=\ner. Given the size of this project =E2=80=94 850 acres total, approximately 7=\n20 acres of fenced area =E2=80=94 this is one of the larger stormwater plans =\nthe Township has dealt with, and early coordination will help reduce back-and=\n-forth once we're in the formal review process. I'd encourage Pinnacle's civi=\nl engineer to reach out to me directly to set up a meeting. We can go through=\n the design approach, any modeling questions, and make sure we're aligned on =\nmethodology before formal submission.\n\n**Vegetative Screening / Buffer Planting**\n\nI've reviewed Condition 16 regarding the Type C vegetative screening buffer a=\nlong the northern and eastern project boundaries and have some practical thou=\nghts on implementation. As you know, the Decision specifies a native species =\nmix with a minimum planting height of 4 feet at installation and an expected =\nmature height of 12 to 15 feet. The screening along those boundaries is parti=\ncularly important given the proximity to occupied residences on non-participa=\nting parcels =E2=80=94 this was a significant concern raised by the public du=\nring the hearings, and I know Harold and the rest of the Board of Supervisors=\n view this as a priority item.\n\nFrom our perspective, we would want to see the screening buffer planted prior=\n to energization of the facility, so that it's in place before the project go=\nes live. That gives the plantings at least some time to begin establishing be=\nfore the project is fully operational, which I think goes a long way with the=\n neighboring property owners.\n\nOn a practical note, depending on the species your landscaping contractor sel=\nects, fall or early spring planting is generally ideal for establishment here=\n in Lancaster County. I'd recommend your contractor coordinate with me on spe=\ncies selection and planting schedule so we can make sure the timeline works w=\nith construction sequencing.\n\n**Road Improvements and Access**\n\nA few items on the road side. We'll need Derek to confirm the proposed haul r=\noutes for construction traffic =E2=80=94 specifically which Township roads wi=\nll be used for primary construction access. Once we know the routes, we'll ne=\ned a pre-construction road condition survey, including both video and photogr=\naphic documentation, for all affected roads. This protects both the Township =\nand Pinnacle in terms of documenting baseline conditions.\n\nAs a reminder, the Decision requires a $500,000 construction-period road main=\ntenance bond. We can discuss the specifics of bonding logistics and timing at=\n the coordination meeting I mention below.\n\nFor the project access point itself, the improvements =E2=80=94 turning radiu=\ns, sight distance, potential need for a deceleration lane =E2=80=94 will need=\n to be designed to PennDOT specifications if access is from a state road, or =\nto Township standards if from a Township road. Can you provide the anticipate=\nd construction traffic volume? I'm looking for estimated daily truck trips, v=\nehicle types, and duration of the heavy-traffic construction phase so Fieldst=\none can evaluate the adequacy of the existing road infrastructure.\n\n**General Coordination**\n\nA couple of additional items. I understand the NPDES General Permit (PAG-02) =\nfor construction stormwater will also need to be secured before any land dist=\nurbance, and the Erosion & Sediment Control Plan needs Conservation District =\napproval =E2=80=94 that's a separate but related review from the post-constru=\nction stormwater management plan, so keep both tracks moving in parallel.\n\nI'd like to suggest we set up a coordination meeting in the next few weeks =\n=E2=80=94 you, Pinnacle's civil engineer, myself, and potentially Priya Mukhe=\nrjee from Ridgepoint Environmental Consultants since the environmental permit=\nting and stormwater/erosion planning overlap quite a bit. We can host at our =\noffice at 220 Centerville Road here in Lancaster, or if the weather cooperate=\ns, a site visit might be productive. Let me know what works.\n\nI'm copying Harold Zimmerman on this to keep the Supervisors informed on wher=\ne things stand.\n\nHappy to discuss any of the above by phone =E2=80=94 feel free to call me dir=\nectly at (717) 556-0483.\n\nBest regards,\n\nGregory Shultz, P.E.\nSenior Project Engineer\nFieldstone Engineering Associates\n220 Centerville Road\nLancaster, PA 17601\nPhone: (717) 556-0483\nEmail: gshultz@fieldstoneengineering.com\n\n*Township Engineer =E2=80=94 Conestoga Township*\n"}
{"turn": 7, "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "call_01_gKze5Lm6MyjQuvw4sOqA1198", "tool_name": "bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd $DOCUMENTS_DIR && cat township-solicitor-pilot-email.eml\"}", "result": "From: Allen Driscoll, Esq. <adriscoll@driscoll-frey.com>\nTo: Christine Navarro <cnavarro@hathaway-lowell.com>\nCc: Harold Zimmerman <hzimmerman@conestogatwp.org>; Derek Holcomb <dholcomb@pinnaclerenewables.com>\nDate: Wed, 15 Jan 2025 03:47:00 -0000\nSubject: Brightfield Solar Project — PILOT Agreement: Outstanding Issues\nContent-Type: text/plain; charset=\"utf-8\"\nContent-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable\nMIME-Version: 1.0\n\nChristine,\n\nI hope the new year is treating you well. I wanted to follow up on the PILOT =\nagreement negotiations for the Brightfield Solar Project now that we have cle=\nared a significant procedural milestone.\n\nAs you know, the Board of Supervisors issued its Decision and Order on Decemb=\ner 6, 2024, granting conditional use approval to Pinnacle Renewables LLC and =\nBrightfield Solar Project LLC for the project. The 30-day appeal period under=\n Section 914.1 of the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code expired on Ja=\nnuary 5, 2025, and no appeal was filed. The Decision is now final. With that =\nbehind us, I want to turn our attention to resolving the remaining open issue=\ns on the PILOT agreement so we can get it across the finish line. As you are =\naware, Condition 30 of the Decision requires execution of a PILOT agreement \"=\nin a form satisfactory to the Township Solicitor\" prior to issuance of any bu=\nilding permits for the project. I have received and reviewed the most recent =\ndraft PILOT agreement your office circulated on January 8, 2025, and I have g=\none through it in detail with Chairman Zimmerman and the full Board of Superv=\nisors.\n\n**Agreed Terms =E2=80=94 General Framework**\n\nLet me start with the good news. The parties appear to have reached agreement=\n on the core PILOT structure, and I want to memorialize that common ground. W=\ne are aligned on the following: an annual payment of $385,000 per year during=\n the initial period, payable in lieu of real property taxes on the solar faci=\nlity and battery energy storage system (BESS) components. This PILOT is separ=\nate from and in addition to real estate taxes on non-exempt improvements, whi=\nch we estimate at approximately $42,000 per year and which Brightfield Solar =\nProject LLC will continue to pay through the normal assessment process. Schoo=\nl district real estate taxes are not subject to the PILOT and remain payable =\nin full. Taken together, the total estimated annual revenue to the Township f=\nrom the project is approximately $427,000 ($385,000 PILOT + $42,000 real esta=\nte taxes). We also agree that the agreement should identify Brightfield Solar=\n Project LLC =E2=80=94 the Pennsylvania LLC and project SPE =E2=80=94 as the =\nobligor, and that the PILOT term should be coterminous with the useful life o=\nf the facility or the duration of the conditional use approval, whichever is =\nshorter. All of this is consistent with how your January 8 draft is structure=\nd, and we have no quarrel with those provisions.\n\nThat said, there are two substantive issues that remain open, and I need to f=\nlag both as material from the Township's perspective.\n\n**Open Issue #1 =E2=80=94 Most-Favored-Nation Clause**\n\nThe Township continues to request inclusion of a most-favored-nation (\"MFN\") =\nclause in the PILOT agreement. As we discussed during our call in December, t=\nhe Board's position is straightforward: if any other utility-scale solar ener=\ngy project in Lancaster County enters into a PILOT or similar tax agreement w=\nith any municipality in the county at a per-MW or per-acre rate that exceeds =\nthe Brightfield rate, the Brightfield PILOT payment would be automatically ad=\njusted upward to match the higher rate. Chairman Zimmerman and the Board are =\nparticularly focused on this point because the Brightfield project is one of =\nthe first large-scale solar facilities in Lancaster County. The Township does=\n not want to find itself locked into below-market terms if the market for sol=\nar PILOTs evolves over the next several years =E2=80=94 and given the pace of=\n development in the region, it very likely will. For reference, the project's=\n current PILOT rate works out to approximately $3,208 per MW per year ($385,0=\n00 =C3=B7 120 MW), or approximately $453 per acre per year ($385,000 =C3=B7 8=\n50 acres). The Township wants assurance that these figures remain competitive=\n over the life of the agreement. I understand that your January 8 draft rejec=\nted the MFN clause, and I have reviewed Pinnacle's position that such a claus=\ne creates uncertainty for project financing and investor returns. We take tha=\nt concern seriously, but the Township considers this a material term. I would=\n ask that you and Derek reconsider the MFN provision, or alternatively propos=\ne a protective mechanism that addresses the Township's concern =E2=80=94 for =\nexample, a periodic rate review at defined intervals, or a floor-and-cap stru=\ncture that provides some upside protection while giving Pinnacle a ceiling fo=\nr budgeting purposes. I should also note that the Township is aware Pinnacle =\nRenewables has a 2.4 GW development pipeline across Mid-Atlantic Grid Coordin=\nation LLC territory and may be developing other projects in Lancaster County.=\n The MFN clause would simply ensure consistent treatment across those project=\ns and give the Board confidence that Conestoga Township is not disadvantaged =\nby being an early mover.\n\n**Open Issue #2 =E2=80=94 Escalator Commencement Date**\n\nThe second open issue is the commencement date for the 2% annual escalation o=\nf the PILOT payment. The Township's position is that the 2% annual escalator =\nshould begin in Year 11 of the PILOT term =E2=80=94 meaning the PILOT payment=\n would remain at $385,000 per year for Years 1 through 10, and then increase =\nby 2% per year starting in Year 11. Your January 8 draft, by contrast, provid=\nes that the 2% annual escalator does not begin until Year 16, holding the pay=\nment flat at $385,000 for the first 15 years with escalation commencing only =\nin Year 16. The financial significance of this disagreement is not trivial. U=\nnder the Township's proposed Year 11 start, the Year 11 payment would be $392=\n,700, and by Year 15 the payment would reach approximately $425,048 ($385,000=\n =C3=97 1.02^5). Under Pinnacle's proposed Year 16 start, payments remain fla=\nt at $385,000 through Year 15, with the first escalated payment of $392,700 n=\not arriving until Year 16. The cumulative difference over the first 15 years =\nis significant to the Township's long-term revenue projections and budget pla=\nnning. The Township's view is that a Year 11 escalator is eminently reasonabl=\ne given the anticipated operational life of 30-plus years and the inevitable =\nerosion of purchasing power from inflation over that period. I will note that=\n the Decision and Order references \"negotiated PILOT terms\" but does not spec=\nify the escalator commencement year, so this is a point for the agreement its=\nelf to resolve. However, I want to be direct: Condition 30 requires the PILOT=\n agreement to be \"in a form satisfactory to the Township Solicitor,\" and I am=\n not in a position to certify satisfaction until both of these issues =E2=80=\n=94 the MFN clause and the escalator start date =E2=80=94 are resolved in a m=\nanner acceptable to the Board.\n\n**Timeline and Next Steps**\n\nI want to be mindful of the project timeline. My understanding is that Pinnac=\nle is targeting an April 1, 2026 construction start date, and given that buil=\nding permits cannot be issued without an executed PILOT agreement under Condi=\ntion 30, we should aim to finalize the agreement well in advance to avoid any=\n permitting delays. I would suggest that we schedule a call or, if preferable=\n, an in-person meeting within the next two weeks to discuss these two outstan=\nding issues and see if we can find a path forward. I am available the week of=\n January 27, 2025 =E2=80=94 please let me know what works on your end. Chairm=\nan Zimmerman has indicated his willingness to participate in a meeting if it =\nwould be helpful to resolve these issues at the supervisors' level, and I exp=\nect Derek may want to be involved as well.\n\nUpon resolution of these two open items, I believe the balance of the agreeme=\nnt is in good shape and we should be able to finalize and circulate for execu=\ntion within 30 days.\n\nI want to close by reiterating that the Township remains fully supportive of =\nthe Brightfield Solar Project and recognizes the significant economic benefit=\ns it will bring to Conestoga Township =E2=80=94 including the annual PILOT an=\nd tax revenues, construction-phase employment, and the agricultural mitigatio=\nn payment to the Lancaster Farmland Trust. These are meaningful contributions=\n, and the Board appreciates Pinnacle's commitment to the community. I am hope=\nful we can resolve these remaining issues collaboratively and get the agreeme=\nnt signed.\n\nPlease let me know your thoughts and your availability for a meeting. I look =\nforward to hearing from you.\n\nBest regards,\n\nAllen Driscoll, Esq.\nDriscoll & Frey LLP\n45 East King Street, Suite 200\nLancaster, PA 17602\nPhone: (717) 555-0184\nEmail: adriscoll@driscoll-frey.com\n\n*Counsel to Conestoga Township*\n"}
