s.27-605(B)(14)(j)(4)\\\",\\\"Cond 29\\\",\\\"Ongoing\\\",\\n    \\\"Assessment records; school district receipts.\\\",\\\"Satisfied (record)\\\",\\\"Mechanism depends on the exemption route actually used (I-24)\\\")\\nrow(C,\\\"D-08\\\",\\\"All financial assurances (decommissioning security, road maintenance bond, agricultural mitigation payment) in place no later than 60 days before commencement of construction.\\\",\\n    \\\"Conflicts with (i)(5) and (f)(6)\\\",\\\"Cond 34\\\",\\\"60 days before construction (approx. 31 Jan 2026 on the stated 1 Apr 2026 start)\\\",\\n    \\\"Written evidence to the Solicitor and the Engineer.\\\",\\\"Conflict\\\",\\\"NEW: would allow assurance after the first building permit (I-18b)\\\")\\n\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------ E\\nC = \\\"E. Operations, Monitoring and Reporting\\\"\\nrow(C,\\\"E-01\\\",\\\"Maintain all project components in good order; repair or replace any failed or hazardous component within 90 days unless the Engineer approves longer.\\\",\\n    \\\"No express Ordinance analogue\\\",\\\"Cond 31\\\",\\\"Ongoing\\\",\\n    \\\"Maintenance log; annual report; inspection.\\\",\\\"Open (no evidence)\\\",\\\"--\\\")\\nrow(C,\\\"E-02\\\",\\\"Annual compliance report on or before 31 March each year covering operations, generation, maintenance, the status of financial assurances and condition compliance; first report on 31 March of the first full calendar year after COD.\\\",\\n    \\\"s.27-605(B)(14)(l)(1) (60 days after each COD anniversary)\\\",\\\"Cond 32\\\",\\\"Annually, by 31 March\\\",\\n    \\\"Submitted report; Board acceptance.\\\",\\\"Conflict\\\",\\\"NEW: deadline and content differ from the Ordinance; first report date is ambiguous (I-19, RC-13)\\\")\\nrow(C,\\\"E-03\\\",\\\"Annual site inspection by the Engineer, Zoning Officer or Board designee on 10 business days' notice, at the applicant's cost.\\\",\\n    \\\"s.27-605(B)(14)(l)(2)\\\",\\\"-- no condition --\\\",\\\"Annually\\\",\\n    \\\"Inspection reports; cost invoices.\\\",\\\"Gap\\\",\\\"Not carried into the Decision (G-19)\\\")\\nrow(C,\\\"E-04\\\",\\\"Enforcement: written notice of violation, opportunity to cure not exceeding 30 days, then revocation proceedings before the Board.\\\",\\n    \\\"s.27-605(B)(14)(l)(3)\\\",\\\"-- no condition --\\\",\\\"On non-compliance\\\",\\n    \\\"Violation notices; cure records; hearing record.\\\",\\\"Gap\\\",\\\"The Decision recites no enforcement mechanism (G-20)\\\")\\nrow(C,\\\"E-05\\\",\\\"Noise compliance measurement within 60 days of a written Township request, at the applicant's expense.\\\",\\n    \\\"s.27-605(B)(14)(g)(1)\\\",\\\"Cond 18\\\",\\\"On Township request\\\",\\n    \\\"Acoustical measurement report.\\\",\\\"Open (no evidence)\\\",\\\"Measurement standard is disputed (I-07)\\\")\\nrow(C,\\\"E-06\\\",\\\"Reporting of any material change to the facility, its ownership or its operations during the reporting period.\\\",\\n    \\\"s.27-605(B)(14)(l)(1)(v)\\\",\\\"-- no condition --\\\",\\\"Annually\\\",\\n    \\\"Annual report disclosure.\\\",\\\"Gap\\\",\\\"Omnibus content item not carried into Condition 32 (G-21)\\\")\\nrow(C,\\\"E-07\\\",\\\"Emergency exercise with the fire department and emergency management within 6 months of COD and annually thereafter.\\\",\\n    \\\"No Ordinance analogue\\\",\\\"Cond 33\\\",\\\"6 months after COD; annually\\\",\\n    \\\"Exercise records and after-action reports.\\\",\\\"Open (no evidence)\\\",\\\"--\\\")\\n\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------ F\\nC = \\\"F. Administrative, Procedural and Record Integrity\\\"\\nrow(C,\\\"F-01\\\",\\\"A single, consistent docket number must identify the application throughout the record, the decision and any appeal.\\\",\\n    \\\"MPC record requirements\\\",\\\"s.I; s.V; Certificate of Service\\\",\\\"Record integrity\\\",\\n    \\\"Certified record index.\\\",\\\"Conflict\\\",\\\"NEW: CU-2024-006 (Decision) vs CU-2024-012 (Planning Commission letter) (I-21)\\\")\\nrow(C,\\\"F-02\\\",\\\"Thirty-day appeal period running from entry of the Decision; approval final if no appeal is taken.\\\",\\n    \\\"MPC s.1002-A, 53 P.S. s.11002-A (and see s.914.1)\\\",\\\"s.I para 10; Notice of Appeal Rights\\\",\\\"Expired January 2025 - no appeal filed per Engineer and Solicitor\\\",\\n    \\\"Docket entry; prothonotary confirmation.\\\",\\\"Satisfied (record)\\\",\\\"Jan 5, 2025 fell on a Sunday - see I-33; appeal-window risk now spent\\\")\\nrow(C,\\\"F-03\\\",\\\"Conditional use approval expires if a building permit is not obtained within 24 months of the Decision (6 December 2026), extendable once by up to 12 months on written request filed before expiry and showing good cause.\\\",\\n    \\\"s.27-605(B)(14)(k)(5)\\\",\\\"-- not recited in the Decision --\\\",\\\"6 December 2026; outer limit 6 December 2027\\\",\\n    \\\"Building permit date; any extension request and Board action.\\\",\\\"Gap\\\",\\\"KEY SCHEDULE RISK: the approval instruments are silent on expiry (G-23, RC-12)\\\")\\nrow(C,\\\"F-04\\\",\\\"Approval is personal to the applicant; transfer or assignment only with prior Board consent, and the transferee must demonstrate financial, technical and organisational capacity.\\\",\\n    \\\"s.27-605(B)(14)(k)(4)\\\",\\\"-- no condition --\\\",\\\"On any transfer\\\",\\n    \\\"Board resolution; capacity showing.\\\",\\\"Gap\\\",\\\"Not carried into the Decision (G-22)\\\")\\nrow(C,\\\"F-05\\\",\\\"Modification, expansion or reconfiguration beyond the approved plan requires an amendment (with notice and hearing) or a new application; only non-material changes may be handled administratively.\\\",\\n    \\\"s.27-605(B)(14)(k)(3)\\\",\\\"Cond 35 (Engineer-approved 'minor field adjustments')\\\",\\\"On any change\\\",\\n    \\\"Board amendment; Engineer certification.\\\",\\\"Conflict\\\",\\\"NEW: Condition 35 delegates approvals the Ordinance reserves to the Board (I-28)\\\")\\nPYEOF\\necho done\"}", "result": "done\n"}
{"turn": 11, "role": "assistant", "text": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_Slhp7rnDJJx7geS1J2FI7373", "name": "bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cat > $WORKSPACE_DIR/build/content_findings.py <<'PYEOF'\\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\\n\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# CROSS-REFERENCE INCONSISTENCY REGISTER\\n# severity: Critical / High / Medium / Low\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\nINCONS = []\\ndef inc(iid, topic, docA, textA, docB, textB, kind, sev, analysis):\\n    INCONS.append(dict(id=iid, topic=topic, docA=docA, textA=textA, docB=docB, textB=textB,\\n                       kind=kind, sev=sev, analysis=analysis))\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-01\\\",\\\"Lot coverage: 580 acres of panels inside a 720-acre fence is 80.6% coverage against a 75% ceiling.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.II and Finding 7\\\",\\n    \\\"Panel coverage approx. 580 acres; fenced area approx. 720 acres. Finding 7 holds the site plan satisfies 'maximum impervious surface coverage requirements'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, Table 27-605-1\\\",\\n    \\\"'Maximum Lot Coverage (impervious surfaces within fenced area) - 75% of fenced area'. s.(f)(1) refers to 'impervious and semi-impervious surfaces (such as solar panels and access roads)'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Numeric / non-compliance\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\n    \\\"580 / 720 = 80.6%, i.e. 40 acres above the 540-acre ceiling (RC-10). The Ordinance's own stormwater provision treats panels as semi-impervious, which makes the 75% ceiling applicable to tracker area on its face. Finding 7 asserts compliance without any impervious-area computation in the record, and neither the Engineer's September 2024 letters nor the Planning Commission letter quantifies impervious coverage. Either the Ordinance standard is not met, or the Township has adopted an unstated interpretation (panels not counted) that should be recorded. Obtain the impervious/semi-impervious area schedule and a written Engineer determination; if panels count, the layout requires redesign or a s.(k)(3) amendment.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-02\\\",\\\"Agricultural mitigation is stated as 620 acres but is priced on 623 acres.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 28\\\",\\n    \\\"'...in the amount of Seven Hundred Forty-Seven Thousand Six Hundred Dollars ($747,600), calculated as 620 acres of prime agricultural land at $1,200 per acre.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Finding 14; applicant letter s.IV.D; Planning Commission letter s.II\\\",\\n    \\\"Finding 14, the applicant letter and the county letter all state 623 acres of prime agricultural land; 623 x $1,200 = $747,600.\\\",\\n    \\\"Numeric\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"620 x $1,200 = $744,000, not $747,600 (RC-04). The dollar figure is right and the acreage recital is wrong, but the error matters twice over: (i) the operative payment condition recites a figure that does not compute, inviting a dispute over whether the obligation is 620 acres or $747,600; and (ii) s.(f)(6) requires mitigation for prime farmland AND farmland of statewide importance, so the true mitigaable acreage is likely higher than 623 - the Decision's own Finding 14 places the balance of the 850 acres in 'farmland of statewide importance, woodland, or other non-prime categories'. At $1,200/acre, every 10 acres of omitted statewide-importance land is $12,000; if all 227 residual acres qualified the shortfall would be approx. $272,400 (RC-05). Correct the acreage recital and obtain Conservation District confirmation of acreage as required by s.(f)(6).\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-03\\\",\\\"Operational road maintenance bond: $150,000 or $100,000?\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 25\\\",\\n    \\\"'...the road maintenance bond shall be reduced to One Hundred Fifty Thousand Dollars ($150,000) for the operational period...'\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter, s.IV.F and Summary item 7\\\",\\n    \\\"'...road maintenance bond of $500,000 during the construction period, to be reduced to $100,000 during the operational period.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Financial term\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"The applicant's written acceptance of conditions assumed a $100,000 operational bond; the Decision imposed $150,000. The applicant has not accepted the higher figure, and Condition 25 will be contested at permit stage unless the applicant confirms acceptance in writing. Note also the Township Engineer's 10 January 2025 email refers only to the $500,000 construction bond and is silent on the reduction, so the point is unresolved on the current file.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-04\\\",\\\"Parcel acreages differ parcel-by-parcel between the applicant's filing letter and the Decision / county letter, although both total 850 acres.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.II and Finding 6; Planning Commission letter, s.I\\\",\\n    \\\"185.3 / 142.7 / 210.4 / 98.6 / 75.2 / 88.9 / 48.9 acres (Parcels 120-45-001 to -007); total 850.0 acres.\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter, Project Summary table\\\",\\n    \\\"145 / 110 / 130 / 95 / 120 / 140 / 110 acres; total 850 acres.\\\",\\n    \\\"Numeric\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"The seven individual variances (+40.3, +32.7, +80.4, +3.6, -44.8, -51.1, -61.1) sum to exactly zero, which suggests one schedule was stretched to preserve the 850-acre headline (RC-02, RC-03). This is not a drafting nicety: parcel acreage drives the lease verification in Condition 3, the agricultural mitigation calculation, the s.(k)(5) scope of approval, and any future amendment. Parcel 120-45-005 varies by 44.8 acres (37%) and Parcel 120-45-007 by 61.1 acres (56%). Obtain the recorded deed acreages and reconcile with the site plan and the leases. Until reconciled, no parcel-level figure in the file can be relied upon.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-05\\\",\\\"Lease terms for Parcels 120-45-003 and -004: two or three five-year extensions?\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.II and Finding 6\\\",\\n    \\\"'...30 years + three 5-year extensions' for Parcels -003 and -004.\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter, Project Summary table\\\",\\n    \\\"'...30 years + two 5-year renewals' for the same parcels.\\\",\\n    \\\"Term\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"The discrepancy is 5 years of site control per parcel, and it matters because Condition 3 requires the applicant to demonstrate 'the legal right to develop, construct and operate... for the full lease term, including any extension periods'. It also matters to the decommissioning horizon: a 45-year maximum term (30 + 3x5) rather than 40 years. Verify against the executed leases, not the summaries.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-06\\\",\\\"Substation / inverter setback from occupied dwellings: 500 ft in the Ordinance, 250 ft in the applicant's summary, and absent from the Decision.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, Table 27-605-1\\\",\\n    \\\"'Setback from Occupied Dwelling on Non-Participating Property - Substations, Inverters, Power Conversion Stations - 500 feet.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter, Summary item 1; Decision and Order, Condition 6\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant: '250 feet from occupied dwellings on non-participating parcels for substations and inverter stations.' Condition 6 imposes only a 250 ft property-line setback and is silent on dwellings.\\\",\\n    \\\"Omission / numeric\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\n    \\\"This is the most consequential omission in the condition set. The Township Engineer, the Planning Commission and the Board all appear to have proceeded on the premise that the 250 ft figure addressed substations generally; the Planning Commission letter even states that 'all solar components are located a minimum of 500 feet from any occupied dwelling', which is a different and broader assertion than the condition set delivers. Because Condition 6 contains no dwelling setback at all, a substation or conversion station could be sited 260 ft from a residence without breaching any condition of approval, while breaching the Ordinance. Either the site plan happens to comply (in which case say so in a condition and on the plan) or the design is built to the 250 ft figure in the applicant's letter (in which case there is an Ordinance non-compliance embedded in the approval). Resolve before any permit issues.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-07\\\",\\\"Noise: two different ANSI measurement standards, two different study authors, and no dwelling receptors.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 18\\\",\\n    \\\"'...shall not exceed forty-five (45) dBA at the nearest non-participating property line, as measured in accordance with ANSI S12.9-2013, Part 3.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Finding 20; applicant letter s.IV.B and Summary item 3\\\",\\n    \\\"Finding 20 and the applicant both describe the study as using 'ANSI/ASA S12.9-2013/Part 2'; the applicant expressly accepted a condition 'measured in accordance with the ANSI/ASA S12.9-2013/Part 2 standard referenced in the noise study'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Standard / inconsistency\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"Part 2 and Part 3 are materially different methods (long-term, wide-area measurement versus short-term measurement with an observer present), and the choice determines how compliance is proved years from now. The applicant accepted Part 2; the Decision imposed Part 3. If the Township later measures under Part 3, the applicant is well placed to say it never agreed to that test. Separately, Finding 20 attributes the study to 'Harris Acoustics Group', who appears nowhere in the procedural history (s.I paras 5-6 list only Fieldstone and Clarendon as Township consultants and Ridgepoint as the applicant's consultant) and nowhere in the applicant's enclosure list, which attributes the same study to Ridgepoint / Priya Mukherjee. Fix the standard by amendment and correct or explain the authorship. Also note s.(b)(7) required modelling at the nearest occupied dwellings, which neither the Finding nor the condition addresses.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-08\\\",\\\"Screening buffer: the Ordinance requires it on every qualifying boundary; the Decision requires it only on the north and east.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, s.27-605(B)(14)(d)(1)\\\",\\n    \\\"'...along any project site boundary that is adjacent to: (i) a non-participating property containing an occupied dwelling located within one thousand (1,000) feet of the project site boundary; or (ii) a public road right-of-way.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 16 (and Planning Commission letter s.III.B)\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 16 requires the buffer 'along the northern and eastern project boundaries, at locations adjacent to non-participating properties with occupied residences'. The county recommended buffers 'along all project boundaries adjacent to non-participating properties with occupied residences' and 'along all required frontages... and public roadways'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Scope\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 16 is narrower than the Ordinance in two independent respects: it is limited to two compass points, and it omits road frontages entirely. A condition cannot waive an Ordinance standard, so the Ordinance standard continues to apply and the Decision should say so. Because Conclusion 2 of the Decision asserts compliance with the Ordinance's 'screening' standard, and Condition 16 does not deliver that standard, the conclusion is unsupported on the face of the instrument. This is also the ground the dissenting Supervisors pressed (s.VII, third ground), so it is the most likely focus of any collateral challenge or neighbour complaint.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-09\\\",\\\"Site access: the Ordinance requires two 20-ft access points; the Decision describes one primary access and 16-ft internal roads.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, s.27-605(B)(14)(h)(3)\\\",\\n    \\\"'Access to the project site shall be provided via a minimum of two (2) access points, each with a minimum driveway width of twenty (20) feet... Access points shall be designed to provide adequate sight distance in accordance with PennDOT standards and shall be approved by the Township Engineer.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 7\\\",\\n    \\\"'The minimum width of internal access roads shall be sixteen (16) feet. The primary construction access point shall be located on Hershey Mill Road...'\\\",\\n    \\\"Scope / numeric\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"No second access point appears anywhere in the condition set, which is both an Ordinance compliance gap and a life-safety issue for fire apparatus access to a 720-acre energised site. The 16 ft / 20 ft difference may be explainable (internal road versus driveway) but the distinction is nowhere drawn. The Township Engineer's 10 January 2025 email compounds the ambiguity: he asks whether access is from a state road or a Township road and whether a deceleration lane is required, which shows the access design is not yet settled. Require a conforming access plan with the second access point, sight-distance analysis and Engineer approval before any permit.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-10\\\",\\\"The traffic management plan obligation is imposed twice, in two conditions with different content lists.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 7\\\",\\n    \\\"'A Traffic Management Plan shall be prepared... not less than sixty (60) days prior to commencement of construction. The Traffic Management Plan shall address haul routes, vehicle staging areas, traffic control measures, and any temporary road closures or detours.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 27\\\",\\n    \\\"'A construction traffic management plan shall be prepared... at least sixty (60) days prior... shall specify designated haul routes, traffic control measures, flagging procedures, hours of construction vehicle operations, load limits, and any temporary road closures or detours.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Duplication\\\",\\\"Low\\\",\\n    \\\"Same deliverable, same deadline, two slightly different content lists. The practical risk is a submission that satisfies one condition and is said to fail the other, or an argument that the second condition is surplusage. Consolidate into a single condition listing the union of the required contents.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-11\\\",\\\"The Planning Commission advised that the BESS setback of 250 ft 'is consistent with the ordinance'. The Ordinance requires 300 ft.\\\",\\n    \\\"Planning Commission letter, s.III.A\\\",\\n    \\\"'With respect to the battery energy storage system, the proposed battery energy storage system setback of 250 feet from non-participating property lines is consistent with the ordinance.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, Table 27-605-1 Note 1 and s.27-605(B)(15)(c)(3)\\\",\\n    \\\"300 ft minimum from any non-participating property line for energy storage facilities.\\\",\\n    \\\"Error in advisory source\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"The Decision itself correctly imposes 300 ft (Condition 12(a)), so the operative condition is right. But the error is worth recording for three reasons: it shows the county's advisory review did not accurately check the dimensional table; it invites the applicant to argue 250 ft as the 'approved' figure; and if the site plan was drawn to 250 ft on the strength of this advice, the BESS compound is 50 ft inside the required setback. Confirm the BESS dimension on the plan. The same letter also asserts that 'all solar components' are 500 ft from dwellings, which overstates what the site plan is shown to demonstrate.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-12\\\",\\\"Decommissioning timing: 12 months in the Ordinance, 18 months in the Decision, 12 months in the applicant's letter - and the cultural-resource condition invokes a federal process that does not apply.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 21\\\",\\n    \\\"'...restoration of the Project site to a condition suitable for agricultural use, within eighteen (18) months of the cessation of operations.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance s.27-605(B)(14)(i)(7); applicant letter s.VI.B\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance: 'Decommissioning shall be completed within twelve (12) months of the date on which the facility ceases generating...'. Applicant: 'restored to a condition suitable for agricultural use within 12 months of facility deactivation'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Timing\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 21 is 50% longer than the Ordinance standard and 50% longer than the applicant's own commitment, so it is both an Ordinance non-conformity and a gratuitous concession the applicant never asked for. Read with Condition 24 (12 months' non-generation, then a 90-day contest window) and s.(i)(7)'s 24-month outer cap, the three mechanisms together produce an end-state that is internally inconsistent and hard to enforce. Restate s.(i)(7) verbatim. Separately, Condition 9 requires 'written confirmation... as determined through the Section 106 consultation process or equivalent state-level review'; no federal action, funding or licence is identified anywhere in the file, so s.106 is not triggered and the PHMC review should be routed under the Pennsylvania History Code, 37 Pa. C.S. ss.101 et seq., as s.(f)(4) contemplates.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-13\\\",\\\"The solar glare analysis was an application-stage deliverable but was deferred to the post-approval, pre-permit stage.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, s.27-605(B)(14)(b)(8) and the closing sentence of s.(b)\\\",\\n    \\\"The glare analysis is listed among the application materials, and 'An application shall not be deemed complete for purposes of scheduling a public hearing until all items listed above have been submitted to the Zoning Officer.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 19; applicant letter; Planning Commission letter s.IV\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 19 defers the analysis to 'prior to issuance of building permits'. The applicant writes in the future tense ('The Applicant will prepare and submit a solar glare analysis'), and the county 'recommends that a solar glare analysis be completed'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Procedural / completeness\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"Three of the six documents in the file, read together, establish that a mandatory application item was not before the Board when it voted. Finding 1 nevertheless states the application was complete and Conclusion 8 states the decision is supported by substantial evidence. The appeal period has run, so the practical exposure is now (i) a validity challenge premised on incomplete record, (ii) a permit-stage impasse if the glare study shows an impact on a roadway or residence within one mile, and (iii) an enforcement problem, because no condition tells the applicant what happens if the study is adverse. The Ordinance's answer (s.(g)(2)) is that identified glare impacts must be mitigated to the Board's satisfaction - so the condition should require Board approval of any needed mitigation, not merely submission of a report.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-14\\\",\\\"Setbacks are measured from different reference points.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 5\\\",\\n    \\\"'All solar panels shall maintain the following minimum setbacks, measured from the nearest edge of the panel at maximum tilt...'\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, Table 27-605-1, Note 2\\\",\\n    \\\"'All setbacks... shall be measured from the nearest point of the applicable component (including any foundation, concrete pad, or supporting structure) to the nearest point of the applicable boundary line...'\\\",\\n    \\\"Measurement basis\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"Measuring from the panel edge at maximum tilt gives a larger setback than measuring from the foundation in most configurations (the panel overhangs its post), so the practical effect is likely conservative - but it is still a departure from the codified measurement basis and creates an argument in any future compliance measurement about which datum governs. Note 3 (the more restrictive of overlapping standards applies) is also not restated anywhere in the Decision.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-15\\\",\\\"The property value assessment does not address the 0-500 ft band that the Ordinance puts in scope.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, s.27-605(B)(14)(b)(12) and (j)(5)\\\",\\n    \\\"The assessment is to 'evaluate the potential impact of the proposed facility on the market value of residential properties within one thousand (1,000) feet of the project site boundary.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Finding 19; applicant letter s.IV.C\\\",\\n    \\\"Finding 19 concludes 'no material negative impact' without stating the geographic scope of the analysis. The applicant describes the finding as 'no statistically significant negative impact on property values for residences located beyond 500 feet of a solar project boundary'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Scope\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"If the study's actual support stops at 500 ft, the Ordinance's 1,000-ft requirement is only half answered, and the unaddressed 500 ft band is precisely the band in which the 500 ft panel setback places the nearest neighbours. The dissenting Supervisors attacked exactly this: the report 'relies heavily on comparable data from other regions' (s.VII, second ground). Obtain the report and confirm (a) the study area, (b) the number of local paired sales, and (c) whether the conclusion is supported for the 0-500 ft band. Finding 19's unqualified phrasing is not supportable on the applicant's own characterisation.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-16\\\",\\\"The Township Solicitor states the Decision does not fix the escalator start year and proposes Year 11. The Decision fixes it at Year 16.\\\",\\n    \\\"Township Solicitor email, 15 January 2025\\\",\\n    \\\"'I will note that the Decision and Order references \\\\\\\"negotiated PILOT terms\\\\\\\" but does not specify the escalator commencement year, so this is a point for the agreement itself to resolve.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Finding 17 and Condition 30\\\",\\n    \\\"Both state '$385,000 per year for Years 1 through 15 of commercial operation, with a two percent (2%) annual escalator commencing thereafter for the remaining term of the agreement.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Term / record accuracy\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\n    \\\"The Decision does specify the escalator: flat for Years 1-15, escalating from Year 16. The Solicitor's Year 11 proposal therefore contradicts the instrument the Township adopted nine weeks earlier, and Condition 30 expressly ties permit issuance to a PILOT 'reflecting the negotiated terms' of $385,000 for Years 1-15. Executing a Year 11 escalator would depart from the Decision; refusing to do so leaves the Township bound to Year 16 unless it amends. The cumulative difference is approx. $118,627 over Years 1-15 and approx. $3.1 million more over a 30-year term (RC-15). The correct route is an amendment of Condition 30 (see I-28 / F-05 on modification procedure), not a side agreement. Separately, the Solicitor's arithmetic is slightly off: $385,000 x 1.02^5 = $425,071, not $425,048 (RC-16).\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-17\\\",\\\"Updated decommissioning estimates: 60 days or 90 days to top up the security, and no submission deadline in the Decision.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 23\\\",\\n    \\\"'...shall increase the decommissioning security to one hundred twenty-five percent (125%) of the updated net cost within ninety (90) days of receipt of the updated estimate by the Township.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, s.27-605(B)(14)(i)(6)\\\",\\n    \\\"Updated estimate 'shall be submitted to the Township within thirty (30) days of completion', and any increase 'within sixty (60) days of the date of the updated estimate'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Timing\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"The Decision doubles the Ordinance's cure period and drops the 30-day submission deadline altogether, which is what actually creates the security lag: without a submission deadline the Township may not 'receive' an estimate until long after it was prepared. Restate the Ordinance language. The same pattern appears in Condition 22, which omits the Ordinance's A.M. Best 'A- VII or better' rating floor for surety bonds and the requirement that the Township be named beneficiary of an escrow (G-26).\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-18\\\",\\\"Financial assurances: two conditions set two different deadlines, one of which permits posting after the first building permit.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 34\\\",\\n    \\\"Decommissioning security, road maintenance bond and agricultural mitigation payment 'shall be in place no later than sixty (60) days prior to the commencement of construction activities.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Conditions 22 and 28; Ordinance s.(i)(5) and (f)(6)\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 22: security 'shall be posted prior to issuance of the first building permit'. Condition 28: payment 'prior to issuance of the first building permit'. Ordinance (i)(5): 'No building permit shall be issued until the Township Solicitor has confirmed that the decommissioning security instrument is in proper form...'\\\",\\n    \\\"Deadline conflict\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"Because a building permit must precede construction, '60 days before construction' can fall after 'the first building permit'. Condition 34 therefore either restates the permit-stage conditions as a later, easier deadline, or is simply a second deadline - and on its face an applicant could tender the $7.875 million instrument, the $500,000 bond and the $747,600 payment after permits issued. The Ordinance cannot be waived by condition, so the Ordinance deadline controls; but the Decision should say so. Also note the Ordinance requires the agricultural payment before a building OR grading permit, which is earlier still, and that the parent guaranty under Condition 2 is omitted from the Condition 34 schedule.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-19\\\",\\\"Annual reporting: the Decision's deadline is later than the Ordinance's and the content list is narrower.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 32\\\",\\n    \\\"'...on or before March 31 of each year... The first annual report shall be due on March 31 of the first full calendar year following the date of commercial operation.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, s.27-605(B)(14)(l)(1)\\\",\\n    \\\"Report due 'within sixty (60) days of each anniversary of the commercial operation date', and must include total energy production in MWh and 'any material changes to the facility, its ownership, or its operations'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Timing / content\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"On the projected 31 December 2027 COD, the Ordinance would require the first report by about 1 March 2029 (60 days after the first anniversary on 31 December 2028), whereas Condition 32's 'March 31 of the first full calendar year following' COD is ambiguous between 31 March 2028 (before a full year of operation had elapsed) and 31 March 2029. Either reading conflicts with the Ordinance (RC-13). Generation in MWh and ownership changes are both dropped from the condition. Restate the Ordinance standard and add the two omitted content items.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-20\\\",\\\"Two different firms are credited with the noise study.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Finding 20\\\",\\n    \\\"'The Applicant submitted a noise impact study prepared by Harris Acoustics Group, a qualified acoustical engineering firm.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter, s.IV.B and enclosure 2\\\",\\n    \\\"'...noise impact assessment prepared by Ridgepoint Environmental Consultants LLC, under the direction of Lead Consultant Priya Mukherjee.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Attribution\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"Harris Acoustics Group is not identified as a retained consultant in the Decision's procedural history (s.I paras 5-6), does not appear in the applicant's enclosure list, and is not mentioned in either email. If a separate acoustical firm did the work, the record should show its retainer, its qualifications and whether it testified. If the Finding misattributes the work, it is an error in a finding of fact supporting Conclusion 4 (which credits 'the Harris Acoustics Group noise study'). Either way the Finding needs correction; the applicant should produce the report cover and signature page.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-21\\\",\\\"Two different docket numbers for the same application.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order (cover, caption, s.V and Certificate of Service)\\\",\\n    \\\"'Application No.: CU-2024-006'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Planning Commission letter (re-line and s.I)\\\",\\n    \\\"'Conditional Use Application No. CU-2024-012'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Docket\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"The county's s.609.1 referral, and therefore the advisory recommendation that Finding 4 relies on, is docketed under a different number from the decision. Possibilities: a transcription error in one document, or the referral was made under a separate county file number. This matters to record integrity, to the Certificate of Service (which refers only to CU-2024-006), and to any party trying to locate the county's file. Confirm the county's file number and correct whichever document is wrong; if they are genuinely two different applications, the referral needs explanation.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-22\\\",\\\"Construction job range.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Finding 16\\\",\\n    \\\"'approximately 350-400 construction jobs during the peak construction period'\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter, closing\\\",\\n    \\\"'will support approximately 300 to 400 jobs during the peak construction period'\\\",\\n    \\\"Numeric\\\",\\\"Low\\\",\\n    \\\"Immaterial to compliance, but the Decision adopted a floor (350) higher than the applicant's own evidence (300). Since no job-count condition attaches, no consequence follows; worth correcting for record accuracy. The same letter also claims the project will generate power 'sufficient to power approximately 25,000 Pennsylvania homes', a figure appearing nowhere in the findings of fact.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-23\\\",\\\"Three different addresses for the Township and its offices.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance excerpt (cover note)\\\",\\n    \\\"'...the Conestoga Township offices, 1220 Millwood Road, Conestoga, Pennsylvania 17516'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter (address block); Planning Commission letter (address block)\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant: '129 East Main Street, Conestoga, PA 17516'. County: 'Conestoga Township Municipal Building, 1217 River Road, Conestoga, PA 17516'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Clerical\\\",\\\"Low\\\",\\n    \\\"No compliance consequence, but three inconsistent municipal addresses across the six documents is a reliable indicator that the applicant's correspondence templates and the county's address records were never reconciled. Useful chiefly as a prompt to verify the addresses used for the mailed notice under Finding 5 and for the Certificate of Service.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-24\\\",\\\"'$427,000 per year in tax revenue to the Township' is not all Township revenue, and the school district is variously included and excluded.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Finding 18\\\",\\n    \\\"'Total estimated annual tax revenue to the Township from the Project is approximately $427,000, consisting of $385,000 per year under the negotiated PILOT terms and approximately $42,000 per year in real estate taxes assessed on non-exempt improvements...'\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter, closing; Township Solicitor email, 15 January 2025\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant: 'will generate an estimated $427,000 in annual tax revenue to the Township and the Conestoga Valley School District'. Solicitor: 'the total estimated annual revenue to the Township from the project is approximately $427,000'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Characterisation\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"Real estate taxes on non-exempt improvements are levied by the county, the township and the school district; only the Township's millage share accrues to the Township. Describing the whole $42,000 as Township revenue overstates Township income, and describing the $427,000 as revenue to the Township 'and' the school district double-counts the school district's share. There is a further structural question the file never answers: the PILOT is 'payable in lieu of real property taxes on the solar facility and BESS components' (per the Solicitor's 15 January 2025 summary), yet s.(j)(1) only authorises a PILOT where the applicant 'intends to seek any tax exemption, abatement, alternative energy credit, or alternative assessment' - and nothing in the record shows that the applicant applied for any such relief. If no exemption was sought, the facility is fully taxable and the Township may have traded an unpriced ad valorem liability for a fixed $385,000; if an exemption was sought, Condition 29's promise that school district taxes remain payable has to be squared with the scope of that exemption, which the Township cannot control (s.(j)(4)). Obtain the applicant's assessment/exemption position in writing.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-25\\\",\\\"Two different statutory bases for the 30-day appeal period.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.I para 10 and Notice of Appeal Rights\\\",\\n    \\\"'the thirty (30) day appeal period under the MPC, 53 P.S. s.11002-A' / 'Pursuant to Section 1002-A of the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code, 53 P.S. s.11002-A'\\\",\\n    \\\"Township Solicitor email, 15 January 2025\\\",\\n    \\\"'The 30-day appeal period under Section 914.1 of the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code expired on January 5, 2025'\\\",\\n    \\\"Citation\\\",\\\"Low\\\",\\n    \\\"Both provisions produce a 30-day appeal window, so nothing turns on it here, but the record should cite the operative provision consistently - and the correct one for a governing-body conditional use decision should be confirmed. Note also that Finding 5 and s.I para 4 variously cite s.609 and the county cites s.609.1 for the referral obligation (I-26).\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-26\\\",\\\"The county planning referral is cited under two different MPC sections.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.I para 4\\\",\\n    \\\"Refers to review by the Lancaster County Planning Commission 'in accordance with the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code', and the Ordinance excerpt cites 'Section 609 of the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code, 53 P.S. s.10609' as the referral basis.\\\",\\n    \\\"Planning Commission letter, opening paragraph\\\",\\n    \\\"'pursuant to Section 609.1 of the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code (Act 247, as amended)'\\\",\\n    \\\"Citation\\\",\\\"Low\\\",\\n    \\\"Section 609 and Section 609.1 (53 P.S. ss.10609 and 10609.1) are different provisions and only one governs referral of a conditional use application. Confirm and conform the citation across the decision, the ordinance excerpt and the county letter.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-27\\\",\\\"Where the screening buffers sit relative to the fence line.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.II and Condition 15\\\",\\n    \\\"Approx. 850 acres total, of which 'the total fenced project area will be approximately 720 acres, with the remaining acreage devoted to setbacks, vegetative buffers, access roads, and stormwater management facilities'; Condition 15 fences 'the entire fenced project area, encompassing approximately 720 acres'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter, Project Summary\\\",\\n    \\\"'The remaining acreage within the fence line will accommodate access roads, stormwater management facilities, the BESS compound, power conversion stations, and vegetative buffers.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Scope\\\",\\\"Low\\\",\\n    \\\"On the Decision's figures the buffers sit outside the 720-acre fence, in the residual 130 acres - which is consistent with s.(e)(4)'s 10 ft fence-to-buffer separation. On the applicant's description they are inside the fence. The landscape and fence plans need to settle this, because s.(d)(2) requires the buffer to be within the project site boundary and s.(e)(4) requires the fence to be 10 ft clear of the buffer's interior edge.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-28\\\",\\\"Modification authority: the Decision lets the Township Engineer approve field changes that the Ordinance reserves to the Board.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 35\\\",\\n    \\\"'...modifications that do not constitute material changes may, at the discretion of the Board of Supervisors, be approved administratively upon written certification by the Township Engineer... Minor field adjustments that do not alter setbacks, height, total project acreage, or the number of project parcels may be approved in writing by the Township Engineer without further action by the Board.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, s.27-605(B)(14)(k)(3)\\\",\\n    \\\"'Modification, expansion, or reconfiguration of the approved project beyond the scope of the approved site plan shall require either: (i) an amendment to the conditional use approval... following the same substantive and procedural requirements as an original conditional use application... including public notice and hearing; or (ii) a new conditional use application.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Delegation\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"The second sentence of Condition 35 is defensible only if the enumerated adjustments are genuinely within 'the scope of the approved site plan'. Given that setbacks, height, acreage and parcel count are the very standards the Ordinance fixes, a field adjustment that touches them is by definition outside the approved scope. Tighten the condition to make clear that no Engineer approval may alter a standard in Table 27-605-1 or the approved plan's dimensions. Note this also bears on how the escalator dispute (I-16) must be resolved: by amendment, not by side letter.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-29\\\",\\\"Fence specification: the Ordinance's height ceiling and prohibited materials are not carried into the condition.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, Table 27-605-1 and s.27-605(B)(14)(e)(2)\\\",\\n    \\\"Fencing maximum height 8 ft; fencing 'topped with angled extension arms or similar deterrent devices'; 'The use of barbed wire, razor wire, or electrified fencing is expressly prohibited.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 15\\\",\\n    \\\"'The Applicant shall install perimeter security fencing of at least seven (7) feet in height around the entire fenced project area...'\\\",\\n    \\\"Specification\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"The Decision sets a floor with no ceiling and drops the design and material restrictions. An 8 ft fence with barbed wire would satisfy Condition 15 and breach the Ordinance. Since fence design and materials must go to the Township Engineer for approval under Condition 15 in any event, add the Ordinance standards to the approval criteria.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-30\\\",\\\"The BESS is described as 'optional' but is granted, priced and secured as a firm part of the project.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.II; Planning Commission letter, s.I\\\",\\n    \\\"'The Application includes an optional battery energy storage system (BESS) as a secondary and accessory use'; the county describes 'an optional 40 MW / 160 MWh battery energy storage system'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.V and Conditions 12, 22, 33; applicant letter; Solicitor email\\\",\\n    \\\"The Order grants 'an accessory 40 MW / 160 MWh battery energy storage system'; the $200 million cost includes $32 million for BESS; the decommissioning security covers BESS; the PILOT is 'payable in lieu of real property taxes on the solar facility and battery energy storage system components'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Scope\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"If the BESS is genuinely optional, the approval should say what happens if it is not built: whether the $32 million of the decommissioning estimate falls away, whether the decommissioning security is recalculated, whether the BESS setbacks and Emergency Response Plan obligations lapse, and whether the PILOT is adjusted. As drafted, the applicant could decline to build the BESS and still owe (or claim) figures computed on a project that includes it. Note also that the Ordinance treats energy storage as a separate conditional use category, s.(B)(15), whose standards apply 'in full' to an accessory BESS - see D-12.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-31\\\",\\\"The Township Engineer dates the first hearing to June; it was held in August.\\\",\\n    \\\"Township Engineer email, 10 January 2025\\\",\\n    \\\"'It's been a long road since the first hearing back in June...'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.I paras 1 and 7\\\",\\n    \\\"Application filed 3 June 2024; 'Public Hearing No. 1 was held on August 14, 2024'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Clerical\\\",\\\"Low\\\",\\n    \\\"Informal correspondence only; no compliance effect. Noted because the engineer's email will form part of the implementation record and is being copied to the Chair.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-32\\\",\\\"The appeal period is stated to have expired on Sunday, 5 January 2025.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.I para 10 and Notice of Appeal Rights; Township Engineer email; Township Solicitor email\\\",\\n    \\\"'expires on January 5, 2025' / 'the appeal period... expired on January 5th with no appeal filed' / 'expired on January 5, 2025'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Calendar and 1 Pa.C.S. s.1908\\\",\\n    \\\"5 January 2025 was a Sunday. Where the last day of a statutory period falls on a Saturday, Sunday or legal holiday, the period runs to the end of the next business day - here Monday, 6 January 2025.\\\",\\n    \\\"Date computation\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"The Decision and both emails understate the appeal window by one day. In this instance nothing turns on it, because no appeal was filed on either date and the Solicitor's email of 15 January 2025 confirms finality. But it is a live example of a date-handling weakness that recurs in the same instrument: 6 December 2026 (the s.(k)(5) permit expiry) is also a Sunday, and 31 March 2029 (Condition 32) is a Saturday (RC-12, RC-13). Build business-day adjustment into every date the Township administers.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-33\\\",\\\"Findings of fact attribute Ordinance standards that the Ordinance does not contain.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Finding 7\\\",\\n    \\\"'...satisfies the dimensional requirements of the Ordinance for the Agricultural-Rural (A-R) zoning district, including minimum lot area, minimum lot width, maximum building coverage, and maximum impervious surface coverage requirements.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, Table 27-605-1\\\",\\n    \\\"The table sets minimum lot area (50 acres), maximum lot coverage (impervious surfaces within the fenced area, 75%), heights, and setbacks. There is no minimum lot width standard and no 'maximum building coverage' standard.\\\",\\n    \\\"Findings\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"A finding of compliance with standards that do not exist cannot support Conclusion 2. The substantive risk is that the two standards that do exist in this family - the 50-acre minimum (met, 850 acres) and the 75% lot coverage ceiling (apparently not met, I-01) - were never actually tested. Rewrite Finding 7 against the four-column table and record the impervious-area computation.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-34\\\",\\\"The compliance analysis rests on an uncertified ordinance excerpt whose certification is unsigned and undated.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance excerpt, certification block and cover note\\\",\\n    \\\"'CERTIFIED as a true and correct excerpt...' with a blank signature line, name 'Karen M. Brandt, Township Secretary' and a blank date; and the disclaimer that 'In the event of any discrepancy between this excerpt and the official codified Township Zoning Ordinance on file with the Township Secretary, the official codified ordinance shall control.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.VIII (attestation)\\\",\\n    \\\"The Decision is attested by 'Martha Weaver, Township Secretary'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Authentication\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"Every mapping in Part 1 of this matrix, and every gap identified in Part 3, is drawn from an excerpt that disclaims its own controlling effect, that has not been signed or dated, and that names a different Township Secretary from the officer who attested the Decision. Two consequences follow: (i) before any of these findings is relied on in a permit review, an enforcement action or an amendment, the corresponding sections of the codified ordinance (including the full text of s.(B)(15), which is only excerpted here) should be pulled and conformed; and (ii) if the codified text differs from the excerpt, the gap analysis in Part 3 must be re-run. The excerpt also omits Article VI provisions other than ss.27-601, 27-602 and 27-605, so requirements in ss.27-603, 27-604, 27-606 and following have not been checked at all.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-35\\\",\\\"Conditions on environmental clearance defer items that the Ordinance requires before the record closes, while the findings assert full compliance.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Findings 1, 12 and 13; Conclusions 2 and 8\\\",\\n    \\\"Finding 1: the application was complete. Finding 12: PHMC consultation 'is required and has not yet been completed as of the date of this Decision'. Finding 13: formal consultation with USFWS and PFBC 'is required and has not yet been completed'. Conclusion 2: the record establishes that the project 'meets the requirements for... decommissioning, and other dimensional and operational standards'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, s.27-605(B)(14)(f)(3) and (f)(4)\\\",\\n    \\\"Species consultations must be completed and clearances obtained 'prior to any land disturbance activity on the affected parcels'; grading near a historic resource may not begin until PHMC issues a written determination.\\\",\\n    \\\"Internal consistency\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"Deferring these consultations to the pre-grading stage is consistent with the Ordinance, which keys them to land disturbance rather than to approval. The tension is with the Decision's own language: an unqualified finding of compliance with 'all' standards cannot sit alongside findings that two mandatory consultations remain outstanding. The drafting fix is straightforward (qualify Conclusions 2 and 8 as compliance 'subject to the conditions herein'), and it removes an unnecessary target from the face of the instrument.\\\")\\n\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# INDEPENDENT RECOMPUTATIONS\\n# result: Match / Does not match / Flag\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\nRECALC = []\\ndef rc(rid, item, stated, src, check, result, note):\\n    RECALC.append(dict(id=rid, item=item, stated=stated, src=src, check=check, result=result, note=note))\\n\\nrc(\\\"RC-01\\\",\\\"Total project acreage\\\",\\\"850.0 acres\\\",\\\"D&O s.II schedule\\\",\\n   \\\"185.3+142.7+210.4+98.6+75.2+88.9+48.9 = 850.0\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"Column adds correctly.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-02\\\",\\\"Total project acreage (applicant schedule)\\\",\\\"850 acres\\\",\\\"Applicant letter table\\\",\\n   \\\"145+110+130+95+120+140+110 = 850\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"Total matches, but see RC-03.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-03\\\",\\\"Parcel-by-parcel variance between schedules\\\",\\\"--\\\",\\\"D&O/County vs applicant letter\\\",\\n   \\\"+40.3, +32.7, +80.4, +3.6, -44.8, -51.1, -61.1 = 0.0\\\",\\\"Does not match\\\",\\\"Individual parcels differ by up to 61.1 acres while the total is held at 850 (I-04). One schedule is wrong.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-04\\\",\\\"Agricultural mitigation: acreage vs payment\\\",\\\"620 acres; $747,600\\\",\\\"D&O Condition 28\\\",\\n   \\\"620 x $1,200 = $744,000. $747,600 / $1,200 = 623.0 acres. 623 x $1,200 = $747,600\\\",\\\"Does not match\\\",\\\"The dollar figure corresponds to 623 acres (Findings 14, applicant letter, county letter). The acreage recital in Condition 28 is wrong by 3 acres / $3,600 (I-02).\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-05\\\",\\\"Agricultural mitigation: statewide-importance acreage\\\",\\\"Not quantified\\\",\\\"Ord. (f)(6); D&O Finding 14\\\",\\n   \\\"Eligible acreage = prime farmland + farmland of statewide importance. 850 - 623 = 227 acres unclassified in the record. If even 100 of those acres qualify: 723 x $1,200 = $867,600 (+$120,000). If all 227 qualify: 850 x $1,200 = $1,020,000 (+$272,400).\\\",\\\"Flag\\\",\\\"Material under-payment risk. s.(f)(6) also requires Conservation District confirmation of acreage, which is absent from the record.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-06\\\",\\\"Decommissioning: net cost and security\\\",\\\"$6,300,000 net; $7,875,000 security\\\",\\\"D&O Finding 22; Condition 22\\\",\\n   \\\"$8,400,000 - $2,100,000 = $6,300,000. $6,300,000 x 1.25 = $7,875,000.\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"Arithmetic correct.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-07\\\",\\\"Decommissioning: salvage-credit floor\\\",\\\"Must not fall below 75% of gross\\\",\\\"Ord. s.(i)(3)\\\",\\n   \\\"75% of $8,400,000 = $6,300,000. Security of $7,875,000 exceeds the floor by $1,575,000.\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"Floor satisfied. Note the floor would bite only if salvage value exceeded 40% of gross.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-08\\\",\\\"DC-to-AC ratio\\\",\\\"1.30\\\",\\\"D&O s.II; applicant letter\\\",\\n   \\\"156 MW DC / 120 MW AC = 1.30\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"--\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-09\\\",\\\"BESS energy capacity\\\",\\\"40 MW / 160 MWh; 4-hour duration\\\",\\\"D&O s.II\\\",\\n   \\\"40 MW x 4 h = 160 MWh\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"Exceeds the 5 MWh threshold in the 'Energy Storage Facility' definition, so s.(B)(15) standards apply in full.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-10\\\",\\\"Maximum lot coverage\\\",\\\"Asserted compliant\\\",\\\"Ord. Table 27-605-1; D&O s.II\\\",\\n   \\\"580 acres panels / 720 acres fenced = 80.56%. 75% of 720 = 540 acres. Exceedance = 40 acres / 5.56 points.\\\",\\\"Does not match\\\",\\\"On the face of the Ordinance (panels treated as semi-impervious in (f)(1)), coverage exceeds the ceiling (I-01). Requires the impervious-area schedule and an Engineer determination.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-11\\\",\\\"Condition 10 deadline: 90 days from the Decision\\\",\\\"6 March 2025\\\",\\\"D&O Condition 10\\\",\\n   \\\"6 Dec 2024 + 90 days = 6 March 2025 (Thursday). Count: 25 days remaining in December + 31 + 28 + 6 = 90.\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"Correct. Interacts with a 60-90 day Conservation District review under Condition 11, which means the CD submission was needed by early January 2025.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-12\\\",\\\"Appeal window: 30 days from 6 December 2024\\\",\\\"5 January 2025\\\",\\\"D&O s.I para 10\\\",\\n   \\\"6 Dec 2024 + 30 days = 5 January 2025, a Sunday. Under 1 Pa.C.S. s.1908 the period runs to Monday 6 January 2025.\\\",\\\"Flag\\\",\\\"One-day understatement (I-32). No practical consequence because no appeal was filed.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-13\\\",\\\"Conditional use expiry: 24 months (+12)\\\",\\\"Not stated\\\",\\\"Ord. s.(k)(5)\\\",\\n   \\\"6 Dec 2024 + 24 months = 6 December 2026 (a Sunday). Maximum extension 12 months = 6 December 2027. Target COD is 31 December 2027 - 25 days after the outer permit limit.\\\",\\\"Flag\\\",\\\"Permits must issue by December 2026 at the latest; on a 1 April 2026 construction start the practical deadline is roughly March 2026. The expiry is not recited in either approval instrument (G-23).\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-14\\\",\\\"First annual report date\\\",\\\"31 March of the first full calendar year after COD\\\",\\\"D&O Condition 32\\\",\\n   \\\"COD 31 Dec 2027. Reading 1: 31 March 2028 - before 12 months of operation. Reading 2: 31 March 2029. Ordinance (l)(1) requires the report within 60 days of the COD anniversary = 1 March 2029.\\\",\\\"Does not match\\\",\\\"Ambiguous and, on either reading, inconsistent with the Ordinance (I-19).\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-15\\\",\\\"PILOT: cumulative Years 1-15 under each escalator proposal\\\",\\\"Both described as 'significant' but not quantified\\\",\\\"Solicitor email, 15 January 2025\\\",\\n   \\\"Applicant (flat Years 1-15): 15 x $385,000 = $5,775,000. Township (2% from Year 11): $3,850,000 + ($392,700 + $400,554 + $408,565 + $416,736 + $425,071) = $5,893,627. Differential = $118,627. Over a 30-year term the differential compounds to approx. $3.1 million.\\\",\\\"Flag\\\",\\\"Quantifies the gap. The Township's proposal also conflicts with Condition 30, which fixes flat payments for Years 1-15 (I-16).\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-16\\\",\\\"PILOT: Year 15 payment under a Year 11 escalator\\\",\\\"$425,048\\\",\\\"Solicitor email\\\",\\n   \\\"$385,000 x 1.02^5 = $425,071.11\\\",\\\"Does not match\\\",\\\"Transcription slip of $23; immaterial but should be corrected in the negotiation record.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-17\\\",\\\"PILOT: rate per MW and per acre\\\",\\\"$3,208/MW/yr; $453/acre/yr\\\",\\\"Solicitor email\\\",\\n   \\\"$385,000 / 120 MW = $3,208.33. $385,000 / 850 acres = $452.94. On a fenced-acre basis: $534.72. On a panel-acre basis: $663.79.\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"Correct as computed. Note the per-acre figure is highly sensitive to which acreage denominator is used (850, 720 or 580) - fix the basis in the agreement.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-18\\\",\\\"Year 16 PILOT payment\\\",\\\"$392,700\\\",\\\"Applicant letter s.VI.A; Solicitor email\\\",\\n   \\\"$385,000 x 1.02 = $392,700\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"--\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-19\\\",\\\"Total estimated annual tax revenue\\\",\\\"$427,000\\\",\\\"D&O Finding 18\\\",\\n   \\\"$385,000 + $42,000 = $427,000\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"Arithmetic correct; composition overstated as Township revenue (I-24).\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-20\\\",\\\"Construction cost split\\\",\\\"$200 million\\\",\\\"D&O Finding 16\\\",\\n   \\\"$168M + $32M = $200M\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"--\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-21\\\",\\\"Hearing record to written decision\\\",\\\"Decision 6 December 2024\\\",\\\"D&O s.I paras 7(b)-(c) and 10\\\",\\n   \\\"Last hearing concluded 9 October 2024; written Decision 6 December 2024 = 58 days. Board vote 18 November 2024 = 40 days after the hearing and 18 days before the written decision.\\\",\\\"Flag\\\",\\\"Whether the MPC's decision deadline runs to the vote or to the written decision should be confirmed against the record (see risk R-18). If a written time extension was granted, it should be in the file.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-22\\\",\\\"Panel coverage relative to prime farmland\\\",\\\"580 acres of panels; 623 acres prime farmland\\\",\\\"D&O s.II; Finding 14\\\",\\n   \\\"580 / 623 = 93.1%, so at most 43 acres of prime farmland would remain unpaneled if all panels sat on prime soils.\\\",\\\"Flag\\\",\\\"Implausible as a design fact; suggests the two figures are drawn from different datasets. Supports the request for the NRCS/Conservation District acreage confirmation required by s.(f)(6).\\\")\\nPYEOF\\necho ok\"}"}], "input_tokens": 90675, "output_tokens": 12108, "finish_reason": "completed"}
{"turn": 11, "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "call_00_Slhp7rnDJJx7geS1J2FI7373", "tool_name": "bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cat > $WORKSPACE_DIR/build/content_findings.py <<'PYEOF'\\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\\n\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# CROSS-REFERENCE INCONSISTENCY REGISTER\\n# severity: Critical / High / Medium / Low\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\nINCONS = []\\ndef inc(iid, topic, docA, textA, docB, textB, kind, sev, analysis):\\n    INCONS.append(dict(id=iid, topic=topic, docA=docA, textA=textA, docB=docB, textB=textB,\\n                       kind=kind, sev=sev, analysis=analysis))\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-01\\\",\\\"Lot coverage: 580 acres of panels inside a 720-acre fence is 80.6% coverage against a 75% ceiling.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.II and Finding 7\\\",\\n    \\\"Panel coverage approx. 580 acres; fenced area approx. 720 acres. Finding 7 holds the site plan satisfies 'maximum impervious surface coverage requirements'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, Table 27-605-1\\\",\\n    \\\"'Maximum Lot Coverage (impervious surfaces within fenced area) - 75% of fenced area'. s.(f)(1) refers to 'impervious and semi-impervious surfaces (such as solar panels and access roads)'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Numeric / non-compliance\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\n    \\\"580 / 720 = 80.6%, i.e. 40 acres above the 540-acre ceiling (RC-10). The Ordinance's own stormwater provision treats panels as semi-impervious, which makes the 75% ceiling applicable to tracker area on its face. Finding 7 asserts compliance without any impervious-area computation in the record, and neither the Engineer's September 2024 letters nor the Planning Commission letter quantifies impervious coverage. Either the Ordinance standard is not met, or the Township has adopted an unstated interpretation (panels not counted) that should be recorded. Obtain the impervious/semi-impervious area schedule and a written Engineer determination; if panels count, the layout requires redesign or a s.(k)(3) amendment.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-02\\\",\\\"Agricultural mitigation is stated as 620 acres but is priced on 623 acres.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 28\\\",\\n    \\\"'...in the amount of Seven Hundred Forty-Seven Thousand Six Hundred Dollars ($747,600), calculated as 620 acres of prime agricultural land at $1,200 per acre.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Finding 14; applicant letter s.IV.D; Planning Commission letter s.II\\\",\\n    \\\"Finding 14, the applicant letter and the county letter all state 623 acres of prime agricultural land; 623 x $1,200 = $747,600.\\\",\\n    \\\"Numeric\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"620 x $1,200 = $744,000, not $747,600 (RC-04). The dollar figure is right and the acreage recital is wrong, but the error matters twice over: (i) the operative payment condition recites a figure that does not compute, inviting a dispute over whether the obligation is 620 acres or $747,600; and (ii) s.(f)(6) requires mitigation for prime farmland AND farmland of statewide importance, so the true mitigaable acreage is likely higher than 623 - the Decision's own Finding 14 places the balance of the 850 acres in 'farmland of statewide importance, woodland, or other non-prime categories'. At $1,200/acre, every 10 acres of omitted statewide-importance land is $12,000; if all 227 residual acres qualified the shortfall would be approx. $272,400 (RC-05). Correct the acreage recital and obtain Conservation District confirmation of acreage as required by s.(f)(6).\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-03\\\",\\\"Operational road maintenance bond: $150,000 or $100,000?\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 25\\\",\\n    \\\"'...the road maintenance bond shall be reduced to One Hundred Fifty Thousand Dollars ($150,000) for the operational period...'\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter, s.IV.F and Summary item 7\\\",\\n    \\\"'...road maintenance bond of $500,000 during the construction period, to be reduced to $100,000 during the operational period.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Financial term\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"The applicant's written acceptance of conditions assumed a $100,000 operational bond; the Decision imposed $150,000. The applicant has not accepted the higher figure, and Condition 25 will be contested at permit stage unless the applicant confirms acceptance in writing. Note also the Township Engineer's 10 January 2025 email refers only to the $500,000 construction bond and is silent on the reduction, so the point is unresolved on the current file.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-04\\\",\\\"Parcel acreages differ parcel-by-parcel between the applicant's filing letter and the Decision / county letter, although both total 850 acres.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.II and Finding 6; Planning Commission letter, s.I\\\",\\n    \\\"185.3 / 142.7 / 210.4 / 98.6 / 75.2 / 88.9 / 48.9 acres (Parcels 120-45-001 to -007); total 850.0 acres.\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter, Project Summary table\\\",\\n    \\\"145 / 110 / 130 / 95 / 120 / 140 / 110 acres; total 850 acres.\\\",\\n    \\\"Numeric\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"The seven individual variances (+40.3, +32.7, +80.4, +3.6, -44.8, -51.1, -61.1) sum to exactly zero, which suggests one schedule was stretched to preserve the 850-acre headline (RC-02, RC-03). This is not a drafting nicety: parcel acreage drives the lease verification in Condition 3, the agricultural mitigation calculation, the s.(k)(5) scope of approval, and any future amendment. Parcel 120-45-005 varies by 44.8 acres (37%) and Parcel 120-45-007 by 61.1 acres (56%). Obtain the recorded deed acreages and reconcile with the site plan and the leases. Until reconciled, no parcel-level figure in the file can be relied upon.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-05\\\",\\\"Lease terms for Parcels 120-45-003 and -004: two or three five-year extensions?\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.II and Finding 6\\\",\\n    \\\"'...30 years + three 5-year extensions' for Parcels -003 and -004.\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter, Project Summary table\\\",\\n    \\\"'...30 years + two 5-year renewals' for the same parcels.\\\",\\n    \\\"Term\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"The discrepancy is 5 years of site control per parcel, and it matters because Condition 3 requires the applicant to demonstrate 'the legal right to develop, construct and operate... for the full lease term, including any extension periods'. It also matters to the decommissioning horizon: a 45-year maximum term (30 + 3x5) rather than 40 years. Verify against the executed leases, not the summaries.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-06\\\",\\\"Substation / inverter setback from occupied dwellings: 500 ft in the Ordinance, 250 ft in the applicant's summary, and absent from the Decision.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, Table 27-605-1\\\",\\n    \\\"'Setback from Occupied Dwelling on Non-Participating Property - Substations, Inverters, Power Conversion Stations - 500 feet.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter, Summary item 1; Decision and Order, Condition 6\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant: '250 feet from occupied dwellings on non-participating parcels for substations and inverter stations.' Condition 6 imposes only a 250 ft property-line setback and is silent on dwellings.\\\",\\n    \\\"Omission / numeric\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\n    \\\"This is the most consequential omission in the condition set. The Township Engineer, the Planning Commission and the Board all appear to have proceeded on the premise that the 250 ft figure addressed substations generally; the Planning Commission letter even states that 'all solar components are located a minimum of 500 feet from any occupied dwelling', which is a different and broader assertion than the condition set delivers. Because Condition 6 contains no dwelling setback at all, a substation or conversion station could be sited 260 ft from a residence without breaching any condition of approval, while breaching the Ordinance. Either the site plan happens to comply (in which case say so in a condition and on the plan) or the design is built to the 250 ft figure in the applicant's letter (in which case there is an Ordinance non-compliance embedded in the approval). Resolve before any permit issues.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-07\\\",\\\"Noise: two different ANSI measurement standards, two different study authors, and no dwelling receptors.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 18\\\",\\n    \\\"'...shall not exceed forty-five (45) dBA at the nearest non-participating property line, as measured in accordance with ANSI S12.9-2013, Part 3.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Finding 20; applicant letter s.IV.B and Summary item 3\\\",\\n    \\\"Finding 20 and the applicant both describe the study as using 'ANSI/ASA S12.9-2013/Part 2'; the applicant expressly accepted a condition 'measured in accordance with the ANSI/ASA S12.9-2013/Part 2 standard referenced in the noise study'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Standard / inconsistency\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"Part 2 and Part 3 are materially different methods (long-term, wide-area measurement versus short-term measurement with an observer present), and the choice determines how compliance is proved years from now. The applicant accepted Part 2; the Decision imposed Part 3. If the Township later measures under Part 3, the applicant is well placed to say it never agreed to that test. Separately, Finding 20 attributes the study to 'Harris Acoustics Group', who appears nowhere in the procedural history (s.I paras 5-6 list only Fieldstone and Clarendon as Township consultants and Ridgepoint as the applicant's consultant) and nowhere in the applicant's enclosure list, which attributes the same study to Ridgepoint / Priya Mukherjee. Fix the standard by amendment and correct or explain the authorship. Also note s.(b)(7) required modelling at the nearest occupied dwellings, which neither the Finding nor the condition addresses.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-08\\\",\\\"Screening buffer: the Ordinance requires it on every qualifying boundary; the Decision requires it only on the north and east.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, s.27-605(B)(14)(d)(1)\\\",\\n    \\\"'...along any project site boundary that is adjacent to: (i) a non-participating property containing an occupied dwelling located within one thousand (1,000) feet of the project site boundary; or (ii) a public road right-of-way.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 16 (and Planning Commission letter s.III.B)\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 16 requires the buffer 'along the northern and eastern project boundaries, at locations adjacent to non-participating properties with occupied residences'. The county recommended buffers 'along all project boundaries adjacent to non-participating properties with occupied residences' and 'along all required frontages... and public roadways'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Scope\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 16 is narrower than the Ordinance in two independent respects: it is limited to two compass points, and it omits road frontages entirely. A condition cannot waive an Ordinance standard, so the Ordinance standard continues to apply and the Decision should say so. Because Conclusion 2 of the Decision asserts compliance with the Ordinance's 'screening' standard, and Condition 16 does not deliver that standard, the conclusion is unsupported on the face of the instrument. This is also the ground the dissenting Supervisors pressed (s.VII, third ground), so it is the most likely focus of any collateral challenge or neighbour complaint.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-09\\\",\\\"Site access: the Ordinance requires two 20-ft access points; the Decision describes one primary access and 16-ft internal roads.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, s.27-605(B)(14)(h)(3)\\\",\\n    \\\"'Access to the project site shall be provided via a minimum of two (2) access points, each with a minimum driveway width of twenty (20) feet... Access points shall be designed to provide adequate sight distance in accordance with PennDOT standards and shall be approved by the Township Engineer.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 7\\\",\\n    \\\"'The minimum width of internal access roads shall be sixteen (16) feet. The primary construction access point shall be located on Hershey Mill Road...'\\\",\\n    \\\"Scope / numeric\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"No second access point appears anywhere in the condition set, which is both an Ordinance compliance gap and a life-safety issue for fire apparatus access to a 720-acre energised site. The 16 ft / 20 ft difference may be explainable (internal road versus driveway) but the distinction is nowhere drawn. The Township Engineer's 10 January 2025 email compounds the ambiguity: he asks whether access is from a state road or a Township road and whether a deceleration lane is required, which shows the access design is not yet settled. Require a conforming access plan with the second access point, sight-distance analysis and Engineer approval before any permit.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-10\\\",\\\"The traffic management plan obligation is imposed twice, in two conditions with different content lists.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 7\\\",\\n    \\\"'A Traffic Management Plan shall be prepared... not less than sixty (60) days prior to commencement of construction. The Traffic Management Plan shall address haul routes, vehicle staging areas, traffic control measures, and any temporary road closures or detours.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 27\\\",\\n    \\\"'A construction traffic management plan shall be prepared... at least sixty (60) days prior... shall specify designated haul routes, traffic control measures, flagging procedures, hours of construction vehicle operations, load limits, and any temporary road closures or detours.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Duplication\\\",\\\"Low\\\",\\n    \\\"Same deliverable, same deadline, two slightly different content lists. The practical risk is a submission that satisfies one condition and is said to fail the other, or an argument that the second condition is surplusage. Consolidate into a single condition listing the union of the required contents.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-11\\\",\\\"The Planning Commission advised that the BESS setback of 250 ft 'is consistent with the ordinance'. The Ordinance requires 300 ft.\\\",\\n    \\\"Planning Commission letter, s.III.A\\\",\\n    \\\"'With respect to the battery energy storage system, the proposed battery energy storage system setback of 250 feet from non-participating property lines is consistent with the ordinance.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, Table 27-605-1 Note 1 and s.27-605(B)(15)(c)(3)\\\",\\n    \\\"300 ft minimum from any non-participating property line for energy storage facilities.\\\",\\n    \\\"Error in advisory source\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"The Decision itself correctly imposes 300 ft (Condition 12(a)), so the operative condition is right. But the error is worth recording for three reasons: it shows the county's advisory review did not accurately check the dimensional table; it invites the applicant to argue 250 ft as the 'approved' figure; and if the site plan was drawn to 250 ft on the strength of this advice, the BESS compound is 50 ft inside the required setback. Confirm the BESS dimension on the plan. The same letter also asserts that 'all solar components' are 500 ft from dwellings, which overstates what the site plan is shown to demonstrate.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-12\\\",\\\"Decommissioning timing: 12 months in the Ordinance, 18 months in the Decision, 12 months in the applicant's letter - and the cultural-resource condition invokes a federal process that does not apply.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 21\\\",\\n    \\\"'...restoration of the Project site to a condition suitable for agricultural use, within eighteen (18) months of the cessation of operations.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance s.27-605(B)(14)(i)(7); applicant letter s.VI.B\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance: 'Decommissioning shall be completed within twelve (12) months of the date on which the facility ceases generating...'. Applicant: 'restored to a condition suitable for agricultural use within 12 months of facility deactivation'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Timing\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 21 is 50% longer than the Ordinance standard and 50% longer than the applicant's own commitment, so it is both an Ordinance non-conformity and a gratuitous concession the applicant never asked for. Read with Condition 24 (12 months' non-generation, then a 90-day contest window) and s.(i)(7)'s 24-month outer cap, the three mechanisms together produce an end-state that is internally inconsistent and hard to enforce. Restate s.(i)(7) verbatim. Separately, Condition 9 requires 'written confirmation... as determined through the Section 106 consultation process or equivalent state-level review'; no federal action, funding or licence is identified anywhere in the file, so s.106 is not triggered and the PHMC review should be routed under the Pennsylvania History Code, 37 Pa. C.S. ss.101 et seq., as s.(f)(4) contemplates.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-13\\\",\\\"The solar glare analysis was an application-stage deliverable but was deferred to the post-approval, pre-permit stage.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, s.27-605(B)(14)(b)(8) and the closing sentence of s.(b)\\\",\\n    \\\"The glare analysis is listed among the application materials, and 'An application shall not be deemed complete for purposes of scheduling a public hearing until all items listed above have been submitted to the Zoning Officer.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 19; applicant letter; Planning Commission letter s.IV\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 19 defers the analysis to 'prior to issuance of building permits'. The applicant writes in the future tense ('The Applicant will prepare and submit a solar glare analysis'), and the county 'recommends that a solar glare analysis be completed'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Procedural / completeness\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"Three of the six documents in the file, read together, establish that a mandatory application item was not before the Board when it voted. Finding 1 nevertheless states the application was complete and Conclusion 8 states the decision is supported by substantial evidence. The appeal period has run, so the practical exposure is now (i) a validity challenge premised on incomplete record, (ii) a permit-stage impasse if the glare study shows an impact on a roadway or residence within one mile, and (iii) an enforcement problem, because no condition tells the applicant what happens if the study is adverse. The Ordinance's answer (s.(g)(2)) is that identified glare impacts must be mitigated to the Board's satisfaction - so the condition should require Board approval of any needed mitigation, not merely submission of a report.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-14\\\",\\\"Setbacks are measured from different reference points.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 5\\\",\\n    \\\"'All solar panels shall maintain the following minimum setbacks, measured from the nearest edge of the panel at maximum tilt...'\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, Table 27-605-1, Note 2\\\",\\n    \\\"'All setbacks... shall be measured from the nearest point of the applicable component (including any foundation, concrete pad, or supporting structure) to the nearest point of the applicable boundary line...'\\\",\\n    \\\"Measurement basis\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"Measuring from the panel edge at maximum tilt gives a larger setback than measuring from the foundation in most configurations (the panel overhangs its post), so the practical effect is likely conservative - but it is still a departure from the codified measurement basis and creates an argument in any future compliance measurement about which datum governs. Note 3 (the more restrictive of overlapping standards applies) is also not restated anywhere in the Decision.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-15\\\",\\\"The property value assessment does not address the 0-500 ft band that the Ordinance puts in scope.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, s.27-605(B)(14)(b)(12) and (j)(5)\\\",\\n    \\\"The assessment is to 'evaluate the potential impact of the proposed facility on the market value of residential properties within one thousand (1,000) feet of the project site boundary.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Finding 19; applicant letter s.IV.C\\\",\\n    \\\"Finding 19 concludes 'no material negative impact' without stating the geographic scope of the analysis. The applicant describes the finding as 'no statistically significant negative impact on property values for residences located beyond 500 feet of a solar project boundary'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Scope\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"If the study's actual support stops at 500 ft, the Ordinance's 1,000-ft requirement is only half answered, and the unaddressed 500 ft band is precisely the band in which the 500 ft panel setback places the nearest neighbours. The dissenting Supervisors attacked exactly this: the report 'relies heavily on comparable data from other regions' (s.VII, second ground). Obtain the report and confirm (a) the study area, (b) the number of local paired sales, and (c) whether the conclusion is supported for the 0-500 ft band. Finding 19's unqualified phrasing is not supportable on the applicant's own characterisation.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-16\\\",\\\"The Township Solicitor states the Decision does not fix the escalator start year and proposes Year 11. The Decision fixes it at Year 16.\\\",\\n    \\\"Township Solicitor email, 15 January 2025\\\",\\n    \\\"'I will note that the Decision and Order references \\\\\\\"negotiated PILOT terms\\\\\\\" but does not specify the escalator commencement year, so this is a point for the agreement itself to resolve.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Finding 17 and Condition 30\\\",\\n    \\\"Both state '$385,000 per year for Years 1 through 15 of commercial operation, with a two percent (2%) annual escalator commencing thereafter for the remaining term of the agreement.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Term / record accuracy\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\n    \\\"The Decision does specify the escalator: flat for Years 1-15, escalating from Year 16. The Solicitor's Year 11 proposal therefore contradicts the instrument the Township adopted nine weeks earlier, and Condition 30 expressly ties permit issuance to a PILOT 'reflecting the negotiated terms' of $385,000 for Years 1-15. Executing a Year 11 escalator would depart from the Decision; refusing to do so leaves the Township bound to Year 16 unless it amends. The cumulative difference is approx. $118,627 over Years 1-15 and approx. $3.1 million more over a 30-year term (RC-15). The correct route is an amendment of Condition 30 (see I-28 / F-05 on modification procedure), not a side agreement. Separately, the Solicitor's arithmetic is slightly off: $385,000 x 1.02^5 = $425,071, not $425,048 (RC-16).\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-17\\\",\\\"Updated decommissioning estimates: 60 days or 90 days to top up the security, and no submission deadline in the Decision.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 23\\\",\\n    \\\"'...shall increase the decommissioning security to one hundred twenty-five percent (125%) of the updated net cost within ninety (90) days of receipt of the updated estimate by the Township.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, s.27-605(B)(14)(i)(6)\\\",\\n    \\\"Updated estimate 'shall be submitted to the Township within thirty (30) days of completion', and any increase 'within sixty (60) days of the date of the updated estimate'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Timing\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"The Decision doubles the Ordinance's cure period and drops the 30-day submission deadline altogether, which is what actually creates the security lag: without a submission deadline the Township may not 'receive' an estimate until long after it was prepared. Restate the Ordinance language. The same pattern appears in Condition 22, which omits the Ordinance's A.M. Best 'A- VII or better' rating floor for surety bonds and the requirement that the Township be named beneficiary of an escrow (G-26).\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-18\\\",\\\"Financial assurances: two conditions set two different deadlines, one of which permits posting after the first building permit.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 34\\\",\\n    \\\"Decommissioning security, road maintenance bond and agricultural mitigation payment 'shall be in place no later than sixty (60) days prior to the commencement of construction activities.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Conditions 22 and 28; Ordinance s.(i)(5) and (f)(6)\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 22: security 'shall be posted prior to issuance of the first building permit'. Condition 28: payment 'prior to issuance of the first building permit'. Ordinance (i)(5): 'No building permit shall be issued until the Township Solicitor has confirmed that the decommissioning security instrument is in proper form...'\\\",\\n    \\\"Deadline conflict\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"Because a building permit must precede construction, '60 days before construction' can fall after 'the first building permit'. Condition 34 therefore either restates the permit-stage conditions as a later, easier deadline, or is simply a second deadline - and on its face an applicant could tender the $7.875 million instrument, the $500,000 bond and the $747,600 payment after permits issued. The Ordinance cannot be waived by condition, so the Ordinance deadline controls; but the Decision should say so. Also note the Ordinance requires the agricultural payment before a building OR grading permit, which is earlier still, and that the parent guaranty under Condition 2 is omitted from the Condition 34 schedule.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-19\\\",\\\"Annual reporting: the Decision's deadline is later than the Ordinance's and the content list is narrower.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 32\\\",\\n    \\\"'...on or before March 31 of each year... The first annual report shall be due on March 31 of the first full calendar year following the date of commercial operation.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, s.27-605(B)(14)(l)(1)\\\",\\n    \\\"Report due 'within sixty (60) days of each anniversary of the commercial operation date', and must include total energy production in MWh and 'any material changes to the facility, its ownership, or its operations'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Timing / content\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"On the projected 31 December 2027 COD, the Ordinance would require the first report by about 1 March 2029 (60 days after the first anniversary on 31 December 2028), whereas Condition 32's 'March 31 of the first full calendar year following' COD is ambiguous between 31 March 2028 (before a full year of operation had elapsed) and 31 March 2029. Either reading conflicts with the Ordinance (RC-13). Generation in MWh and ownership changes are both dropped from the condition. Restate the Ordinance standard and add the two omitted content items.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-20\\\",\\\"Two different firms are credited with the noise study.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Finding 20\\\",\\n    \\\"'The Applicant submitted a noise impact study prepared by Harris Acoustics Group, a qualified acoustical engineering firm.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter, s.IV.B and enclosure 2\\\",\\n    \\\"'...noise impact assessment prepared by Ridgepoint Environmental Consultants LLC, under the direction of Lead Consultant Priya Mukherjee.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Attribution\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"Harris Acoustics Group is not identified as a retained consultant in the Decision's procedural history (s.I paras 5-6), does not appear in the applicant's enclosure list, and is not mentioned in either email. If a separate acoustical firm did the work, the record should show its retainer, its qualifications and whether it testified. If the Finding misattributes the work, it is an error in a finding of fact supporting Conclusion 4 (which credits 'the Harris Acoustics Group noise study'). Either way the Finding needs correction; the applicant should produce the report cover and signature page.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-21\\\",\\\"Two different docket numbers for the same application.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order (cover, caption, s.V and Certificate of Service)\\\",\\n    \\\"'Application No.: CU-2024-006'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Planning Commission letter (re-line and s.I)\\\",\\n    \\\"'Conditional Use Application No. CU-2024-012'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Docket\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"The county's s.609.1 referral, and therefore the advisory recommendation that Finding 4 relies on, is docketed under a different number from the decision. Possibilities: a transcription error in one document, or the referral was made under a separate county file number. This matters to record integrity, to the Certificate of Service (which refers only to CU-2024-006), and to any party trying to locate the county's file. Confirm the county's file number and correct whichever document is wrong; if they are genuinely two different applications, the referral needs explanation.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-22\\\",\\\"Construction job range.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Finding 16\\\",\\n    \\\"'approximately 350-400 construction jobs during the peak construction period'\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter, closing\\\",\\n    \\\"'will support approximately 300 to 400 jobs during the peak construction period'\\\",\\n    \\\"Numeric\\\",\\\"Low\\\",\\n    \\\"Immaterial to compliance, but the Decision adopted a floor (350) higher than the applicant's own evidence (300). Since no job-count condition attaches, no consequence follows; worth correcting for record accuracy. The same letter also claims the project will generate power 'sufficient to power approximately 25,000 Pennsylvania homes', a figure appearing nowhere in the findings of fact.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-23\\\",\\\"Three different addresses for the Township and its offices.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance excerpt (cover note)\\\",\\n    \\\"'...the Conestoga Township offices, 1220 Millwood Road, Conestoga, Pennsylvania 17516'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter (address block); Planning Commission letter (address block)\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant: '129 East Main Street, Conestoga, PA 17516'. County: 'Conestoga Township Municipal Building, 1217 River Road, Conestoga, PA 17516'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Clerical\\\",\\\"Low\\\",\\n    \\\"No compliance consequence, but three inconsistent municipal addresses across the six documents is a reliable indicator that the applicant's correspondence templates and the county's address records were never reconciled. Useful chiefly as a prompt to verify the addresses used for the mailed notice under Finding 5 and for the Certificate of Service.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-24\\\",\\\"'$427,000 per year in tax revenue to the Township' is not all Township revenue, and the school district is variously included and excluded.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Finding 18\\\",\\n    \\\"'Total estimated annual tax revenue to the Township from the Project is approximately $427,000, consisting of $385,000 per year under the negotiated PILOT terms and approximately $42,000 per year in real estate taxes assessed on non-exempt improvements...'\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter, closing; Township Solicitor email, 15 January 2025\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant: 'will generate an estimated $427,000 in annual tax revenue to the Township and the Conestoga Valley School District'. Solicitor: 'the total estimated annual revenue to the Township from the project is approximately $427,000'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Characterisation\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"Real estate taxes on non-exempt improvements are levied by the county, the township and the school district; only the Township's millage share accrues to the Township. Describing the whole $42,000 as Township revenue overstates Township income, and describing the $427,000 as revenue to the Township 'and' the school district double-counts the school district's share. There is a further structural question the file never answers: the PILOT is 'payable in lieu of real property taxes on the solar facility and BESS components' (per the Solicitor's 15 January 2025 summary), yet s.(j)(1) only authorises a PILOT where the applicant 'intends to seek any tax exemption, abatement, alternative energy credit, or alternative assessment' - and nothing in the record shows that the applicant applied for any such relief. If no exemption was sought, the facility is fully taxable and the Township may have traded an unpriced ad valorem liability for a fixed $385,000; if an exemption was sought, Condition 29's promise that school district taxes remain payable has to be squared with the scope of that exemption, which the Township cannot control (s.(j)(4)). Obtain the applicant's assessment/exemption position in writing.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-25\\\",\\\"Two different statutory bases for the 30-day appeal period.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.I para 10 and Notice of Appeal Rights\\\",\\n    \\\"'the thirty (30) day appeal period under the MPC, 53 P.S. s.11002-A' / 'Pursuant to Section 1002-A of the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code, 53 P.S. s.11002-A'\\\",\\n    \\\"Township Solicitor email, 15 January 2025\\\",\\n    \\\"'The 30-day appeal period under Section 914.1 of the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code expired on January 5, 2025'\\\",\\n    \\\"Citation\\\",\\\"Low\\\",\\n    \\\"Both provisions produce a 30-day appeal window, so nothing turns on it here, but the record should cite the operative provision consistently - and the correct one for a governing-body conditional use decision should be confirmed. Note also that Finding 5 and s.I para 4 variously cite s.609 and the county cites s.609.1 for the referral obligation (I-26).\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-26\\\",\\\"The county planning referral is cited under two different MPC sections.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.I para 4\\\",\\n    \\\"Refers to review by the Lancaster County Planning Commission 'in accordance with the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code', and the Ordinance excerpt cites 'Section 609 of the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code, 53 P.S. s.10609' as the referral basis.\\\",\\n    \\\"Planning Commission letter, opening paragraph\\\",\\n    \\\"'pursuant to Section 609.1 of the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code (Act 247, as amended)'\\\",\\n    \\\"Citation\\\",\\\"Low\\\",\\n    \\\"Section 609 and Section 609.1 (53 P.S. ss.10609 and 10609.1) are different provisions and only one governs referral of a conditional use application. Confirm and conform the citation across the decision, the ordinance excerpt and the county letter.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-27\\\",\\\"Where the screening buffers sit relative to the fence line.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.II and Condition 15\\\",\\n    \\\"Approx. 850 acres total, of which 'the total fenced project area will be approximately 720 acres, with the remaining acreage devoted to setbacks, vegetative buffers, access roads, and stormwater management facilities'; Condition 15 fences 'the entire fenced project area, encompassing approximately 720 acres'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Applicant letter, Project Summary\\\",\\n    \\\"'The remaining acreage within the fence line will accommodate access roads, stormwater management facilities, the BESS compound, power conversion stations, and vegetative buffers.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Scope\\\",\\\"Low\\\",\\n    \\\"On the Decision's figures the buffers sit outside the 720-acre fence, in the residual 130 acres - which is consistent with s.(e)(4)'s 10 ft fence-to-buffer separation. On the applicant's description they are inside the fence. The landscape and fence plans need to settle this, because s.(d)(2) requires the buffer to be within the project site boundary and s.(e)(4) requires the fence to be 10 ft clear of the buffer's interior edge.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-28\\\",\\\"Modification authority: the Decision lets the Township Engineer approve field changes that the Ordinance reserves to the Board.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 35\\\",\\n    \\\"'...modifications that do not constitute material changes may, at the discretion of the Board of Supervisors, be approved administratively upon written certification by the Township Engineer... Minor field adjustments that do not alter setbacks, height, total project acreage, or the number of project parcels may be approved in writing by the Township Engineer without further action by the Board.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, s.27-605(B)(14)(k)(3)\\\",\\n    \\\"'Modification, expansion, or reconfiguration of the approved project beyond the scope of the approved site plan shall require either: (i) an amendment to the conditional use approval... following the same substantive and procedural requirements as an original conditional use application... including public notice and hearing; or (ii) a new conditional use application.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Delegation\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"The second sentence of Condition 35 is defensible only if the enumerated adjustments are genuinely within 'the scope of the approved site plan'. Given that setbacks, height, acreage and parcel count are the very standards the Ordinance fixes, a field adjustment that touches them is by definition outside the approved scope. Tighten the condition to make clear that no Engineer approval may alter a standard in Table 27-605-1 or the approved plan's dimensions. Note this also bears on how the escalator dispute (I-16) must be resolved: by amendment, not by side letter.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-29\\\",\\\"Fence specification: the Ordinance's height ceiling and prohibited materials are not carried into the condition.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, Table 27-605-1 and s.27-605(B)(14)(e)(2)\\\",\\n    \\\"Fencing maximum height 8 ft; fencing 'topped with angled extension arms or similar deterrent devices'; 'The use of barbed wire, razor wire, or electrified fencing is expressly prohibited.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Condition 15\\\",\\n    \\\"'The Applicant shall install perimeter security fencing of at least seven (7) feet in height around the entire fenced project area...'\\\",\\n    \\\"Specification\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"The Decision sets a floor with no ceiling and drops the design and material restrictions. An 8 ft fence with barbed wire would satisfy Condition 15 and breach the Ordinance. Since fence design and materials must go to the Township Engineer for approval under Condition 15 in any event, add the Ordinance standards to the approval criteria.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-30\\\",\\\"The BESS is described as 'optional' but is granted, priced and secured as a firm part of the project.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.II; Planning Commission letter, s.I\\\",\\n    \\\"'The Application includes an optional battery energy storage system (BESS) as a secondary and accessory use'; the county describes 'an optional 40 MW / 160 MWh battery energy storage system'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.V and Conditions 12, 22, 33; applicant letter; Solicitor email\\\",\\n    \\\"The Order grants 'an accessory 40 MW / 160 MWh battery energy storage system'; the $200 million cost includes $32 million for BESS; the decommissioning security covers BESS; the PILOT is 'payable in lieu of real property taxes on the solar facility and battery energy storage system components'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Scope\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"If the BESS is genuinely optional, the approval should say what happens if it is not built: whether the $32 million of the decommissioning estimate falls away, whether the decommissioning security is recalculated, whether the BESS setbacks and Emergency Response Plan obligations lapse, and whether the PILOT is adjusted. As drafted, the applicant could decline to build the BESS and still owe (or claim) figures computed on a project that includes it. Note also that the Ordinance treats energy storage as a separate conditional use category, s.(B)(15), whose standards apply 'in full' to an accessory BESS - see D-12.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-31\\\",\\\"The Township Engineer dates the first hearing to June; it was held in August.\\\",\\n    \\\"Township Engineer email, 10 January 2025\\\",\\n    \\\"'It's been a long road since the first hearing back in June...'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.I paras 1 and 7\\\",\\n    \\\"Application filed 3 June 2024; 'Public Hearing No. 1 was held on August 14, 2024'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Clerical\\\",\\\"Low\\\",\\n    \\\"Informal correspondence only; no compliance effect. Noted because the engineer's email will form part of the implementation record and is being copied to the Chair.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-32\\\",\\\"The appeal period is stated to have expired on Sunday, 5 January 2025.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.I para 10 and Notice of Appeal Rights; Township Engineer email; Township Solicitor email\\\",\\n    \\\"'expires on January 5, 2025' / 'the appeal period... expired on January 5th with no appeal filed' / 'expired on January 5, 2025'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Calendar and 1 Pa.C.S. s.1908\\\",\\n    \\\"5 January 2025 was a Sunday. Where the last day of a statutory period falls on a Saturday, Sunday or legal holiday, the period runs to the end of the next business day - here Monday, 6 January 2025.\\\",\\n    \\\"Date computation\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"The Decision and both emails understate the appeal window by one day. In this instance nothing turns on it, because no appeal was filed on either date and the Solicitor's email of 15 January 2025 confirms finality. But it is a live example of a date-handling weakness that recurs in the same instrument: 6 December 2026 (the s.(k)(5) permit expiry) is also a Sunday, and 31 March 2029 (Condition 32) is a Saturday (RC-12, RC-13). Build business-day adjustment into every date the Township administers.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-33\\\",\\\"Findings of fact attribute Ordinance standards that the Ordinance does not contain.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Finding 7\\\",\\n    \\\"'...satisfies the dimensional requirements of the Ordinance for the Agricultural-Rural (A-R) zoning district, including minimum lot area, minimum lot width, maximum building coverage, and maximum impervious surface coverage requirements.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, Table 27-605-1\\\",\\n    \\\"The table sets minimum lot area (50 acres), maximum lot coverage (impervious surfaces within the fenced area, 75%), heights, and setbacks. There is no minimum lot width standard and no 'maximum building coverage' standard.\\\",\\n    \\\"Findings\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"A finding of compliance with standards that do not exist cannot support Conclusion 2. The substantive risk is that the two standards that do exist in this family - the 50-acre minimum (met, 850 acres) and the 75% lot coverage ceiling (apparently not met, I-01) - were never actually tested. Rewrite Finding 7 against the four-column table and record the impervious-area computation.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-34\\\",\\\"The compliance analysis rests on an uncertified ordinance excerpt whose certification is unsigned and undated.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance excerpt, certification block and cover note\\\",\\n    \\\"'CERTIFIED as a true and correct excerpt...' with a blank signature line, name 'Karen M. Brandt, Township Secretary' and a blank date; and the disclaimer that 'In the event of any discrepancy between this excerpt and the official codified Township Zoning Ordinance on file with the Township Secretary, the official codified ordinance shall control.'\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, s.VIII (attestation)\\\",\\n    \\\"The Decision is attested by 'Martha Weaver, Township Secretary'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Authentication\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n    \\\"Every mapping in Part 1 of this matrix, and every gap identified in Part 3, is drawn from an excerpt that disclaims its own controlling effect, that has not been signed or dated, and that names a different Township Secretary from the officer who attested the Decision. Two consequences follow: (i) before any of these findings is relied on in a permit review, an enforcement action or an amendment, the corresponding sections of the codified ordinance (including the full text of s.(B)(15), which is only excerpted here) should be pulled and conformed; and (ii) if the codified text differs from the excerpt, the gap analysis in Part 3 must be re-run. The excerpt also omits Article VI provisions other than ss.27-601, 27-602 and 27-605, so requirements in ss.27-603, 27-604, 27-606 and following have not been checked at all.\\\")\\n\\ninc(\\\"I-35\\\",\\\"Conditions on environmental clearance defer items that the Ordinance requires before the record closes, while the findings assert full compliance.\\\",\\n    \\\"Decision and Order, Findings 1, 12 and 13; Conclusions 2 and 8\\\",\\n    \\\"Finding 1: the application was complete. Finding 12: PHMC consultation 'is required and has not yet been completed as of the date of this Decision'. Finding 13: formal consultation with USFWS and PFBC 'is required and has not yet been completed'. Conclusion 2: the record establishes that the project 'meets the requirements for... decommissioning, and other dimensional and operational standards'.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance, s.27-605(B)(14)(f)(3) and (f)(4)\\\",\\n    \\\"Species consultations must be completed and clearances obtained 'prior to any land disturbance activity on the affected parcels'; grading near a historic resource may not begin until PHMC issues a written determination.\\\",\\n    \\\"Internal consistency\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n    \\\"Deferring these consultations to the pre-grading stage is consistent with the Ordinance, which keys them to land disturbance rather than to approval. The tension is with the Decision's own language: an unqualified finding of compliance with 'all' standards cannot sit alongside findings that two mandatory consultations remain outstanding. The drafting fix is straightforward (qualify Conclusions 2 and 8 as compliance 'subject to the conditions herein'), and it removes an unnecessary target from the face of the instrument.\\\")\\n\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# INDEPENDENT RECOMPUTATIONS\\n# result: Match / Does not match / Flag\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\nRECALC = []\\ndef rc(rid, item, stated, src, check, result, note):\\n    RECALC.append(dict(id=rid, item=item, stated=stated, src=src, check=check, result=result, note=note))\\n\\nrc(\\\"RC-01\\\",\\\"Total project acreage\\\",\\\"850.0 acres\\\",\\\"D&O s.II schedule\\\",\\n   \\\"185.3+142.7+210.4+98.6+75.2+88.9+48.9 = 850.0\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"Column adds correctly.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-02\\\",\\\"Total project acreage (applicant schedule)\\\",\\\"850 acres\\\",\\\"Applicant letter table\\\",\\n   \\\"145+110+130+95+120+140+110 = 850\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"Total matches, but see RC-03.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-03\\\",\\\"Parcel-by-parcel variance between schedules\\\",\\\"--\\\",\\\"D&O/County vs applicant letter\\\",\\n   \\\"+40.3, +32.7, +80.4, +3.6, -44.8, -51.1, -61.1 = 0.0\\\",\\\"Does not match\\\",\\\"Individual parcels differ by up to 61.1 acres while the total is held at 850 (I-04). One schedule is wrong.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-04\\\",\\\"Agricultural mitigation: acreage vs payment\\\",\\\"620 acres; $747,600\\\",\\\"D&O Condition 28\\\",\\n   \\\"620 x $1,200 = $744,000. $747,600 / $1,200 = 623.0 acres. 623 x $1,200 = $747,600\\\",\\\"Does not match\\\",\\\"The dollar figure corresponds to 623 acres (Findings 14, applicant letter, county letter). The acreage recital in Condition 28 is wrong by 3 acres / $3,600 (I-02).\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-05\\\",\\\"Agricultural mitigation: statewide-importance acreage\\\",\\\"Not quantified\\\",\\\"Ord. (f)(6); D&O Finding 14\\\",\\n   \\\"Eligible acreage = prime farmland + farmland of statewide importance. 850 - 623 = 227 acres unclassified in the record. If even 100 of those acres qualify: 723 x $1,200 = $867,600 (+$120,000). If all 227 qualify: 850 x $1,200 = $1,020,000 (+$272,400).\\\",\\\"Flag\\\",\\\"Material under-payment risk. s.(f)(6) also requires Conservation District confirmation of acreage, which is absent from the record.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-06\\\",\\\"Decommissioning: net cost and security\\\",\\\"$6,300,000 net; $7,875,000 security\\\",\\\"D&O Finding 22; Condition 22\\\",\\n   \\\"$8,400,000 - $2,100,000 = $6,300,000. $6,300,000 x 1.25 = $7,875,000.\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"Arithmetic correct.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-07\\\",\\\"Decommissioning: salvage-credit floor\\\",\\\"Must not fall below 75% of gross\\\",\\\"Ord. s.(i)(3)\\\",\\n   \\\"75% of $8,400,000 = $6,300,000. Security of $7,875,000 exceeds the floor by $1,575,000.\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"Floor satisfied. Note the floor would bite only if salvage value exceeded 40% of gross.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-08\\\",\\\"DC-to-AC ratio\\\",\\\"1.30\\\",\\\"D&O s.II; applicant letter\\\",\\n   \\\"156 MW DC / 120 MW AC = 1.30\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"--\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-09\\\",\\\"BESS energy capacity\\\",\\\"40 MW / 160 MWh; 4-hour duration\\\",\\\"D&O s.II\\\",\\n   \\\"40 MW x 4 h = 160 MWh\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"Exceeds the 5 MWh threshold in the 'Energy Storage Facility' definition, so s.(B)(15) standards apply in full.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-10\\\",\\\"Maximum lot coverage\\\",\\\"Asserted compliant\\\",\\\"Ord. Table 27-605-1; D&O s.II\\\",\\n   \\\"580 acres panels / 720 acres fenced = 80.56%. 75% of 720 = 540 acres. Exceedance = 40 acres / 5.56 points.\\\",\\\"Does not match\\\",\\\"On the face of the Ordinance (panels treated as semi-impervious in (f)(1)), coverage exceeds the ceiling (I-01). Requires the impervious-area schedule and an Engineer determination.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-11\\\",\\\"Condition 10 deadline: 90 days from the Decision\\\",\\\"6 March 2025\\\",\\\"D&O Condition 10\\\",\\n   \\\"6 Dec 2024 + 90 days = 6 March 2025 (Thursday). Count: 25 days remaining in December + 31 + 28 + 6 = 90.\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"Correct. Interacts with a 60-90 day Conservation District review under Condition 11, which means the CD submission was needed by early January 2025.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-12\\\",\\\"Appeal window: 30 days from 6 December 2024\\\",\\\"5 January 2025\\\",\\\"D&O s.I para 10\\\",\\n   \\\"6 Dec 2024 + 30 days = 5 January 2025, a Sunday. Under 1 Pa.C.S. s.1908 the period runs to Monday 6 January 2025.\\\",\\\"Flag\\\",\\\"One-day understatement (I-32). No practical consequence because no appeal was filed.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-13\\\",\\\"Conditional use expiry: 24 months (+12)\\\",\\\"Not stated\\\",\\\"Ord. s.(k)(5)\\\",\\n   \\\"6 Dec 2024 + 24 months = 6 December 2026 (a Sunday). Maximum extension 12 months = 6 December 2027. Target COD is 31 December 2027 - 25 days after the outer permit limit.\\\",\\\"Flag\\\",\\\"Permits must issue by December 2026 at the latest; on a 1 April 2026 construction start the practical deadline is roughly March 2026. The expiry is not recited in either approval instrument (G-23).\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-14\\\",\\\"First annual report date\\\",\\\"31 March of the first full calendar year after COD\\\",\\\"D&O Condition 32\\\",\\n   \\\"COD 31 Dec 2027. Reading 1: 31 March 2028 - before 12 months of operation. Reading 2: 31 March 2029. Ordinance (l)(1) requires the report within 60 days of the COD anniversary = 1 March 2029.\\\",\\\"Does not match\\\",\\\"Ambiguous and, on either reading, inconsistent with the Ordinance (I-19).\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-15\\\",\\\"PILOT: cumulative Years 1-15 under each escalator proposal\\\",\\\"Both described as 'significant' but not quantified\\\",\\\"Solicitor email, 15 January 2025\\\",\\n   \\\"Applicant (flat Years 1-15): 15 x $385,000 = $5,775,000. Township (2% from Year 11): $3,850,000 + ($392,700 + $400,554 + $408,565 + $416,736 + $425,071) = $5,893,627. Differential = $118,627. Over a 30-year term the differential compounds to approx. $3.1 million.\\\",\\\"Flag\\\",\\\"Quantifies the gap. The Township's proposal also conflicts with Condition 30, which fixes flat payments for Years 1-15 (I-16).\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-16\\\",\\\"PILOT: Year 15 payment under a Year 11 escalator\\\",\\\"$425,048\\\",\\\"Solicitor email\\\",\\n   \\\"$385,000 x 1.02^5 = $425,071.11\\\",\\\"Does not match\\\",\\\"Transcription slip of $23; immaterial but should be corrected in the negotiation record.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-17\\\",\\\"PILOT: rate per MW and per acre\\\",\\\"$3,208/MW/yr; $453/acre/yr\\\",\\\"Solicitor email\\\",\\n   \\\"$385,000 / 120 MW = $3,208.33. $385,000 / 850 acres = $452.94. On a fenced-acre basis: $534.72. On a panel-acre basis: $663.79.\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"Correct as computed. Note the per-acre figure is highly sensitive to which acreage denominator is used (850, 720 or 580) - fix the basis in the agreement.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-18\\\",\\\"Year 16 PILOT payment\\\",\\\"$392,700\\\",\\\"Applicant letter s.VI.A; Solicitor email\\\",\\n   \\\"$385,000 x 1.02 = $392,700\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"--\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-19\\\",\\\"Total estimated annual tax revenue\\\",\\\"$427,000\\\",\\\"D&O Finding 18\\\",\\n   \\\"$385,000 + $42,000 = $427,000\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"Arithmetic correct; composition overstated as Township revenue (I-24).\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-20\\\",\\\"Construction cost split\\\",\\\"$200 million\\\",\\\"D&O Finding 16\\\",\\n   \\\"$168M + $32M = $200M\\\",\\\"Match\\\",\\\"--\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-21\\\",\\\"Hearing record to written decision\\\",\\\"Decision 6 December 2024\\\",\\\"D&O s.I paras 7(b)-(c) and 10\\\",\\n   \\\"Last hearing concluded 9 October 2024; written Decision 6 December 2024 = 58 days. Board vote 18 November 2024 = 40 days after the hearing and 18 days before the written decision.\\\",\\\"Flag\\\",\\\"Whether the MPC's decision deadline runs to the vote or to the written decision should be confirmed against the record (see risk R-18). If a written time extension was granted, it should be in the file.\\\")\\nrc(\\\"RC-22\\\",\\\"Panel coverage relative to prime farmland\\\",\\\"580 acres of panels; 623 acres prime farmland\\\",\\\"D&O s.II; Finding 14\\\",\\n   \\\"580 / 623 = 93.1%, so at most 43 acres of prime farmland would remain unpaneled if all panels sat on prime soils.\\\",\\\"Flag\\\",\\\"Implausible as a design fact; suggests the two figures are drawn from different datasets. Supports the request for the NRCS/Conservation District acreage confirmation required by s.(f)(6).\\\")\\nPYEOF\\necho ok\"}", "result": "ok\n"}
{"turn": 12, "role": "assistant", "text": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_6iJKYdA0aN1mxeiMiZN11842", "name": "bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cat > $WORKSPACE_DIR/build/content_gaps.py <<'PYEOF'\\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\\n\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# PART 3 - ORDINANCE REQUIREMENTS NOT CARRIED INTO THE DECISION\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\nGAPS = []\\ndef gap(gid, oreq, cond, effect, action, pr):\\n    GAPS.append(dict(id=gid, oreq=oreq, cond=cond, effect=effect, action=action, pr=pr))\\n\\ngap(\\\"G-01\\\",\\\"Gen-tie line and other off-site infrastructure are outside the project site and require separate zoning, land development, subdivision and permitting approvals in every municipality traversed, with easements secured: s.(a) 'Gen-Tie Line'; s.(k)(2).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 4 states only that the approval does not extend outside the site boundary. No condition requires easements or approvals for the 2.3-mile line.\\\",\\n    \\\"Construction cannot be energised without the gen-tie line, yet nothing in the approval makes its approvals a milestone. The Planning Commission expressly flagged this (s.III.F) and it was not carried into a condition.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add a condition requiring recorded easements and copies of all municipal/PennDOT/railroad crossing approvals for the gen-tie line before energisation, and a progress reporting obligation.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-02\\\",\\\"150 ft setback from public road rights-of-way applies to ALL components: Table 27-605-1 ('Setback from Public Road Right-of-Way - All Components').\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 5(b) applies the ROW setback to panels only. Conditions 6 and 12 impose no ROW setback for substations, conversion stations or BESS.\\\",\\n    \\\"A BESS compound or substation could be sited inside the 150 ft ROW setback without breaching any condition. Particularly sensitive where the approval relies on screening from the roadway.\\\",\\n    \\\"Impose the 150 ft ROW setback expressly on all components in Conditions 5, 6 and 12.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-03\\\",\\\"500 ft setback from occupied dwellings on non-participating parcels for substations, inverters and power conversion stations: Table 27-605-1.\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 6 imposes only a 250 ft property-line setback and says nothing about dwellings.\\\",\\n    \\\"A substation or conversion station could sit 250 ft from a residence in compliance with the conditions and in breach of the Ordinance (I-06). The applicant's own accepted-conditions summary states 250 ft, which suggests the design basis may be wrong.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the 500 ft dwelling setback to Condition 6 and require the site plan to dimension it for every substation and conversion station.\\\",\\\"Critical\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-04\\\",\\\"Maximum fencing height of 8 ft: Table 27-605-1.\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 15 sets a floor ('at least seven (7) feet') with no ceiling.\\\",\\n    \\\"An 8 ft+ fence would satisfy the condition and breach the table. The visual-impact concerns that drove the screening conditions apply equally to fence height.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the 8 ft maximum to Condition 15.\\\",\\\"Medium\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-05\\\",\\\"Fencing must be topped with angled extension arms, and barbed wire, razor wire and electrified fencing are expressly prohibited: s.(e)(2).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 15 is silent on both the deterrent topping and the prohibition.\\\",\\n    \\\"A chain-link fence with barbed wire outriggers would comply with the conditions and breach the Ordinance - and would be the single most visible feature to neighbours.\\\",\\n    \\\"Restate s.(e)(2) in full as an approval criterion for the fence design submission.\\\",\\\"Medium\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-06\\\",\\\"Fence to be set back at least 10 ft from the interior edge of the vegetative buffer, for maintenance access: s.(e)(4).\\\",\\n    \\\"Not addressed.\\\",\\n    \\\"Practical consequence for buffer maintenance and for the s.(d)(2) requirement that the buffer sit inside the site boundary. Creates dispute risk about who maintains what strip.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the 10 ft separation to Conditions 15 and 16, and require it to be dimensioned on the landscape plan.\\\",\\\"Medium\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-07\\\",\\\"Vehicular gates at each access point, locked when unattended, with a lock box for emergency responders: s.(e)(5).\\\",\\n    \\\"Not addressed.\\\",\\n    \\\"Emergency access to a 720-acre energised site depends on this. The Emergency Response Plan (Condition 33) does not substitute for a physical access requirement.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add a gate and lock-box condition, with the fire company's keying protocol confirmed in writing.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-08\\\",\\\"'No Trespassing' and 'Danger - High Voltage' signage at intervals not exceeding 250 ft and at all gates, OSHA and NEC compliant: s.(e)(6).\\\",\\n    \\\"Not addressed.\\\",\\n    \\\"Liability and life-safety exposure on an energised site; also a standard inspection item under s.(l)(2).\\\",\\n    \\\"Add a signage condition mirroring s.(e)(6).\\\",\\\"Medium\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-09\\\",\\\"No panels, BESS, permanent roads or structures in any wetland, watercourse, floodplain or riparian buffer; wetland delineation and floodplain analysis required where potentially present: s.(f)(5).\\\",\\n    \\\"The Decision contains no finding of fact on wetlands, watercourses, floodplains or riparian buffers, and no condition. The Phase I ESA (Finding 11) does not address wetlands.\\\",\\n    \\\"This is the largest single evidential hole in the record. An 850-acre site in Lancaster County will contain wet soils, drainage swales and at least some mapped floodplain; the Ordinance prohibits structures there absolutely, and a s.404 permit may be required. Discovery of a jurisdictional wetland after permits issue would force a redesign with no amendment procedure agreed.\\\",\\n    \\\"Commission a wetland delineation and floodplain analysis before the final stormwater submission (they share the same survey base) and add a prohibition condition plus a notification obligation if wetlands are identified.\\\",\\\"Critical\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-10\\\",\\\"Type C buffer along every qualifying boundary, minimum 30 ft wide, located entirely within the site: s.(d)(1)-(2).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 16 limits the buffer to the northern and eastern boundaries 'adjacent to non-participating properties with occupied residences', and imposes no width.\\\",\\n    \\\"Narrower than the Ordinance in extent (no road frontages, no west or south boundaries) and in width (I-08). Cannot be cured by condition; the Ordinance standard survives.\\\",\\n    \\\"Restate s.(d)(1)-(2) as the standard, and require the landscape plan to show a dimensioned 30 ft width on every qualifying frontage.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-11\\\",\\\"At least 60% evergreen; species native to south-eastern Pennsylvania per DCNR; no species on the Pennsylvania noxious weed list: s.(d)(3) and (d)(6).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 16(a) requires native stock 'or, in the absence of a Township-approved list, species commonly accepted for screening purposes in Lancaster County'.\\\",\\n    \\\"The fallback clause displaces the Ordinance's native-species mandate and the noxious-weed prohibition, and there is no evergreen-minimum at all. Winter opacity is the whole point of the requirement.\\\",\\n    \\\"Adopt the DCNR list by reference, delete the 'commonly accepted' fallback, and add the 60% evergreen minimum.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-12\\\",\\\"Buffer to achieve 75% visual opacity within 5 years, determined by the Township Engineer or a qualified landscape architect: s.(d)(4) and the Type C definition.\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 16 contains no opacity standard and no date by which screening must be effective.\\\",\\n    \\\"This is the performance standard that the neighbours, the county and the dissenting Supervisors all relied on, and it is absent from the operative conditions. Condition 16 requires only that 4 ft plantings reach 12-15 ft - a growth expectation, not a measured opacity outcome.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the 75% / 5-year opacity standard with the Engineer or landscape architect determination mechanism.\\\",\\\"Critical\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-13\\\",\\\"Landscaping plan to be prepared by a licensed landscape architect registered in Pennsylvania and submitted with the application: s.(d)(5).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 16 requires a 'detailed screening plan' to the Township but specifies no professional qualification and no submission timing.\\\",\\n    \\\"The plan is not in the record and may be prepared by a contractor rather than a licensed professional. Note the parallel defect on the site plan itself, which s.(b)(2) requires to be sealed by a PE or licensed surveyor.\\\",\\n    \\\"Require a sealed landscape plan before any planting, and confirm the seal on the Site Plan.\\\",\\\"Medium\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-14\\\",\\\"Minimum of two access points, each with a minimum 20 ft driveway width, sight distance to PennDOT standards, Engineer approval: s.(h)(3).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 7 describes one primary construction access point on Hershey Mill Road and 16 ft internal roads.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance non-compliance and a fire-access deficiency on a 720-acre site (I-09).\\\",\\n    \\\"Require a conforming access plan, identifying the second access point, with sight-distance analysis and PennDOT highway occupancy permit if the road is state-maintained.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-15\\\",\\\"Operational phase limited to an average of 10 vehicle trips per day on a monthly basis, with a log available to the Township: s.(h)(4).\\\",\\n    \\\"No condition; Condition 32's annual report does not require trip data.\\\",\\n    \\\"The applicant forecasts 2-5 trips per day, comfortably inside the cap, so the omission costs nothing now but leaves the standard unenforceable later.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the cap and the log requirement to Conditions 31/32.\\\",\\\"Low\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-16\\\",\\\"Road maintenance agreement with the Township in a form satisfactory to the Solicitor: s.(h)(2).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 25 requires a $500,000 bond; no agreement is required. The Planning Commission also recommended an agreement.\\\",\\n    \\\"The agreement is the instrument that fixes repair obligations, load limits, routing and scheduling. A bond without an agreement leaves the Township's remedy undefined.\\\",\\n    \\\"Require an executed road maintenance agreement before construction, alongside the bond.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-17\\\",\\\"No interference with radio, television, telephone, cellular or emergency communications; complaints investigated and resolved within 30 days: s.(g)(3).\\\",\\n    \\\"Not addressed.\\\",\\n    \\\"Modest but real risk: inverters and the gen-tie line can affect reception. Without a condition, the Township's only route is the general nuisance/enforcement power.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the interference standard and a complaint-response obligation.\\\",\\\"Low\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-18\\\",\\\"Dust suppression during construction; stabilisation of disturbed areas within 30 days of final grading of each phase; native and pollinator-friendly seed mixes: s.(g)(5).\\\",\\n    \\\"Not addressed, although the approved Decommissioning Plan contemplates revegetation.\\\",\\n    \\\"Dust is the most frequent construction-phase complaint on projects of this size. The Engineer's and the applicant's documents both reference E&S controls but neither picks up the 30-day stabilisation standard or the seed-mix requirement.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the s.(g)(5) standards, linked to the approved E&S plan.\\\",\\\"Medium\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-19\\\",\\\"Annual site inspection by the Engineer, Zoning Officer or Board designee on 10 business days' notice, at the applicant's cost: s.(l)(2).\\\",\\n    \\\"No inspection right is conferred by the Decision.\\\",\\n    \\\"The Township's ability to verify screening, fencing, noise and maintenance compliance depends on this right. Without it, enforcement rests on s.(l)(3) alone, which is also not restated (G-20).\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the inspection right and the cost-recovery term.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-20\\\",\\\"Enforcement: written notice of violation, opportunity to cure not exceeding 30 days, then revocation proceedings before the Board: s.(l)(3).\\\",\\n    \\\"The Decision recites no enforcement or cure mechanism.\\\",\\n    \\\"The Ordinance standard applies regardless, but an applicant facing an unannounced revocation action would have a strong procedural objection. Restating it protects the Township.\\\",\\n    \\\"Restate s.(l)(3), including the imminent-threat exception.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-21\\\",\\\"Reporting of any material change to the facility, its ownership or its operations: s.(l)(1)(v).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 32's content list omits ownership and material-change reporting.\\\",\\n    \\\"The Township will not learn of a transfer of the project company, a capacity change or a repowering until it happens - which also bears on G-22.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the ownership and material-change disclosure to Condition 32.\\\",\\\"Medium\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-22\\\",\\\"Approval personal to the applicant; transfer or assignment only with prior Board consent, with a capacity showing by the transferee: s.(k)(4).\\\",\\n    \\\"Not addressed.\\\",\\n    \\\"The approval and all its financial assurances could be assigned without Township review of the successor's capacity. This is a standard protection to lose.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add an assignment condition tracking s.(k)(4).\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-23\\\",\\\"Approval expires if a building permit is not obtained within 24 months of the Decision, extendable once by up to 12 months on written request filed before expiry showing good cause: s.(k)(5).\\\",\\n    \\\"Neither the Decision nor any condition states the expiry date.\\\",\\n    \\\"The date is 6 December 2026 (outer limit 6 December 2027) and it is not administratively tracked anywhere. The stated construction start of 1 April 2026 leaves roughly nine months of cushion, almost all of it consumed by the pre-permit conditions in Parts 1 and 2 (RC-13).\\\",\\n    \\\"Calendar the expiry, confirm the extension procedure in writing, and consider requiring the applicant to report quarterly on pre-permit milestones.\\\",\\\"Critical\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-24\\\",\\\"PILOT agreement to be executed AND recorded in the Lancaster County Recorder of Deeds before the first building permit, where required by the Board: s.(j)(2).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 30 requires execution 'in a form satisfactory to the Township Solicitor' but says nothing about recording.\\\",\\n    \\\"Recording is what gives the Township's revenue interest notice effect against successors and lenders - and it matters most in the assignment scenario the approval also fails to control (G-22).\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the recording requirement to Condition 30.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-25\\\",\\\"Stormwater plan to demonstrate post-development runoff not exceeding pre-development for the 2, 10, 25, 50 and 100-year design storms: s.(f)(1).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 10 incorporates the Ordinance by reference but does not restate the design-storm performance standard.\\\",\\n    \\\"Low practical risk because the Ordinance applies anyway, but the Engineer's review criteria should be stated so that the 6 March 2025 submission can be tested against them.\\\",\\n    \\\"Restate the design storms and the pre/post standard as review criteria in Condition 10.\\\",\\\"Low\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-26\\\",\\\"Surety bond issuer to carry an A.M. Best rating of A- VII or better; escrow to name the Township as beneficiary: s.(i)(4).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 22 requires a surety 'authorized to transact business in the Commonwealth' with no rating floor, and an escrow at a rated institution with no beneficiary term.\\\",\\n    \\\"The Ordinance's credit-quality screen for the $7.875 million instrument is dropped. Given the applicant has nominated 'Northbrook Surety Company' (Finding 24) with no commitment letter, the rating screen is the Township's main protection.\\\",\\n    \\\"Restate s.(i)(4)(ii) and (iii) in Condition 22 and require rating evidence with the instrument.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-27\\\",\\\"Decommissioning to be completed within 12 months of the 12-month cessation trigger, with an absolute 24-month cap: s.(i)(7).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 21 allows 18 months; Condition 24 creates a separate abandonment mechanism with a 90-day contest window.\\\",\\n    \\\"Two inconsistent regimes (I-12, I-18). The 24-month cap - the most important backstop - appears in neither instrument.\\\",\\n    \\\"Restate s.(i)(7) verbatim and delete the 18-month figure.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-28\\\",\\\"General standard s.(a)-(e) of s.27-602, in particular (c) 'shall not adversely affect the character, property values, or orderly development of the surrounding neighborhood' and (e) compliance with all other regulations.\\\",\\n    \\\"Conclusions 2, 4 and 6 address these in conclusory terms; there are no discrete findings on s.27-602(c) beyond the property value assessment (see I-15), and no findings on wetlands/floodplain compliance (G-09) or on the full text of s.(B)(15).\\\",\\n    \\\"The general standards are the legal hinge for the approval. Conclusory treatment invites challenge where an underlying study is thin or a standard is unaddressed.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add discrete findings against each of s.27-602(a)-(e), and qualify Conclusions 2 and 8 as 'subject to the conditions herein'.\\\",\\\"Medium\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-29\\\",\\\"Two access points, sight distance and driveway width are also fire-access requirements; s.(e)(5) lock boxes and Condition 33 emergency planning presuppose vehicular access to all energised areas.\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 7 addresses internal road width and turning movements for construction vehicles only.\\\",\\n    \\\"The Fire Company has not been asked to comment on access, turning radii or water supply for a BESS fire. Condition 33 only requires a plan 30 days before COD.\\\",\\n    \\\"Obtain written fire company comment on the access and BESS layout before permit issuance, and record it in the file.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\n\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# RISK REGISTER\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\nRISKS = []\\ndef risk(rid, desc, linked, lik, imp, rating, exposure, mit, owner):\\n    RISKS.append(dict(id=rid, desc=desc, linked=linked, lik=lik, imp=imp, rating=rating,\\n                      exposure=exposure, mit=mit, owner=owner))\\n\\nrisk(\\\"R-01\\\",\\\"Approval rests on a lot-coverage computation that, on the Ordinance's own treatment of panels as semi-impervious, exceeds the 75% ceiling by 40 acres.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-01, RC-10, A-11\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\n     \\\"Legal (Ordinance non-compliance embedded in the approval); design (possible 40-acre layout reduction); schedule.\\\",\\n     \\\"Obtain the impervious/semi-impervious area schedule and a written Engineer determination of how panels are treated. If panels count, either reduce coverage or record the interpretation in an amendment with notice.\\\",\\\"Township Engineer / Zoning Officer\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-02\\\",\\\"Agricultural mitigation is understated: 3 acres by arithmetic, plus an unknown acreage of farmland of statewide importance that the Ordinance requires to be included.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-02, RC-04, RC-05, B-10\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Financial (up to approx. $272,400); legal (s.(f)(6) requires Conservation District confirmation, which is absent).\\\",\\n     \\\"Obtain NRCS soils mapping and Conservation District written confirmation of the qualifying acreage; correct Condition 28 by amendment; true-up any shortfall.\\\",\\\"Zoning Officer / Solicitor\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-03\\\",\\\"The Township's own PILOT negotiating position (2% escalator from Year 11) contradicts Condition 30 and Finding 17, which fix flat $385,000 payments for Years 1-15.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-16, RC-15, D-06\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\n     \\\"Legal (side agreement departing from the decision); financial (approx. $118,627 over Years 1-15; approx. $3.1M over 30 years).\\\",\\n     \\\"Decide the position: either amend Condition 30 under s.(k)(3) with the proper procedure, or accept Year 16 and pursue value elsewhere (e.g. the MFN clause). Do not paper over it in the agreement.\\\",\\\"Township Solicitor / Board\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-04\\\",\\\"Most-favoured-nation clause unresolved; the applicant has rejected it and the Township treats it as material.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-16, D-06\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Schedule (Condition 30 gates permit issuance); financial (rate protection foregone if dropped).\\\",\\n     \\\"Consider the Solicitor's fallback options (periodic rate review or a floor-and-cap) and take a Board decision on a documented negotiating mandate.\\\",\\\"Township Solicitor / Board\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-05\\\",\\\"The 6 March 2025 stormwater deadline slips because Conservation District review (60-90 days per the Township Engineer) had not visibly started by 10 January 2025.\\\",\\n     \\\"B-05, B-06, RC-11\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Schedule (final plan cannot be approved; no building or grading permit can issue); credibility.\\\",\\n     \\\"Confirm the CD submission date immediately. If it cannot be met, document the position and consider a Board-approved extension - do not let the date pass silently. Note the Decision attaches no consequence to a missed deadline, which is itself a drafting weakness.\\\",\\\"Applicant / Township Engineer\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-06\\\",\\\"The conditional use approval expires on 6 December 2026 if no building permit has issued, and the expiry is not recorded in either approval instrument.\\\",\\n     \\\"G-23, RC-13, F-03\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Legal (loss of the entitlement); financial (approx. $200M project); political.\\\",\\n     \\\"Calendar the date; require quarterly pre-permit milestone reporting; ensure any extension request is filed in writing before 6 December 2026 (a Sunday) with a good-cause showing.\\\",\\\"Zoning Officer / Solicitor\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-07\\\",\\\"PHMC consultation remains outstanding and gates every grading permit for the project.\\\",\\n     \\\"B-03, I-12\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Schedule (no grading until resolved); the Stoltzfus Farmstead sits at the 200 ft trigger distance.\\\",\\n     \\\"Route the consultation under the Pennsylvania History Code (37 Pa. C.S. ss.101 et seq.), not s.106; confirm the 200 ft measurement against the surveyed boundary; set a date for the PHMC submission.\\\",\\\"Applicant / Ridgepoint\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-08\\\",\\\"Bog turtle Phase 2 (and possibly Phase 3) surveys remain outstanding on Parcels -003 and -004, and habitat surveys are seasonally constrained.\\\",\\n     \\\"B-04\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Schedule (a missed survey window can cost a full season); scope (avoidance buffers if habitat is confirmed).\\\",\\n     \\\"Confirm survey scoping with USFWS now and calendar the seasonal window; consider sequencing grading on other parcels first, noting Condition 8 is parcel-specific.\\\",\\\"Applicant / Ridgepoint\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-09\\\",\\\"Wetlands, watercourses and floodplain have not been evaluated anywhere in the record, although the Ordinance prohibits structures in them.\\\",\\n     \\\"G-09, B-09\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Legal (absolute prohibition); design (layout may not be buildable as approved); permitting (possible s.404).\\\",\\n     \\\"Commission a wetland delineation and floodplain analysis on the same survey base as the stormwater submission; add a notification and amendment mechanism.\\\",\\\"Applicant / Township Engineer\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-10\\\",\\\"The 500 ft substation-to-dwelling setback is absent from the conditions and the applicant's accepted-conditions summary states 250 ft.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-06, G-03\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Legal (Ordinance non-compliance); layout (relocation of substations if the design is at 250 ft).\\\",\\n     \\\"Check the site plan dimension for every substation and conversion station today. If short of 500 ft, the layout must change before permits; if compliant, condition it expressly.\\\",\\\"Township Engineer\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-11\\\",\\\"Only one access point is conditioned, against a two-access-point Ordinance standard, on a 720-acre energised site.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-09, G-14\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Life safety (fire apparatus access); Ordinance non-compliance; insurability.\\\",\\n     \\\"Require the second access point on the plan, obtain fire company sign-off, and confirm whether Hershey Mill Road is state or Township maintained (PennDOT HOP may be required).\\\",\\\"Township Engineer / Fire Company\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-12\\\",\\\"Screening will not achieve the Ordinance's 75% opacity in 5 years because the standard is not in the conditions and the planting palette has no evergreen floor.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-08, G-10, G-11, G-12\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Political/community (the issue the dissent and the neighbours pressed); legal (Ordinance standard survives the condition).\\\",\\n     \\\"Restate the Ordinance standard; require a sealed landscape plan with a species schedule showing at least 60% evergreen; plant at energisation as the Engineer recommends (email of 10 January 2025).\\\",\\\"Township Engineer\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-13\\\",\\\"Decommissioning is governed by three inconsistent timetables (Ordinance 12/24 months; Condition 21 18 months; Condition 24 90-day contest).\\\",\\n     \\\"I-12, I-18, D-01, D-05, G-27\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Financial (the $7.875M instrument may not be drawable when needed); legal (unenforceable conditions).\\\",\\n     \\\"Restate s.(i)(7)-(8) verbatim; delete the 18-month figure; delete or subordinate Condition 24 to the Ordinance mechanism.\\\",\\\"Solicitor\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-14\\\",\\\"Financial assurances can, on the face of Condition 34, be posted after the first building permit.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-18, D-08\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Financial (unsecured decommissioning and road obligations); legal (condition would be void to the extent it conflicts with s.(i)(5)).\\\",\\n     \\\"Amend Condition 34 so that 'no later than 60 days before construction' operates as an outer limit and never later than the first permit.\\\",\\\"Solicitor\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-15\\\",\\\"The noise compliance standard is disputed (ANSI S12.9 Part 2 vs Part 3) and the study's authorship is unresolved.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-07, I-20, B-13a\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Enforcement (a future compliance test may be contested on methodology); record integrity.\\\",\\n     \\\"Amend Condition 18 to the Part 2 method the applicant accepted (or obtain written confirmation that Part 3 is accepted); correct Finding 20 or produce the acoustical firm's retainer and report.\\\",\\\"Solicitor / Township Engineer\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-16\\\",\\\"The glare analysis, a mandatory application item, was not before the Board and is now deferred to the pre-permit stage with no consequence for an adverse result.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-13, B-14\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Legal (validity challenge premised on an incomplete record); schedule (a roadway-glare finding could force panel re-orientation or screening).\\\",\\n     \\\"Require the study to be submitted with a mitigation plan subject to Board approval where impacts are identified (mirroring s.(g)(2)); obtain it early, before panel procurement.\\\",\\\"Applicant / Township Engineer\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-17\\\",\\\"PILOT revenue is characterised as $427,000 of Township income when only the Township's millage share of the $42,000 accrues to it, and the underlying exemption basis for a PILOT is undocumented.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-24, D-06, D-07\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Financial (revenue projections); legal (s.(j)(1) authorisation is premised on the applicant seeking an exemption or alternative assessment).\\\",\\n     \\\"Obtain the applicant's written position on assessment/exemption and confirm with the County Board of Assessment Appeals; restate the revenue summary on a by-taxing-body basis.\\\",\\\"Solicitor / Finance\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-18\\\",\\\"The written Decision issued 58 days after the last hearing concluded, and the file contains no written time extension.\\\",\\n     \\\"RC-21, F-02\\\",\\\"Low\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n     \\\"Legal (if the statutory decision deadline was missed, the approval itself is exposed).\\\",\\n     \\\"Confirm the applicable MPC deadline and whether the vote (18 November 2024, 40 days) satisfied it; locate any written extension of time; record the conclusion. The appeal window has run, which materially reduces but does not eliminate the exposure.\\\",\\\"Solicitor\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-19\\\",\\\"Two docket numbers and two parcel schedules exist for the same application, and the ordinance excerpt is uncertified.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-04, I-21, I-34\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Record integrity (every downstream document - notices, service, appeals, the county file - may reference the wrong number); analysis risk (Part 3 depends on the excerpt).\\\",\\n     \\\"Reconcile the docket number with the county; obtain recorded deed acreages; obtain a certified copy of the codified ordinance and re-run Part 3 against it.\\\",\\\"Zoning Officer / Solicitor\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-20\\\",\\\"Two Ordinance requirements are unenforceable as conditioned and one condition grants the Township Engineer approval authority the Ordinance reserves to the Board.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-28, I-29, F-05\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n     \\\"Legal (administrative approvals open to challenge by neighbours with standing); design drift.\\\",\\n     \\\"Tighten Condition 35 to exclude any change to a Table 27-605-1 standard or an approved plan dimension; add the fence height and material standards.\\\",\\\"Solicitor\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-21\\\",\\\"The BESS is labelled 'optional' but is granted, costed, bonded and taxed as part of the project.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-30\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n     \\\"Financial (security and PILOT computed on a project including BESS); enforcement (BESS conditions unenforceable if not built).\\\",\\n     \\\"Confirm whether the BESS will be built. If not certain, add a mechanism to recalculate the decommissioning estimate, security and PILOT on final as-built scope.\\\",\\\"Solicitor\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-22\\\",\\\"Operational compliance is not measurable: no trip log, no EMI complaint procedure, no dust or stabilisation standard, no inspection right.\\\",\\n     \\\"G-15, G-17, G-18, G-19\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\n     \\\"Enforcement (nothing to measure against); community relations.\\\",\\n     \\\"Adopt the standard Ordinance language in an amendment or, at minimum, record it as administrative guidance in the first annual report cycle.\\\",\\\"Zoning Officer\\\")\\n\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# OPEN INFORMATION REQUESTS\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\nOPEN = []\\ndef op(oid, doc, why, frm, by, pr):\\n    OPEN.append(dict(id=oid, doc=doc, why=why, frm=frm, by=by, pr=pr))\\n\\nop(\\\"D-01\\\",\\\"Certified copy of the codified Zoning Ordinance, including the full text of s.27-605(B)(15) and Articles VI-VII in their entirety.\\\",\\n   \\\"All of Part 1 and Part 3 rests on an uncertified, undated excerpt that disclaims controlling effect and names a different Township Secretary from the one who attested the Decision (I-34).\\\",\\\"Township Secretary\\\",\\\"Immediate\\\",\\\"Critical\\\")\\nop(\\\"D-02\\\",\\\"The approved Site Plan dated 1 September 2024 (Exhibit A-1), sealed, with the impervious/semi-impervious area schedule and all dimension strings.\\\",\\n   \\\"Needed to test six setback standards, the 45 ft substation height, the 75% lot coverage ceiling and the lease/parcel boundaries (A-01, A-11, I-01, I-33).\\\",\\\"Applicant / Township Engineer\\\",\\\"Immediate\\\",\\\"Critical\\\")\\nop(\\\"D-03\\\",\\\"Recorded deed acreages and the executed leases or recorded memoranda for all seven parcels, plus the Commonwealth Title & Escrow verification.\\\",\\n   \\\"The two parcel schedules disagree parcel-by-parcel (I-04); Condition 3 requires evidence of recording and the applicant's letter says the leases are in escrow (A-03).\\\",\\\"Applicant / title company\\\",\\\"Immediate\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\nop(\\\"D-04\\\",\\\"The property value impact assessment (Clarendon Appraisal Group, 25 September 2024) complete, with its scope and paired-sales dataset.\\\",\\n   \\\"Finding 19 is unqualified but the applicant describes the finding as covering residences 'beyond 500 feet', while the Ordinance requires evaluation within 1,000 ft (I-15).\\\",\\\"Township / Clarendon\\\",\\\"Immediate\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\nop(\\\"D-05\\\",\\\"The noise impact study in full, with the cover and signature pages, the receptor schedule and the ANSI methodology statement.\\\",\\n   \\\"Two different firms are credited (I-20) and two different ANSI parts are cited (I-07).\\\",\\\"Applicant\\\",\\\"Immediate\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\nop(\\\"D-06\\\",\\\"The cultural resources survey and all PHMC correspondence.\\\",\\n   \\\"Condition 9 gates every grading permit; Finding 12 concedes consultation is incomplete (B-03).\\\",\\\"Applicant / Ridgepoint\\\",\\\"Immediate\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\nop(\\\"D-07\\\",\\\"All PNDI correspondence and the bog turtle survey scoping with USFWS and PFBC.\\\",\\n   \\\"Condition 8 gates land disturbance on Parcels -003 and -004 and is seasonally constrained (B-04).\\\",\\\"Applicant / Ridgepoint\\\",\\\"Immediate\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\nop(\\\"D-08\\\",\\\"Wetland delineation and floodplain analysis for the site, or a written professional opinion that none is required.\\\",\\n   \\\"No finding or condition addresses s.(f)(5), which prohibits structures in wetlands and floodplains (G-09).\\\",\\\"Applicant\\\",\\\"Before final stormwater submission\\\",\\\"Critical\\\")\\nop(\\\"D-09\\\",\\\"NRCS soils mapping and the Conservation District's written confirmation of the prime and statewide-importance acreage.\\\",\\n   \\\"s.(f)(6) requires CD confirmation and the mitigation acreage is disputed between 620 and 623 acres, with statewide-importance land unquantified (I-02, RC-05).\\\",\\\"Applicant / Conservation District\\\",\\\"Before building permit\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\nop(\\\"D-10\\\",\\\"The Township Engineer's review letters (30 September 2024 dimensional review and 1 October 2024 stormwater comments) and any traffic impact study in the application record.\\\",\\n   \\\"The Decision relies on both letters and on a traffic impact study; neither is in the file (A-01, A-19).\\\",\\\"Township Engineer\\\",\\\"Immediate\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\nop(\\\"D-11\\\",\\\"The written confirmation that Hershey Mill Road is a state or Township road, with the corresponding access permit status.\\\",\\n   \\\"The Engineer's email of 10 January 2025 asks the question; the answer governs whether a PennDOT highway occupancy permit is required (G-14).\\\",\\\"Township Engineer\\\",\\\"Before access design\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\nop(\\\"D-12\\\",\\\"Full text of s.27-605(B)(15) and confirmation of which performance standards apply to the accessory BESS.\\\",\\n   \\\"The excerpt contains only subsections (c)(3)-(4); s.(B)(14)(a) requires all s.(B)(15) standards to be met 'in full' (I-30, C-02).\\\",\\\"Township Secretary\\\",\\\"Immediate\\\",\\\"Critical\\\")\\nop(\\\"D-13\\\",\\\"The applicant's written position on any real property tax exemption, abatement, credit or alternative assessment affecting the facility or BESS.\\\",\\n   \\\"s.(j)(1) authorises a PILOT only where such relief is sought; the record is silent (I-24).\\\",\\\"Applicant / Solicitor\\\",\\\"Before PILOT execution\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\nop(\\\"D-14\\\",\\\"Any written extension of the decision deadline or record-closing order for the 9 October 2024 hearing.\\\",\\n   \\\"The written Decision issued 58 days after the hearing concluded (RC-21).\\\",\\\"Township Secretary / hearing officer\\\",\\\"Immediate\\\",\\\"Medium\\\")\\n\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# ACTION PLAN\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\nACTIONS = []\\ndef act(aid, action, owner, when, pr, links):\\n    ACTIONS.append(dict(id=aid, action=action, owner=owner, when=when, pr=pr, links=links))\\n\\nact(\\\"P-01\\\",\\\"Issue a document request to the applicant for the site plan, noise and glare studies, property value report, cultural resources and PNDI correspondence (D-02 to D-09).\\\",\\n    \\\"Zoning Officer\\\",\\\"Within 7 days\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\\"D-02 - D-09\\\")\\nact(\\\"P-02\\\",\\\"Obtain a certified copy of the codified Ordinance and re-run Part 3 of this matrix against it, including the full text of s.(B)(15).\\\",\\n    \\\"Solicitor / Zoning Officer\\\",\\\"Within 14 days\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\\"I-34, D-01, D-12\\\")\\nact(\\\"P-03\\\",\\\"Determine the treatment of panel area for the 75% lot coverage ceiling, in writing. If panels count, begin the amendment/redesign conversation immediately.\\\",\\n    \\\"Township Engineer\\\",\\\"Within 14 days\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\\"I-01, RC-10, R-01\\\")\\nact(\\\"P-04\\\",\\\"Reconcile the docket number (CU-2024-006 vs CU-2024-012) with the county and correct the record.\\\",\\n    \\\"Zoning Officer\\\",\\\"Within 14 days\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"I-21, R-19\\\")\\nact(\\\"P-05\\\",\\\"Reconcile the two parcel schedules against the recorded deeds; confirm the lease terms for Parcels -003 and -004.\\\",\\n    \\\"Zoning Officer / title company\\\",\\\"Within 21 days\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"I-04, I-05\\\")\\nact(\\\"P-06\\\",\\\"Confirm the stormwater submission date to the Conservation District. If 6 March 2025 is not achievable, seek a documented Board extension before the date passes.\\\",\\n    \\\"Township Engineer\\\",\\\"Within 7 days\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\\"B-05, B-06, R-05\\\")\\nact(\\\"P-07\\\",\\\"Take a Board decision on the PILOT escalator: amend Condition 30 or accept Year 16. Do not resolve it by side letter.\\\",\\n    \\\"Solicitor / Board\\\",\\\"At next Board meeting\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\\"I-16, RC-15, R-03\\\")\\nact(\\\"P-08\\\",\\\"Take a Board decision on the MFN clause and give the Solicitor a documented negotiating mandate, including the fallback positions.\\\",\\n    \\\"Board\\\",\\\"At next Board meeting\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"R-04\\\")\\nact(\\\"P-09\\\",\\\"Check the site plan dimension for the 500 ft substation/inverter/reference dwelling setback and the 300 ft BESS property-line setback.\\\",\\n    \\\"Township Engineer\\\",\\\"Within 14 days\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\\"I-06, I-11, G-03, R-10\\\")\\nact(\\\"P-10\\\",\\\"Require a wetland delineation and floodplain analysis before the final stormwater plan is accepted.\\\",\\n    \\\"Township Engineer\\\",\\\"Before 6 March 2025\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\\"G-09, R-09\\\")\\nact(\\\"P-11\\\",\\\"Recalculate the agricultural mitigation on NRCS/CD-confirmed acreage and correct Condition 28 by amendment.\\\",\\n    \\\"Zoning Officer / Solicitor\\\",\\\"Before building permit\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"I-02, RC-04, RC-05, R-02\\\")\\nact(\\\"P-12\\\",\\\"Sequence the pre-permit gates (PHMC, USFWS/PFBC, glare, stormwater, PILOT, decommissioning security, parent guaranty, recorded leases, agricultural payment) into a dated critical path to 6 December 2026.\\\",\\n    \\\"Solicitor / Township Engineer\\\",\\\"Within 30 days\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\\"G-23, RC-13, R-06\\\")\\nact(\\\"P-13\\\",\\\"Consolidate the condition defects identified in Part 3 into a single corrective amendment (R-01 to R-22), so that the Ordinance standards that were omitted or relaxed are restored in one instrument.\\\",\\n    \\\"Solicitor\\\",\\\"Within 60 days\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"Part 3\\\")\\nact(\\\"P-14\\\",\\\"Require the glaring analysis early, with a mitigation plan subject to Board approval where impacts are identified.\\\",\\n    \\\"Township Engineer\\\",\\\"Before panel procurement\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"I-13, B-14, R-16\\\")\\nact(\\\"P-15\\\",\\\"Obtain written fire company comment on access, turning radii and BESS fire-water supply, and confirm the gate and lock-box protocol.\\\",\\n    \\\"Zoning Officer / Fire Company\\\",\\\"Before permit issuance\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"G-07, G-29, R-11\\\")\\nact(\\\"P-16\\\",\\\"Correct the record-level errors: Finding 7 (standards that do not exist), Finding 20 (study author), Finding 16 (job range), the appeal-date recital and the s.609/s.609.1 citation.\\\",\\n    \\\"Solicitor\\\",\\\"Within 30 days\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"I-20, I-22, I-25, I-26, I-32, I-33\\\")\\nact(\\\"P-17\\\",\\\"Establish the annual compliance calendar: 31 March reporting, annual inspection, 5-yearly cost estimate update, annual emergency exercise.\\\",\\n    \\\"Zoning Officer\\\",\\\"Before COD\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"E-02 - E-07, G-19\\\")\\nact(\\\"P-18\\\",\\\"Record the 6 December 2026 expiry in the permit tracking system with an alert at 12 months out.\\\",\\n    \\\"Zoning Officer\\\",\\\"Immediately\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"G-23, R-06\\\")\\nPYEOF\\npython3 -c \\\"\\nimport sys; sys.path.insert(0,'$WORKSPACE_DIR/build')\\nimport content_matrix, content_findings, content_gaps\\nprint('matrix rows', len(content_matrix.MATRIX))\\nprint('inconsistencies', len(content_findings.INCONS))\\nprint('recalcs', len(content_findings.RECALC))\\nprint('gaps', len(content_gaps.GAPS))\\nprint('risks', len(content_gaps.RISKS))\\nprint('open', len(content_gaps.OPEN))\\nprint('actions', len(content_gaps.ACTIONS))\\nfrom collections import Counter\\nprint(Counter(r['status'] for r in content_matrix.MATRIX))\\nprint(Counter(i['sev'] for i in content_findings.INCONS))\\nprint(Counter(g['pr'] for g in content_gaps.GAPS))\\nprint(Counter(r['rating'] for r in content_gaps.RISKS))\\n\\\"\"}"}], "input_tokens": 102797, "output_tokens": 9234, "finish_reason": "completed"}
{"turn": 12, "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "call_00_6iJKYdA0aN1mxeiMiZN11842", "tool_name": "bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cat > $WORKSPACE_DIR/build/content_gaps.py <<'PYEOF'\\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\\n\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# PART 3 - ORDINANCE REQUIREMENTS NOT CARRIED INTO THE DECISION\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\nGAPS = []\\ndef gap(gid, oreq, cond, effect, action, pr):\\n    GAPS.append(dict(id=gid, oreq=oreq, cond=cond, effect=effect, action=action, pr=pr))\\n\\ngap(\\\"G-01\\\",\\\"Gen-tie line and other off-site infrastructure are outside the project site and require separate zoning, land development, subdivision and permitting approvals in every municipality traversed, with easements secured: s.(a) 'Gen-Tie Line'; s.(k)(2).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 4 states only that the approval does not extend outside the site boundary. No condition requires easements or approvals for the 2.3-mile line.\\\",\\n    \\\"Construction cannot be energised without the gen-tie line, yet nothing in the approval makes its approvals a milestone. The Planning Commission expressly flagged this (s.III.F) and it was not carried into a condition.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add a condition requiring recorded easements and copies of all municipal/PennDOT/railroad crossing approvals for the gen-tie line before energisation, and a progress reporting obligation.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-02\\\",\\\"150 ft setback from public road rights-of-way applies to ALL components: Table 27-605-1 ('Setback from Public Road Right-of-Way - All Components').\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 5(b) applies the ROW setback to panels only. Conditions 6 and 12 impose no ROW setback for substations, conversion stations or BESS.\\\",\\n    \\\"A BESS compound or substation could be sited inside the 150 ft ROW setback without breaching any condition. Particularly sensitive where the approval relies on screening from the roadway.\\\",\\n    \\\"Impose the 150 ft ROW setback expressly on all components in Conditions 5, 6 and 12.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-03\\\",\\\"500 ft setback from occupied dwellings on non-participating parcels for substations, inverters and power conversion stations: Table 27-605-1.\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 6 imposes only a 250 ft property-line setback and says nothing about dwellings.\\\",\\n    \\\"A substation or conversion station could sit 250 ft from a residence in compliance with the conditions and in breach of the Ordinance (I-06). The applicant's own accepted-conditions summary states 250 ft, which suggests the design basis may be wrong.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the 500 ft dwelling setback to Condition 6 and require the site plan to dimension it for every substation and conversion station.\\\",\\\"Critical\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-04\\\",\\\"Maximum fencing height of 8 ft: Table 27-605-1.\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 15 sets a floor ('at least seven (7) feet') with no ceiling.\\\",\\n    \\\"An 8 ft+ fence would satisfy the condition and breach the table. The visual-impact concerns that drove the screening conditions apply equally to fence height.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the 8 ft maximum to Condition 15.\\\",\\\"Medium\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-05\\\",\\\"Fencing must be topped with angled extension arms, and barbed wire, razor wire and electrified fencing are expressly prohibited: s.(e)(2).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 15 is silent on both the deterrent topping and the prohibition.\\\",\\n    \\\"A chain-link fence with barbed wire outriggers would comply with the conditions and breach the Ordinance - and would be the single most visible feature to neighbours.\\\",\\n    \\\"Restate s.(e)(2) in full as an approval criterion for the fence design submission.\\\",\\\"Medium\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-06\\\",\\\"Fence to be set back at least 10 ft from the interior edge of the vegetative buffer, for maintenance access: s.(e)(4).\\\",\\n    \\\"Not addressed.\\\",\\n    \\\"Practical consequence for buffer maintenance and for the s.(d)(2) requirement that the buffer sit inside the site boundary. Creates dispute risk about who maintains what strip.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the 10 ft separation to Conditions 15 and 16, and require it to be dimensioned on the landscape plan.\\\",\\\"Medium\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-07\\\",\\\"Vehicular gates at each access point, locked when unattended, with a lock box for emergency responders: s.(e)(5).\\\",\\n    \\\"Not addressed.\\\",\\n    \\\"Emergency access to a 720-acre energised site depends on this. The Emergency Response Plan (Condition 33) does not substitute for a physical access requirement.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add a gate and lock-box condition, with the fire company's keying protocol confirmed in writing.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-08\\\",\\\"'No Trespassing' and 'Danger - High Voltage' signage at intervals not exceeding 250 ft and at all gates, OSHA and NEC compliant: s.(e)(6).\\\",\\n    \\\"Not addressed.\\\",\\n    \\\"Liability and life-safety exposure on an energised site; also a standard inspection item under s.(l)(2).\\\",\\n    \\\"Add a signage condition mirroring s.(e)(6).\\\",\\\"Medium\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-09\\\",\\\"No panels, BESS, permanent roads or structures in any wetland, watercourse, floodplain or riparian buffer; wetland delineation and floodplain analysis required where potentially present: s.(f)(5).\\\",\\n    \\\"The Decision contains no finding of fact on wetlands, watercourses, floodplains or riparian buffers, and no condition. The Phase I ESA (Finding 11) does not address wetlands.\\\",\\n    \\\"This is the largest single evidential hole in the record. An 850-acre site in Lancaster County will contain wet soils, drainage swales and at least some mapped floodplain; the Ordinance prohibits structures there absolutely, and a s.404 permit may be required. Discovery of a jurisdictional wetland after permits issue would force a redesign with no amendment procedure agreed.\\\",\\n    \\\"Commission a wetland delineation and floodplain analysis before the final stormwater submission (they share the same survey base) and add a prohibition condition plus a notification obligation if wetlands are identified.\\\",\\\"Critical\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-10\\\",\\\"Type C buffer along every qualifying boundary, minimum 30 ft wide, located entirely within the site: s.(d)(1)-(2).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 16 limits the buffer to the northern and eastern boundaries 'adjacent to non-participating properties with occupied residences', and imposes no width.\\\",\\n    \\\"Narrower than the Ordinance in extent (no road frontages, no west or south boundaries) and in width (I-08). Cannot be cured by condition; the Ordinance standard survives.\\\",\\n    \\\"Restate s.(d)(1)-(2) as the standard, and require the landscape plan to show a dimensioned 30 ft width on every qualifying frontage.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-11\\\",\\\"At least 60% evergreen; species native to south-eastern Pennsylvania per DCNR; no species on the Pennsylvania noxious weed list: s.(d)(3) and (d)(6).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 16(a) requires native stock 'or, in the absence of a Township-approved list, species commonly accepted for screening purposes in Lancaster County'.\\\",\\n    \\\"The fallback clause displaces the Ordinance's native-species mandate and the noxious-weed prohibition, and there is no evergreen-minimum at all. Winter opacity is the whole point of the requirement.\\\",\\n    \\\"Adopt the DCNR list by reference, delete the 'commonly accepted' fallback, and add the 60% evergreen minimum.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-12\\\",\\\"Buffer to achieve 75% visual opacity within 5 years, determined by the Township Engineer or a qualified landscape architect: s.(d)(4) and the Type C definition.\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 16 contains no opacity standard and no date by which screening must be effective.\\\",\\n    \\\"This is the performance standard that the neighbours, the county and the dissenting Supervisors all relied on, and it is absent from the operative conditions. Condition 16 requires only that 4 ft plantings reach 12-15 ft - a growth expectation, not a measured opacity outcome.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the 75% / 5-year opacity standard with the Engineer or landscape architect determination mechanism.\\\",\\\"Critical\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-13\\\",\\\"Landscaping plan to be prepared by a licensed landscape architect registered in Pennsylvania and submitted with the application: s.(d)(5).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 16 requires a 'detailed screening plan' to the Township but specifies no professional qualification and no submission timing.\\\",\\n    \\\"The plan is not in the record and may be prepared by a contractor rather than a licensed professional. Note the parallel defect on the site plan itself, which s.(b)(2) requires to be sealed by a PE or licensed surveyor.\\\",\\n    \\\"Require a sealed landscape plan before any planting, and confirm the seal on the Site Plan.\\\",\\\"Medium\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-14\\\",\\\"Minimum of two access points, each with a minimum 20 ft driveway width, sight distance to PennDOT standards, Engineer approval: s.(h)(3).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 7 describes one primary construction access point on Hershey Mill Road and 16 ft internal roads.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ordinance non-compliance and a fire-access deficiency on a 720-acre site (I-09).\\\",\\n    \\\"Require a conforming access plan, identifying the second access point, with sight-distance analysis and PennDOT highway occupancy permit if the road is state-maintained.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-15\\\",\\\"Operational phase limited to an average of 10 vehicle trips per day on a monthly basis, with a log available to the Township: s.(h)(4).\\\",\\n    \\\"No condition; Condition 32's annual report does not require trip data.\\\",\\n    \\\"The applicant forecasts 2-5 trips per day, comfortably inside the cap, so the omission costs nothing now but leaves the standard unenforceable later.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the cap and the log requirement to Conditions 31/32.\\\",\\\"Low\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-16\\\",\\\"Road maintenance agreement with the Township in a form satisfactory to the Solicitor: s.(h)(2).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 25 requires a $500,000 bond; no agreement is required. The Planning Commission also recommended an agreement.\\\",\\n    \\\"The agreement is the instrument that fixes repair obligations, load limits, routing and scheduling. A bond without an agreement leaves the Township's remedy undefined.\\\",\\n    \\\"Require an executed road maintenance agreement before construction, alongside the bond.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-17\\\",\\\"No interference with radio, television, telephone, cellular or emergency communications; complaints investigated and resolved within 30 days: s.(g)(3).\\\",\\n    \\\"Not addressed.\\\",\\n    \\\"Modest but real risk: inverters and the gen-tie line can affect reception. Without a condition, the Township's only route is the general nuisance/enforcement power.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the interference standard and a complaint-response obligation.\\\",\\\"Low\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-18\\\",\\\"Dust suppression during construction; stabilisation of disturbed areas within 30 days of final grading of each phase; native and pollinator-friendly seed mixes: s.(g)(5).\\\",\\n    \\\"Not addressed, although the approved Decommissioning Plan contemplates revegetation.\\\",\\n    \\\"Dust is the most frequent construction-phase complaint on projects of this size. The Engineer's and the applicant's documents both reference E&S controls but neither picks up the 30-day stabilisation standard or the seed-mix requirement.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the s.(g)(5) standards, linked to the approved E&S plan.\\\",\\\"Medium\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-19\\\",\\\"Annual site inspection by the Engineer, Zoning Officer or Board designee on 10 business days' notice, at the applicant's cost: s.(l)(2).\\\",\\n    \\\"No inspection right is conferred by the Decision.\\\",\\n    \\\"The Township's ability to verify screening, fencing, noise and maintenance compliance depends on this right. Without it, enforcement rests on s.(l)(3) alone, which is also not restated (G-20).\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the inspection right and the cost-recovery term.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-20\\\",\\\"Enforcement: written notice of violation, opportunity to cure not exceeding 30 days, then revocation proceedings before the Board: s.(l)(3).\\\",\\n    \\\"The Decision recites no enforcement or cure mechanism.\\\",\\n    \\\"The Ordinance standard applies regardless, but an applicant facing an unannounced revocation action would have a strong procedural objection. Restating it protects the Township.\\\",\\n    \\\"Restate s.(l)(3), including the imminent-threat exception.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-21\\\",\\\"Reporting of any material change to the facility, its ownership or its operations: s.(l)(1)(v).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 32's content list omits ownership and material-change reporting.\\\",\\n    \\\"The Township will not learn of a transfer of the project company, a capacity change or a repowering until it happens - which also bears on G-22.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the ownership and material-change disclosure to Condition 32.\\\",\\\"Medium\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-22\\\",\\\"Approval personal to the applicant; transfer or assignment only with prior Board consent, with a capacity showing by the transferee: s.(k)(4).\\\",\\n    \\\"Not addressed.\\\",\\n    \\\"The approval and all its financial assurances could be assigned without Township review of the successor's capacity. This is a standard protection to lose.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add an assignment condition tracking s.(k)(4).\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-23\\\",\\\"Approval expires if a building permit is not obtained within 24 months of the Decision, extendable once by up to 12 months on written request filed before expiry showing good cause: s.(k)(5).\\\",\\n    \\\"Neither the Decision nor any condition states the expiry date.\\\",\\n    \\\"The date is 6 December 2026 (outer limit 6 December 2027) and it is not administratively tracked anywhere. The stated construction start of 1 April 2026 leaves roughly nine months of cushion, almost all of it consumed by the pre-permit conditions in Parts 1 and 2 (RC-13).\\\",\\n    \\\"Calendar the expiry, confirm the extension procedure in writing, and consider requiring the applicant to report quarterly on pre-permit milestones.\\\",\\\"Critical\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-24\\\",\\\"PILOT agreement to be executed AND recorded in the Lancaster County Recorder of Deeds before the first building permit, where required by the Board: s.(j)(2).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 30 requires execution 'in a form satisfactory to the Township Solicitor' but says nothing about recording.\\\",\\n    \\\"Recording is what gives the Township's revenue interest notice effect against successors and lenders - and it matters most in the assignment scenario the approval also fails to control (G-22).\\\",\\n    \\\"Add the recording requirement to Condition 30.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-25\\\",\\\"Stormwater plan to demonstrate post-development runoff not exceeding pre-development for the 2, 10, 25, 50 and 100-year design storms: s.(f)(1).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 10 incorporates the Ordinance by reference but does not restate the design-storm performance standard.\\\",\\n    \\\"Low practical risk because the Ordinance applies anyway, but the Engineer's review criteria should be stated so that the 6 March 2025 submission can be tested against them.\\\",\\n    \\\"Restate the design storms and the pre/post standard as review criteria in Condition 10.\\\",\\\"Low\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-26\\\",\\\"Surety bond issuer to carry an A.M. Best rating of A- VII or better; escrow to name the Township as beneficiary: s.(i)(4).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 22 requires a surety 'authorized to transact business in the Commonwealth' with no rating floor, and an escrow at a rated institution with no beneficiary term.\\\",\\n    \\\"The Ordinance's credit-quality screen for the $7.875 million instrument is dropped. Given the applicant has nominated 'Northbrook Surety Company' (Finding 24) with no commitment letter, the rating screen is the Township's main protection.\\\",\\n    \\\"Restate s.(i)(4)(ii) and (iii) in Condition 22 and require rating evidence with the instrument.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-27\\\",\\\"Decommissioning to be completed within 12 months of the 12-month cessation trigger, with an absolute 24-month cap: s.(i)(7).\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 21 allows 18 months; Condition 24 creates a separate abandonment mechanism with a 90-day contest window.\\\",\\n    \\\"Two inconsistent regimes (I-12, I-18). The 24-month cap - the most important backstop - appears in neither instrument.\\\",\\n    \\\"Restate s.(i)(7) verbatim and delete the 18-month figure.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-28\\\",\\\"General standard s.(a)-(e) of s.27-602, in particular (c) 'shall not adversely affect the character, property values, or orderly development of the surrounding neighborhood' and (e) compliance with all other regulations.\\\",\\n    \\\"Conclusions 2, 4 and 6 address these in conclusory terms; there are no discrete findings on s.27-602(c) beyond the property value assessment (see I-15), and no findings on wetlands/floodplain compliance (G-09) or on the full text of s.(B)(15).\\\",\\n    \\\"The general standards are the legal hinge for the approval. Conclusory treatment invites challenge where an underlying study is thin or a standard is unaddressed.\\\",\\n    \\\"Add discrete findings against each of s.27-602(a)-(e), and qualify Conclusions 2 and 8 as 'subject to the conditions herein'.\\\",\\\"Medium\\\")\\ngap(\\\"G-29\\\",\\\"Two access points, sight distance and driveway width are also fire-access requirements; s.(e)(5) lock boxes and Condition 33 emergency planning presuppose vehicular access to all energised areas.\\\",\\n    \\\"Condition 7 addresses internal road width and turning movements for construction vehicles only.\\\",\\n    \\\"The Fire Company has not been asked to comment on access, turning radii or water supply for a BESS fire. Condition 33 only requires a plan 30 days before COD.\\\",\\n    \\\"Obtain written fire company comment on the access and BESS layout before permit issuance, and record it in the file.\\\",\\\"High\\\")\\n\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# RISK REGISTER\\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------\\nRISKS = []\\ndef risk(rid, desc, linked, lik, imp, rating, exposure, mit, owner):\\n    RISKS.append(dict(id=rid, desc=desc, linked=linked, lik=lik, imp=imp, rating=rating,\\n                      exposure=exposure, mit=mit, owner=owner))\\n\\nrisk(\\\"R-01\\\",\\\"Approval rests on a lot-coverage computation that, on the Ordinance's own treatment of panels as semi-impervious, exceeds the 75% ceiling by 40 acres.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-01, RC-10, A-11\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\n     \\\"Legal (Ordinance non-compliance embedded in the approval); design (possible 40-acre layout reduction); schedule.\\\",\\n     \\\"Obtain the impervious/semi-impervious area schedule and a written Engineer determination of how panels are treated. If panels count, either reduce coverage or record the interpretation in an amendment with notice.\\\",\\\"Township Engineer / Zoning Officer\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-02\\\",\\\"Agricultural mitigation is understated: 3 acres by arithmetic, plus an unknown acreage of farmland of statewide importance that the Ordinance requires to be included.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-02, RC-04, RC-05, B-10\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Financial (up to approx. $272,400); legal (s.(f)(6) requires Conservation District confirmation, which is absent).\\\",\\n     \\\"Obtain NRCS soils mapping and Conservation District written confirmation of the qualifying acreage; correct Condition 28 by amendment; true-up any shortfall.\\\",\\\"Zoning Officer / Solicitor\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-03\\\",\\\"The Township's own PILOT negotiating position (2% escalator from Year 11) contradicts Condition 30 and Finding 17, which fix flat $385,000 payments for Years 1-15.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-16, RC-15, D-06\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"Critical\\\",\\n     \\\"Legal (side agreement departing from the decision); financial (approx. $118,627 over Years 1-15; approx. $3.1M over 30 years).\\\",\\n     \\\"Decide the position: either amend Condition 30 under s.(k)(3) with the proper procedure, or accept Year 16 and pursue value elsewhere (e.g. the MFN clause). Do not paper over it in the agreement.\\\",\\\"Township Solicitor / Board\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-04\\\",\\\"Most-favoured-nation clause unresolved; the applicant has rejected it and the Township treats it as material.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-16, D-06\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Schedule (Condition 30 gates permit issuance); financial (rate protection foregone if dropped).\\\",\\n     \\\"Consider the Solicitor's fallback options (periodic rate review or a floor-and-cap) and take a Board decision on a documented negotiating mandate.\\\",\\\"Township Solicitor / Board\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-05\\\",\\\"The 6 March 2025 stormwater deadline slips because Conservation District review (60-90 days per the Township Engineer) had not visibly started by 10 January 2025.\\\",\\n     \\\"B-05, B-06, RC-11\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Schedule (final plan cannot be approved; no building or grading permit can issue); credibility.\\\",\\n     \\\"Confirm the CD submission date immediately. If it cannot be met, document the position and consider a Board-approved extension - do not let the date pass silently. Note the Decision attaches no consequence to a missed deadline, which is itself a drafting weakness.\\\",\\\"Applicant / Township Engineer\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-06\\\",\\\"The conditional use approval expires on 6 December 2026 if no building permit has issued, and the expiry is not recorded in either approval instrument.\\\",\\n     \\\"G-23, RC-13, F-03\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Legal (loss of the entitlement); financial (approx. $200M project); political.\\\",\\n     \\\"Calendar the date; require quarterly pre-permit milestone reporting; ensure any extension request is filed in writing before 6 December 2026 (a Sunday) with a good-cause showing.\\\",\\\"Zoning Officer / Solicitor\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-07\\\",\\\"PHMC consultation remains outstanding and gates every grading permit for the project.\\\",\\n     \\\"B-03, I-12\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Schedule (no grading until resolved); the Stoltzfus Farmstead sits at the 200 ft trigger distance.\\\",\\n     \\\"Route the consultation under the Pennsylvania History Code (37 Pa. C.S. ss.101 et seq.), not s.106; confirm the 200 ft measurement against the surveyed boundary; set a date for the PHMC submission.\\\",\\\"Applicant / Ridgepoint\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-08\\\",\\\"Bog turtle Phase 2 (and possibly Phase 3) surveys remain outstanding on Parcels -003 and -004, and habitat surveys are seasonally constrained.\\\",\\n     \\\"B-04\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Schedule (a missed survey window can cost a full season); scope (avoidance buffers if habitat is confirmed).\\\",\\n     \\\"Confirm survey scoping with USFWS now and calendar the seasonal window; consider sequencing grading on other parcels first, noting Condition 8 is parcel-specific.\\\",\\\"Applicant / Ridgepoint\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-09\\\",\\\"Wetlands, watercourses and floodplain have not been evaluated anywhere in the record, although the Ordinance prohibits structures in them.\\\",\\n     \\\"G-09, B-09\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Legal (absolute prohibition); design (layout may not be buildable as approved); permitting (possible s.404).\\\",\\n     \\\"Commission a wetland delineation and floodplain analysis on the same survey base as the stormwater submission; add a notification and amendment mechanism.\\\",\\\"Applicant / Township Engineer\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-10\\\",\\\"The 500 ft substation-to-dwelling setback is absent from the conditions and the applicant's accepted-conditions summary states 250 ft.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-06, G-03\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Legal (Ordinance non-compliance); layout (relocation of substations if the design is at 250 ft).\\\",\\n     \\\"Check the site plan dimension for every substation and conversion station today. If short of 500 ft, the layout must change before permits; if compliant, condition it expressly.\\\",\\\"Township Engineer\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-11\\\",\\\"Only one access point is conditioned, against a two-access-point Ordinance standard, on a 720-acre energised site.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-09, G-14\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Life safety (fire apparatus access); Ordinance non-compliance; insurability.\\\",\\n     \\\"Require the second access point on the plan, obtain fire company sign-off, and confirm whether Hershey Mill Road is state or Township maintained (PennDOT HOP may be required).\\\",\\\"Township Engineer / Fire Company\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-12\\\",\\\"Screening will not achieve the Ordinance's 75% opacity in 5 years because the standard is not in the conditions and the planting palette has no evergreen floor.\\\",\\n     \\\"I-08, G-10, G-11, G-12\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\\"Medium\\\",\\\"High\\\",\\n     \\\"Political/community (the issue the dissent and the neighbours pressed); legal (Ordinance standard survives the condition).\\\",\\n     \\\"Restate the Ordinance standard; require a sealed landscape plan with a species schedule showing at least 60% evergreen; plant at energisation as the Engineer recommends (email of 10 January 2025).\\\",\\\"Township Engineer\\\")\\nrisk(\\\"R-13\\\",\\\"Decommissioning