\n                  consequence. Medium: delay, dispute, or a moderate financial or political cost. Low: administrative\n                  nuisance.\nRating            The rating shown is the conservative combination of the two, escalated where the exposure is to the\n                  validity of the approval rather than to a single transaction.\n\n\nLimitations\n  Documentary review only. No site inspection was undertaken, no permit file was inspected, and no\n      Township officer, consultant or applicant representative was interviewed. Status entries describe\n      what the six documents establish, not what has occurred in fact.\n     The ordinance analysis rests on an excerpt. Section 27-605(B)(14) was reviewed in an excerpt\n      that disclaims its own controlling effect, is unsigned and undated, and is certified in the name of a\n      different Township Secretary from the officer who attested the Decision. The excerpt also omits s.27-\n      603, s.27-604, s.27-606 and following, and reproduces only subsections (c)(3)-(4) of s.27-605(B)(15).\n      Requirements in the omitted provisions have not been checked. Every gap in Part 4 must be re-tested\n      against the codified text before reliance.\n     Six documents, many referenced but not provided. The primary evidentiary documents — site\n      plan, noise study, glare analysis, appraisal report, environmental reports, stormwater plans, traffic\n      study, decommissioning plan, PILOT draft and leases — were not in the file. Conclusions about them\n      depend on how the six reviewed documents characterise them, and where two documents\n      characterise the same document differently that difference is itself recorded as an inconsistency.\n     No legal opinion is expressed. Characterisations of what the Ordinance requires, and of the\n"}
